WEBVTT

1
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.320
Theatre – it can show us who we are,
who we were and who we could be.

2
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.560
It means imagining bigger,

3
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.440
understanding better,

4
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.760
feeling together –

5
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.560
on our stages and on tour,

6
00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.400
in schools

7
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.120
and with communities,

8
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.640
on cinema screens worldwide

9
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.760
and now at home.

10
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.800
We are the National Theatre.

11
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.840
We make theatre for everyone.

12
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.120
Art helps us to see the world in new ways.

13
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.280
The stories behind great works
help to bring them to life,

14
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.360
and hearing them shouldn't be a privilege.

15
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.000
That's why we built Bloomberg Connects.

16
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.720
An app that lets you discover a growing
number of museums, gardens and parks,

17
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.080
behind-the-scenes interviews
and public art from around the world,

18
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.640
all in a single download
right in the palm of your hand.

19
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.040
Bloomberg Connects offers
a world of inspiration.

20
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.680
Join the National Theatre community
and hundreds of cultural institutions

21
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.000
to get a deeper look into the world of art
through Bloomberg Connects.

22
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.240
(Woman) 'Good evening,
and welcome to the National Theatre.

23
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.800
'Before we start
this evening's entertainment,

24
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.920
'please, please, please
switch off your phones.

25
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.480
'Switch them off now,

26
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.840
'get them out of your bag,
out of your pockets, out of wherever it is,

27
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.400
'switch them onto flight mode, turn them off,
whatever you have to do,

28
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.320
'because if they go off during the show,

29
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.400
'I will come and find you.'

30
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:44.800
(Audience laughing, clapping)

31
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.760
'Thank you,
and enjoy the show.'

32
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.080
(♪ Drum roll)

33
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.840
(♪ Drum roll ends with single synth note,
audience whooping)

34
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.920
(Audience cheering, applauding)

35
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.000
(Audience laughing)

36
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.840
(♪ Piano continues)

37
00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.760
(Audience laughing)

38
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.640
(♪ Violins start)

39
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.440
(♪ Drums start)

40
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.480
(♪ Saxophone starts)

41
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.680
(♪ Music intensifies)

42
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.560
(♪ Music becomes discordant)

43
00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.080
(♪ Music ends)

44
00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.680
(♪ Audience cheering, applauding)

45
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.040
(Grunting)

46
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.160
(♪ Piano strikes up,
playing discordant notes)

47
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.200
(♪ "Girls Just Want to Have Fun"
playing on piano)

48
00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:33.600
(Audience chuckling)

49
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.360
(♪ Piano stops)

50
00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.840
Did you hear what I was playing, Lane?

51
00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.600
I didn't think it polite to listen, sir.

52
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.480
Ah... Well, I'm sorry for that,
for your sake.

53
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.920
I don't play accurately ­
anyone can play accurately -

54
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.720
but I play with wonderful expression.

55
00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.440
As far as the piano is concerned,
sentiment is my forte.

56
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.840
- I keep science for life. (Chuckles)
- Yes, sir.

57
00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.360
And, uh...
speaking of the science of life,

58
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.840
have you got the cucumber sandwiches cut
for Lady Bracknell?

59
00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.040
Yes, sir.

60
00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.760
By the way, Lane,

61
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.720
I see from your book
that on Thursday night,

62
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.160
when Lord Shoreham and Mr Worthing
were dining with me,

63
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.520
eight bottles of champagne
are entered as having been consumed.

64
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.840
- Yes, sir. Eight bottles and a pint.
- Oh.

65
00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.360
Why is it
that at a bachelor's establishment

66
00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.680
the servants should invariably
drink the champagne?

67
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.440
I ask merely for information.
(Chuckles)

68
00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.040
I attribute it to the superior quality
of the wine, sir.

69
00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.600
- Oh!
- I have often observed

70
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.280
that in married households
the champagne is rarely of a first-rate brand.

71
00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.720
Good heavens!

72
00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.600
Is marriage so demoralising as that?

73
00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.120
I believe it is a very pleasant state, sir.

74
00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.920
I've had very little experience of it myself
up to the present.

75
00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.600
I've only been married once,

76
00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:17.080
and that was in consequence
of a misunderstanding...

77
00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.280
(Audience laughing)

78
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.320
...between myself and a young person.

79
00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.320
I don't know that I'm much interested
in your family life, Lane.

80
00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.680
No, no, sir,
it's not a very interesting subject.

81
00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.240
I never think of it myself.

82
00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.400
Very natural, I am sure.
That will do, Lane, thank you.

83
00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.440
- Thank you, sir.
- (Doorbell ringing)

84
00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.720
Lane's views on marriage
seem somewhat lax.

85
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.680
Really, if the lower orders
don't set us a good example,

86
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:44.800
what on earth is the use of them?

87
00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:49.960
They seem, as a class, to have
absolutely no sense of moral responsibility.

88
00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.880
Mr Ernest Worthing.

89
00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.680
Oh!

90
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.360
How are you, my dear Ernest?

91
00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.560
- What brings you up to town?
- Oh, pleasure, pleasure!

92
00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.600
- Oh?
- What else should bring one anywhere?

93
00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.520
- (Algernon laughing)
- Eating as usual, I see, Algy!

94
00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.480
Uh, I believe it is customary in good society
to take some slight refreshment at five o'clock.

95
00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.160
Where have you been
since last Thursday?

96
00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.400
- In the country.
- What on earth do you do there?

97
00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.160
When one's in town,
one amuses oneself.

98
00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.800
When one's in the country,
one amuses other people.

99
00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.720
It's excessively boring.

100
00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.880
Who are these people you amuse?

101
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.680
- Oh, neighbours, neighbours.
- Mm!

102
00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:28.080
Got nice neighbours
in your part of Shropshire?

103
00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.600
Perfectly horrid!
Never speak to one of them.

104
00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.000
How immensely you must amuse them!

105
00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:36.720
By the way,
Shropshire is your county, is it not?

106
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.080
- Shropshire?
- Mm.

107
00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:41.440
Yes, of course. Hello!
Why all these cups?

108
00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.400
Why cucumber sandwiches?

109
00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.800
Why such reckless extravagance
in one so young?

110
00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.160
- Who is coming to tea?
- Oh! Merely Aunt Augusta and Gwendolen.

111
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:53.400
- How perfectly delightful!
- Yes, yes, that is all very well,

112
00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.600
but I am afraid Aunt Augusta
won't quite approve of your being here.

113
00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.080
May I ask why?

114
00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:03.960
My dear fellow, the way you flirt
with Gwendolen is perfectly disgraceful.

115
00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.520
It is almost as bad as the way
Gwendolen flirts with you.

116
00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.000
I'm in love with Gwendolen.

117
00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.200
(Laughs heartily)

118
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:14.600
I've come up to town
expressly to propose to her.

119
00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.560
(Bursts out laughing)

120
00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.880
I thought you had come up for pleasure?

121
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.840
I call that business.

122
00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:23.680
How utterly unromantic you are!

123
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.240
Oh, I really don't see anything romantic
in proposing.

124
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.240
It's very romantic to be in love,

125
00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.960
but there is nothing romantic
about a definite proposal.

126
00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:37.520
Why, one may be accepted.
One usually is, I believe.

127
00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.440
Then the excitement is all over.

128
00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.520
The very essence of romance
is uncertainty.

129
00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.160
If ever I get married,
I'll certainly try to forget the fact. (Chuckles)

130
00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.560
I have no doubt about that, dear Algy.

131
00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:52.440
The Divorce Court
was specially invented

132
00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.080
for people whose memories
are so curiously constituted.

133
00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.600
Oh! There is no use speculating
on that subject.

134
00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.360
Divorces are made in heaven.

135
00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.000
- (Exclaims) Ah...!
- (Audience laughing)

136
00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.560
Please don't touch
the cucumber sandwiches.

137
00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.280
They are ordered specially
for Aunt Augusta.

138
00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:11.760
(Audience laughing)

139
00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:13.360
Well, you've been eating them
all the time.

140
00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:16.560
That is quite a different matter.
She is my aunt.

141
00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.000
Have some bread and butter.

142
00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:19.520
The bread and butter is for Gwendolen.

143
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.280
Gwendolen is devoted
to bread and butter.

144
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:23.640
Mm!

145
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.520
Oh, my God! Mm!

146
00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.480
Very good bread and butter it is, too.

147
00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.080
Yes, well, you need not eat
as if you were going to eat it all.

148
00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.000
You behave as if you were married
to her already. (Chuckles)

149
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.400
You are not married
to her already, and...

150
00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.400
I don't think you ever will be.

151
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.400
Why on earth do you say that?

152
00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:46.920
(Audience laughing)

153
00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:49.400
Well...

154
00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:53.880
in the first place, girls never marry
the men they flirt with.

155
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.480
Girls don't think it right.

156
00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.800
- Oh, that is nonsense!
- It isn't.

157
00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:59.080
It is a great truth.

158
00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:03.720
It accounts for the extraordinary number
of bachelors one sees all over the place.

159
00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.240
(Audience laughing)

160
00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.720
(Laughing)

161
00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:12.320
In the second place,
I don't give my consent.

162
00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:16.400
- Your consent?
- My dear fellow, Gwendolen is my first cousin.

163
00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.280
And before I allow you to marry her,

164
00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.680
you will have to clear up
the whole question...

165
00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.600
- (Rings bell)
- ...of Cecily.

166
00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:27.680
- Cecily?
- Mm!

167
00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.200
What on earth do you mean?

168
00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:33.800
What do you mean, Algy, by Cecily?
I don't know anyone by the name of Cecily.

169
00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:37.520
Bring me that cigarette case
Mr Worthing left in the smoking-room

170
00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.040
the last time he dined here.

171
00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.760
Yes, sir.

172
00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.560
Do you mean to say you've had
my cigarette case all this time?

173
00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.560
I wish to goodness you'd let me know.

174
00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.920
I've been writing frantic letters
to Scotland Yard about it.

175
00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:53.040
I was very nearly offering a large reward.

176
00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:56.960
Well, I wish you would offer one.
I happen to be more than usually hard up.

177
00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.920
There is no good offering a large reward
now that the thing is found.

178
00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:04.200
Uh! I think that is rather mean of you,
Ernest, I must say.

179
00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:05.840
Thank you.

180
00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.240
However...
Uh! It makes no matter,

181
00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.960
for, now that I look at the inscription inside,
I find that the thing isn't yours after all.

182
00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:19.240
Of course it's mine.
You've seen me with it a hundred times,

183
00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:22.160
and you've no right whatsoever
to read what's written inside.

184
00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:27.360
It is a very ungentlemanly thing
to read a private cigarette case.

185
00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:28.520
Oh!

186
00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:31.680
It is absurd
to have a hard-and-fast rule

187
00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:34.200
about what one should read
and what one shouldn't.

188
00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:37.520
More than half of modern culture
depends on what one shouldn't read.

189
00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:41.240
I am quite aware of the fact,
and I don't propose to discuss modern culture.

190
00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.200
It isn't the sort of thing
one should talk of in private.

191
00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.000
- Oh!
- I simply want my cigarette case back.

192
00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:48.320
Yes, but this isn't
your cigarette case.

193
00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:53.280
This cigarette case is a present
from someone of the name of Cecily.

194
00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:55.880
You said
you didn't know anyone of that name.

195
00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.000
(Audience laughing)

196
00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:05.280
Well, if you want to know,
Cecily happens to be my aunt.

197
00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.840
- Your aunt!
- Yes.

198
00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.480
Charming old lady she is, too.
Lives in Tunbridge Wells.

199
00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:11.960
Can you just give it back to me, Algy.

200
00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:16.440
But why does she
call herself little Cecily

201
00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:19.400
if she is your aunt
and lives at Tunbridge Wells?

202
00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:22.440
"From little Cecily with her fondest love."

203
00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.400
My dear fellow,
what on earth is there in that?

204
00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:26.880
Some aunts are tall,
some aunts are not tall.

205
00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.280
(Audience laughing)

206
00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.320
That's a matter that surely an aunt
may be allowed to decide for herself.

207
00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.200
You seem to think that every aunt should be
exactly like your aunt! That's absurd!

208
00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:36.960
For heaven's sake,
give me back my cigarette case.

209
00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:42.560
Yes. But why does your aunt
call you her uncle?

210
00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:44.480
(Audience laughing)

211
00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:49.920
"From little Cecily, with her fondest love
to her dear Uncle Jack."

212
00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:55.480
There is no objection, I admit,
to an aunt being a small aunt,

213
00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.640
but why an aunt,
no matter what her size may be,

214
00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:03.800
should call her own nephew her uncle,
I can't quite make out.

215
00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:08.280
Besides, your name isn't Jack at all.
Huh? (Chuckles)

216
00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.480
It's Ernest.

217
00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.560
It isn't Ernest, it's Jack.

218
00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.640
- Huh!
- (Audience laughing)

219
00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:20.480
You have always told me
your name was Ernest.

220
00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:24.800
I've introduced you to everyone as Ernest.
You answer to the name of Ernest.

221
00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:27.560
You look as if your name was Ernest.

222
00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:30.840
You are the most earnest-looking person
I ever saw in my life.

223
00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.200
It's perfectly absurd your saying
your name isn't Ernest.

224
00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:35.440
It is written on your cards.
Here is one of them.

225
00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:39.480
"Mr Ernest Worthing, B4, The Albany."

226
00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.360
I'll keep this as a proof
that your name is Ernest

227
00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:49.800
if ever you attempt to deny it to me,
or to Gwendolen, or to anybody else.

228
00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:54.480
Well, my name is Ernest in town
and Jack in the country,

229
00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:57.040
and that cigarette case was given to me
in the country.

230
00:15:57.200 --> 00:15:59.560
Yes, but that does not account
for the fact

231
00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:02.640
that your small Aunt Cecily,
who lives at Tunbridge Wells,

232
00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:04.480
calls you her uncle.

233
00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:07.600
Uh? Oh, come, old boy, you'd much better
have the thing out at once.

234
00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:09.920
My dear fellow, you talk exactly
as if you were a dentist.

235
00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:13.000
It's very vulgar to talk like a dentist
when one isn't a dentist.

236
00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.440
- (Audience laughing)
- It produces a false impression.

237
00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:18.640
Well, that is exactly
what dentists always do.

238
00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:21.560
Now, go on!
Uh? Tell me the whole thing.

239
00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:24.640
I may mention... (Laughs)

240
00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.520
...I have always suspected you...

241
00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:37.920
...of being a secret
and confirmed Bunburyist,

242
00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.680
and I'm quite sure of it now.

243
00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.480
- Bunburyist?
- Mm!

244
00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.280
What on earth do you mean
by a Bunburyist?

245
00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.800
I'll reveal to you the meaning
of that incomparable expression

246
00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.920
as soon as you are kind enough
to inform me

247
00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:54.000
why you are Ernest in town
and Jack in the country.

248
00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.320
Well, produce my cigarette case first.

249
00:16:57.440 --> 00:16:58.560
(Algernon scoffs)

250
00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.360
Here it is.

251
00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:06.560
Now produce your explanation,
and pray make it improbable.

252
00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:11.320
My dear fellow, there is nothing improbable
about my explanation at all.

253
00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.240
In fact, it's perfectly ordinary.

254
00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:16.760
Old Mr Thomas Cardew,
who adopted me when I was a little boy,

255
00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:21.560
made me in his will guardian
to his granddaughter, Miss Cecily Cardew.

256
00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:24.200
- Uh-huh.
- Cecily, who addresses me as her uncle

257
00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:27.680
out of motives of respect
that you could not possibly appreciate,

258
00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:29.440
lives at my place in the country

259
00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:32.040
under the charge of her admirable governess,
Miss Prism.

260
00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:34.400
Where is that place in the country,
by the way?

261
00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:36.200
That is nothing to you, dear boy.

262
00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:38.480
- You're not going to be invited.
- Oh!

263
00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:41.240
I may tell you quite candidly
that the place is not in Shropshire.

264
00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.200
Well, I suspected that, my dear fellow!

265
00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:47.000
I have Bunburyed all over Shropshire
on two separate occasions.

266
00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:48.960
Now, go on.

267
00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:52.600
Why are you Ernest in town
and Jack in the country?

268
00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:57.320
My dear fellow, I don't know whether
you'll be able to understand my real motives.

269
00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.440
- You're hardly serious enough.
- (Algernon blows raspberry)

270
00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:06.400
When one is placed
in the position of guardian,

271
00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.480
one has to adopt a very high moral tone
on all subjects.

272
00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:12.520
It's one's duty to do so.

273
00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.240
And as a high moral tone
can hardly be said

274
00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:20.840
to conduce very much
to either one's health or one's happiness...

275
00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:22.640
(Audience chuckling)

276
00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.160
...in order to come up to town

277
00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.240
I have always pretended
to have a younger brother

278
00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.400
- by the name of Ernest...
- (Algernon laughing)

279
00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:35.080
...who lives in the Albany
and gets into the most dreadful scrapes.

280
00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:36.400
Mm!

281
00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.520
(Audience laughing)

282
00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:42.160
That, my dear Algy, is the whole truth,
pure and simple.

283
00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.600
The truth is rarely pure and never simple.

284
00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.640
Modern life would be very tedious
if it were either,

285
00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.480
and modern literature
a complete impossibility!

286
00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:52.400
That wouldn't be at all a bad thing.

287
00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:56.400
Literary criticism is not your forte,
my dear fellow. Don't try it.

288
00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:59.720
You should leave that to the people
who don't know anything.

289
00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:02.640
They do it so well in the daily papers.

290
00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:07.160
What you really are is a Bunburyist.

291
00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:09.760
Ha! I was quite right in saying
you were a Bunburyist.

292
00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.640
You are one of the most advanced
Bunburyists I know.

293
00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:14.040
What on earth do you mean?

294
00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:16.880
Oh... Huh!

295
00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:21.080
You have invented a very useful
younger brother by the name of Ernest,

296
00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:25.840
in order that you may be able
to come up to town as often as you like. Uh?

297
00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:31.560
I have invented an invaluable
permanent invalid called Bunbury,

298
00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.560
in order that I may be able to go down
to the country whenever I choose.

299
00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.440
Bunbury is perfectly invaluable.

300
00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:41.360
If it wasn't for Bunbury's
extraordinary bad health, for instance,

301
00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:43.760
I wouldn't be able to dine with you
tonight at Willis's,

302
00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:46.640
for I've really been engaged
to Aunt Augusta for more than a week.

303
00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:48.240
I haven't asked you to dine
with me anywhere tonight.

304
00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:51.400
I know. You are absurdly careless
about sending out invitations.

305
00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:53.240
It's very foolish of you.

306
00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:56.440
Nothing annoys people so much
as not receiving invitations.

307
00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:58.960
You had much better dine
with your Aunt Augusta.

308
00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:02.960
(Laughs) I haven't the smallest intention
of doing anything of the kind.

309
00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:05.160
To begin with,
I dined there on Monday,

310
00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:08.880
and once a week is quite enough
to dine with one's own relations.

311
00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.840
In the second place,
whenever I do dine there,

312
00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:15.160
I am always treated
as a member of the family,

313
00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.240
and sent down
with either no woman at all, or two.

314
00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:24.480
In the third place, I know perfectly well
whom she will place me next to tonight.

315
00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:28.600
- She will place me next to Mary Farquhar...
- Oh, God!

316
00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:32.480
...who always flirts with her own husband
across the dinner table.

317
00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:34.280
That is not very pleasant.

318
00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:36.600
Indeed, it is not even decent,

319
00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:39.800
and that sort of thing
is enormously on the increase.

320
00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:42.680
The amount of women who flirt
with their own husbands in London

321
00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:44.760
- is perfectly scandalous.
- (Audience laughing)

322
00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.320
It looks so bad.

323
00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.720
It's simply washing
one's clean linen in public.

324
00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:53.400
Besides, now that I know you
to be a confirmed Bunburyist,

325
00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:57.000
I naturally want to talk to you
about Bunburying. Uh?

326
00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:58.600
I want to tell you the rules.

327
00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.240
I am not a Bunburyist at all.

328
00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:04.560
(Audience chuckling)

329
00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:08.800
If Gwendolen accepts me,

330
00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:11.160
I'm going to kill my brother.

331
00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.480
(Audience laughing)

332
00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.960
Indeed I think I'll kill him in any case.

333
00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:18.920
Cecily's a little too much interested in him.
It is rather a bore.

334
00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:21.320
So, I'm going to get rid of Ernest.

335
00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:24.040
And I strongly advise you
to do the same with Mr...

336
00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.720
With your invalid friend
who has the absurd name.

337
00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:31.360
Nothing will induce me
to part with Bunbury,

338
00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:35.200
and if you ever get married,
which seems to me extremely problematic,

339
00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.320
you'll be very glad to know Bunbury.

340
00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:40.680
A man who marries without knowing Bunbury
has a very tedious time of it.

341
00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.520
That is nonsense.

342
00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:44.320
If I marry a charming girl like Gwendolen,

343
00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:47.680
and she is the only girl I ever saw in my life
who I would marry,

344
00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.400
- I certainly wouldn't want to know Bunbury.
- Then your wife will.

345
00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:56.480
You don't seem to realise that in married life
three is company and two is none.

346
00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:58.160
That, my dear young friend,

347
00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:02.080
is the theory that the corrupt French Drama
has been propounding for the last fifty years.

348
00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:05.560
Yes, and that the happy English home
has proved in half the time.

349
00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:09.360
For heaven's sake, don't try to be cynical.
It's perfectly easy to be cynical.

350
00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:12.560
Oh, my dear fellow,
it isn't easy to be anything nowadays.

351
00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:14.600
There's such a lot
of beastly competition about.

352
00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:16.600
- (Doorbell rings incessantly)
- Oh, God!

353
00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:20.040
(Audience laughing)

354
00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:23.200
Ah!
That must be Aunt Augusta.

355
00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:26.760
Only relatives, or creditors,
ever ring in that Wagnerian manner.

356
00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:30.360
Now, if I get her out of the way
for ten minutes,

357
00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:34.600
so that you can have an opportunity
for proposing to Gwendolen,

358
00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:36.480
may I dine with you tonight at Willis's?

359
00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.040
Oh, yes, I suppose so, if you want to.

360
00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:42.600
Yes, yes, yes,
but you must be serious about it.

361
00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:45.720
I hate people
who are not serious about meals.

362
00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:47.280
It's so shallow of them.

363
00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:51.680
Lady Bracknell and Miss Fairfax.

364
00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:56.280
Good afternoon, dear Algernon,

365
00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:59.160
I hope you are behaving very well.

366
00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:01.720
I'm feeling very well, Aunt Augusta.

367
00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:04.280
That's not quite the same thing.

368
00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:08.440
In fact, the two things
rarely go together. Mm?

369
00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:11.880
(Audience chuckling)

370
00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.400
Oh, dear me, you are smart!

371
00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.920
I am always smart!
Aren't I, Mr Worthing?

372
00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:20.280
You're quite perfect, Miss Fairfax.

373
00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:24.760
Oh! I hope I am not that,
for it would leave no room for developments,

374
00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:28.880
and I intend to develop
in many directions. Ooh!

375
00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:30.560
(Chuckling)

376
00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:32.800
I'm sorry if we are a little late,
Algernon,

377
00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.560
but I was obliged
to call on dear Lady Harbury.

378
00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:36.720
Ah, yes.

379
00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:40.000
I hadn't been there
since her poor husband's death.

380
00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:43.640
I never saw a woman so altered.

381
00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.880
- She looks quite twenty years younger.
- Ooh!

382
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:48.960
And now I'll have a cup of tea

383
00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:52.680
and one of those nice cucumber sandwiches
you promised me.

384
00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:54.600
Certainly, Aunt Augusta.

385
00:23:55.960 --> 00:23:59.080
Won't you come and sit here,
Gwendolen?

386
00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:02.560
Thanks, mamma,
I'm quite comfortable where I am.

387
00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.160
(Audience chuckling)

388
00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.400
Good heavens!
Lane!

389
00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:14.320
Why are there no cucumber sandwiches?

390
00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.480
I ordered them specially.

391
00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:21.560
There were no cucumbers
in the market this morning, sir.

392
00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:23.920
- I went down twice.
- No cucumbers!

393
00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:27.400
No, sir.
Not even for ready money.

394
00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:28.920
Thank you, Lane, that will do.

395
00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.320
- Thank you, sir.
- Thank you.

396
00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:31.720
- Thank you, sir.
- Thank you.

397
00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:33.600
(Audience laughing)

398
00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:39.520
Aunt Augusta, I am greatly distressed
about there being no cucumbers,

399
00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:41.160
not even for ready money.

400
00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:43.360
- It really makes no matter, Algernon.
- Oh!

401
00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:45.920
- I had some crumpets with Lady Harbury...
- Ah!

402
00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.640
...who seems to me to be living
entirely for pleasure now.

403
00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:53.520
I hear her hair has turned quite gold
from grief.

404
00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.920
It certainly has changed its colour.

405
00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:58.920
From what cause
I, of course, cannot say.

406
00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.440
- Mm!
- (Chuckling) Thank you.

407
00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:04.320
Oh! I've quite a treat
for you tonight, Algernon.

408
00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:06.520
- Mm?
- I'm going to send you down

409
00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:09.440
- with Mary Farquhar.
- Ah!

410
00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:14.840
She is such a nice woman,
and so attentive to her husband.

411
00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:17.640
It's delightful to watch them.

412
00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.920
I am afraid, Aunt Augusta,

413
00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:25.680
I will have to give up the pleasure
of dining with you tonight after all.

414
00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.240
I hope not, Algernon.

415
00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:31.360
It would put my table completely out.

416
00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:35.400
- Your uncle would have to dine upstairs.
- (Audience chuckling)

417
00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.640
- Fortunately, he is accustomed to that.
- (Audience laughing)

418
00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:41.400
It is a great bore,

419
00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:44.080
and needless to say
a terrible disappointment to me,

420
00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:47.760
but the fact is,
I have just had a telegram

421
00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:51.880
to say that my poor friend Bunbury
is very ill again.

422
00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:54.280
They seem to think
that I should be with him.

423
00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.160
It is very strange.

424
00:25:56.280 --> 00:26:00.400
This Mr Bunbury seems to suffer
from curiously bad health.

425
00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:04.480
Yes, yes, poor Bunbury
is a dreadful invalid.

426
00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:07.920
Well, I must say, Algernon,

427
00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:11.720
that I think it is high time
that Mr Bunbury made up his mind

428
00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.280
- whether he's going to live or to die.
- (Audience laughing)

429
00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:19.600
This shilly-shallying
with the question is absurd.

430
00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:24.160
Nor do I in any way approve
of the modern sympathy with invalids.

431
00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:27.440
I consider it morbid.

432
00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:31.920
Illness of any kind is hardly a thing
to be encouraged in others.

433
00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:36.880
Health is the primary duty of life.

434
00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.520
I am always telling that
to your poor uncle,

435
00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:41.400
but he never seems to take much notice

436
00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:45.320
as far as any improvement
in his ailments goes.

437
00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:50.000
I should be much obliged
if you would ask Mr Bunbury, from me,

438
00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:53.840
to be kind enough
not to have a relapse on Saturday,

439
00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:57.880
- for I rely on you to arrange my music for me.
- Oh?

440
00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.200
It is my last reception,

441
00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:05.400
and one wants something
that will encourage conversation,

442
00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.920
particularly at the end of the season

443
00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:11.480
when everyone has practically said
whatever they had to say,

444
00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:15.000
which, in most cases,
was probably not much.

445
00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:18.080
- (Audience chuckling)
- I'll speak to Bunbury, Aunt Augusta,

446
00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:19.840
if he is still conscious,

447
00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:23.160
and I think I can promise you
he will be all right by Saturday.

448
00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:27.000
- Good!
- Of course the music is a great difficulty.

449
00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:29.280
If one plays good music,
people won't listen,

450
00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.520
and if one plays bad music,
people won't talk.

451
00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:35.040
But I will run over the programme
I've drawn out,

452
00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:38.520
if you will kindly follow me
into the next room for a moment.

453
00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:40.400
Thank you, Algernon.

454
00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:42.520
That is very thoughtful of you.

455
00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:46.760
I'm sure
the programme will be delightful,

456
00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:49.560
after a few expurgations.

457
00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:53.240
French songs I cannot possibly allow.

458
00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:56.280
People always seem to think
that they are improper,

459
00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:59.320
and either look shocked,
which is vulgar,

460
00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:02.360
or laugh, which is worse.

461
00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:06.840
But German sounds
a thoroughly respectable language,

462
00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:10.280
and indeed, I believe is so.

463
00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:12.920
Gwendolen,
you will accompany me.

464
00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:14.360
Certainly, mamma.

465
00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:24.480
- (Growls lasciviously)
- (Audience laughing)

466
00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:26.800
(Crockery clattering)

467
00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:32.720
(Audience laughing)

468
00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:35.040
(Grunting)

469
00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:39.040
(She gasps)

470
00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:43.160
Charming day it has been,
Miss Fairfax.

471
00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:46.000
Pray don't talk to me about the weather,
Mr Worthing.

472
00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:48.000
Whenever people talk to me
about the weather,

473
00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:53.200
I always feel quite certain
that they mean something else.

474
00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:55.360
And that makes me so nervous.

475
00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:57.760
- I do mean something else.
- I thought so.

476
00:28:57.880 --> 00:28:59.600
In fact, I am never wrong.

477
00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:01.160
And I'd like to be allowed
to take advantage

478
00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:02.720
of Lady Bracknell's temporary absence...

479
00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:04.600
I would certainly advise you to do so.

480
00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:06.880
Mamma has a way
of coming back suddenly into a room

481
00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:08.560
that I've often had to speak to her about.

482
00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:11.720
- Miss Fairfax...
- Hmm?

483
00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:16.080
Ever since I met you,
I have admired you more than any girl...

484
00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:19.600
- (She chuckles nervously)
- ...I have ever met since... I met you.

485
00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.560
Yes, I am quite aware of the fact.

486
00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:28.960
And I often wish in public, at any rate,
you had been more demonstrative.

487
00:29:29.080 --> 00:29:32.920
For me you've always had
an irresistible fascination.

488
00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:36.640
Even before I met you,
I was far from indifferent to you.

489
00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:40.920
We live, as I hope you know,
Mr Worthing, in an age of ideals.

490
00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:44.760
The fact is constantly mentioned
in the more expensive monthly magazines,

491
00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:48.600
and has reached
the provincial pulpits I am told.

492
00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:55.240
And my ideal has always been
to love someone of the name of Ernest.

493
00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:56.800
(Chuckling)

494
00:29:57.560 --> 00:29:59.080
(Audience chuckling)

495
00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:03.560
There is something about that name
that inspires absolute confidence.

496
00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:08.080
The moment Algernon first mentioned to me
that he had a friend called Ernest,

497
00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:11.080
I knew I was destined to love you.

498
00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.160
- Do you really love me, Gwendolen?
- Passionately!

499
00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:17.360
Darling! You don't know
how happy you've made me.

500
00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:18.880
(Chuckles) My own Ernest!

501
00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:21.960
But you don't mean to say that you couldn't
love me if my name wasn't Ernest?

502
00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:24.760
- (Audience laughing)
- But your name is Ernest.

503
00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:27.400
Yes, I know it is.

504
00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:29.000
But supposing it was something else?

505
00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.160
Do you mean to say
that you couldn't love me then?

506
00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:36.080
Ah! Well, that is clearly
a metaphysical speculation,

507
00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:39.480
and like most metaphysical speculations
has very little reference at all

508
00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:43.240
to the actual facts of real life,
as we know them.

509
00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:44.720
Mm-hmm!

510
00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:49.360
Personally, darling,
to speak quite candidly,

511
00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:51.720
I don't much care
for the name of Ernest.

512
00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:54.360
I don't think the name suits me at all.

513
00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:56.600
It suits you perfectly.

514
00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:58.800
It is a divine name.

515
00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:03.080
It has a music of its own.
It produces vibrations.

516
00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:05.040
Well, really, Gwendolen,

517
00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:09.360
I must say I think there are lots
of other much nicer names.

518
00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:15.600
I think Jack, for instance,
a charming name.

519
00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:17.360
Jack?

520
00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:20.760
No!

521
00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:24.760
There is very little music in the name Jack,
if any at all, indeed.

522
00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:28.800
It does not thrill.
It produces absolutely no vibrations.

523
00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:30.080
(Audience laughing)

524
00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:31.800
I have known several Jacks,

525
00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:34.960
and they were all, without exception,
more than usually plain.

526
00:31:35.080 --> 00:31:38.800
Besides, Jack is
a notorious domesticity for John!

527
00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:41.240
- (Moaning)
- (Audience laughing)

528
00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:44.760
And I pity any woman
who is married to a man called John.

529
00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:46.640
She would probably
never be allowed to know

530
00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:50.000
the entrancing pleasure
of a single moment's solitude.

531
00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:54.280
Mm-mm!
The only really safe name is Ernest.

532
00:31:54.400 --> 00:31:57.760
Gwendolen, I must get christened at once -
I mean we must get married at once.

533
00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:01.080
- There's no time to be lost.
- Married, Mr Worthing?

534
00:32:01.240 --> 00:32:02.640
Well, surely.

535
00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:05.240
You know that I love you,

536
00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:11.000
and you led me to believe, Miss Fairfax,
that you were not absolutely indifferent to me.

537
00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:14.440
I adore you.
But you haven't proposed to me yet.

538
00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:16.600
Nothing at all has been said
about marriage.

539
00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:19.520
The subject hasn't even been touched on.

540
00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:20.760
(Audience chuckling)

541
00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:23.600
Well -
may I propose to you now?

542
00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:25.680
Oh, I think it would be
an admirable opportunity.

543
00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:28.200
And to spare you any possible
disappointment, Mr Worthing,

544
00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:30.560
I think it only fair to tell you
quite frankly beforehand

545
00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:32.360
that I am fully determined to accept you.

546
00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:33.960
Gwendolen!

547
00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:36.560
Yes, Mr Worthing,
what have you got to say to me?

548
00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:38.560
You know what I've got to say to you.

549
00:32:38.680 --> 00:32:40.680
Yes, but you don't say it.

550
00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:42.640
(Growling, squealing)

551
00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.640
(Audience laughing)

552
00:32:46.160 --> 00:32:47.680
Gwendolen, will you marry me?

553
00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:50.480
Of course I will, darling.

554
00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:52.800
How long you've been about it!

555
00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:56.400
I'm afraid you've had very little experience
in how to propose.

556
00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:59.320
My own one, I've never loved
anyone in the world but you.

557
00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:02.360
Yes, but men often propose
for practice.

558
00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.320
I know my brother Gerald does.

559
00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:06.440
All my girlfriends tell me so.

560
00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:09.120
Uh... Oh!

561
00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:11.840
Huh!

562
00:33:11.960 --> 00:33:16.320
What wonderfully blue eyes
you have, Ernest!

563
00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.360
They are quite, quite blue.

564
00:33:19.960 --> 00:33:23.160
I hope you'll always look at me
just like that,

565
00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:26.600
especially when there are
other people present.

566
00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:29.160
Mr Worthing!

567
00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:33.760
- Rise, sir, from this semi-recumbent posture.
- (Audience laughing)

568
00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:35.920
It is most indecorous.

569
00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:37.880
Mamma! Ooh!

570
00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:39.800
I must beg you to retire.

571
00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.240
This is no place for you...

572
00:33:42.360 --> 00:33:46.360
Besides, Mr Worthing
has not quite finished yet.

573
00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:49.080
(Audience laughing)

574
00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:51.080
Finished what, may I ask?

575
00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.840
I am engaged to Mr Worthing, mamma.

576
00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:59.000
Pardon me,
you are not engaged to anyone.

577
00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:02.240
When you do become
engaged to someone,

578
00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:07.600
I, or your father, should his health permit him,
will inform you of the fact.

579
00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:12.160
An engagement should come
on a young girl as a surprise,

580
00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:15.720
pleasant or unpleasant,
as the case may be.

581
00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:20.240
It is hardly a matter that she could be allowed
to arrange for herself.

582
00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:21.600
(Squeals) Ooh!

583
00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:25.880
And now I have a few questions
to put to you, Mr Worthing.

584
00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:27.960
While I am making these inquiries,

585
00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:31.680
you, Gwendolen,
will wait for me below in the carriage.

586
00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:34.520
- Mamma!
- In the carriage, Gwendolen!

587
00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:36.840
Mm...

588
00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:43.440
- Gwendolen, the carriage!
- (Squeals) Yes, mamma.

589
00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:45.040
(Grunting)

590
00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:47.040
(Audience laughing)

591
00:34:48.720 --> 00:34:51.880
You can take a seat, Mr Worthing.

592
00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:54.440
Thank you, Lady Bracknell,
I prefer standing.

593
00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:56.000
Mm!

594
00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:59.040
I feel bound to tell you

595
00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:03.720
that you are not down on my list
of eligible young men,

596
00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:08.200
although I have the same list
as the dear Duchess of Bolton has.

597
00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:10.360
We work together, in fact.

598
00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:13.920
However, I am quite ready
to enter your name,

599
00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:19.320
should your answers be
what a really affectionate mother requires.

600
00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:22.640
Do you smoke?

601
00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:26.920
Well, yes, I must admit I smoke.

602
00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:29.680
- I am glad to hear it.
- (Audience laughing)

603
00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:32.520
A man should always have
an occupation of some kind.

604
00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:33.840
(Audience laughing)

605
00:35:33.960 --> 00:35:38.080
There are far too many idle men
in London as it is.

606
00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:40.520
- How old are you?
- Twenty-nine.

607
00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:43.360
A very good age to be married at.

608
00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:45.720
I have always been of the opinion

609
00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:51.560
that a man who desires to get married
should know either everything or nothing.

610
00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:53.880
Which do you know?

611
00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:58.920
Uh... I know nothing, Lady Bracknell.

612
00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:02.080
- I am pleased to hear it.
- (Audience laughing)

613
00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:07.920
I do not approve of anything
that tampers with natural ignorance.

614
00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:11.680
Ignorance
is like a delicate exotic fruit -

615
00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:15.640
touch it and the bloom is gone.

616
00:36:15.760 --> 00:36:20.080
The whole theory of modern education
is radically unsound.

617
00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:26.000
Fortunately, in England at any rate,
education produces no effect whatsoever.

618
00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:27.680
(Audience laughing)

619
00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:33.040
If it did, it would prove
a serious danger to the upper classes,

620
00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:37.240
and probably lead to acts of violence
in Grosvenor Square.

621
00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:38.960
What is your income?

622
00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:42.280
Between seven and eight thousand a year.

623
00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:44.480
In land, or in investments?

624
00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:46.720
In investments, chiefly.

625
00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:49.560
That is satisfactory.

626
00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:53.160
What between the duties expected
of one during one's lifetime,

627
00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:56.400
and the duties exacted
from one after one's death,

628
00:36:56.520 --> 00:37:00.440
land has ceased to be
either a profit or a pleasure.

629
00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:05.400
It gives one position,
and prevents one from keeping it up.

630
00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:08.480
That's all that can be said about land.

631
00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:13.880
Well, I have a country house with some land,
of course, attached to it,

632
00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:17.000
about fifteen hundred acres, I believe,

633
00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:19.160
but I don't depend on that
for my real income.

634
00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:21.160
In fact, as far as I can make out,

635
00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:24.280
the poachers are the only people
who make anything out of it.

636
00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:27.240
A country house!
How many bedrooms?

637
00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:29.600
Well, that point
can be cleared up later.

638
00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:32.480
You have a town house, I hope?

639
00:37:32.600 --> 00:37:36.960
A girl with a simple, unspoiled nature,
like Gwendolen,

640
00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:40.680
could hardly be expected
to reside in the country.

641
00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:46.280
Well, I own a house in Belgrave Square,
but it's let by the year to Lady Bloxham.

642
00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:49.560
Of course, I can get it back whenever I like,
at six months' notice.

643
00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:52.240
Lady Bloxham?

644
00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:54.920
- I don't know her.
- Oh, she goes about very little.

645
00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:58.000
She's a lady
considerably advanced in years.

646
00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:02.640
Mm! Nowadays that is no guarantee
of respectability of character.

647
00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:06.360
- What number in Belgrave Square?
- 149.

648
00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:10.400
- Oh! The unfashionable side.
- (Audience laughing)

649
00:38:10.520 --> 00:38:12.400
I thought there was something.

650
00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.680
However, that could easily be altered.

651
00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:19.040
Do you mean the fashion, or the side?

652
00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:21.600
Both, if necessary, I presume.

653
00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:23.720
What are your politics?

654
00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:26.520
Well, I'm afraid I really have none.
I'm a Liberal.

655
00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:29.960
Oh, they count as Tories.

656
00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:34.440
They dine with us.
Or come in the evening, at any rate.

657
00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:37.800
Now to minor matters.

658
00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:40.680
Are your parents living?

659
00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:47.200
I have lost both my parents.

660
00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:48.760
Both?

661
00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:52.640
To lose one parent
may be regarded as a misfortune.

662
00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:55.920
But to lose both
seems like carelessness.

663
00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:57.480
(Audience laughing)

664
00:38:57.600 --> 00:38:59.280
Who was your father?

665
00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:02.040
He was evidently
a man of some wealth.

666
00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:07.160
Was he born in what the Radical papers
call the purple of commerce,

667
00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:11.160
or did he rise from the ranks
of the aristocracy?

668
00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:13.360
I'm afraid I really don't know.

669
00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:17.000
The fact is, Lady Bracknell,
I said I had lost my parents.

670
00:39:17.160 --> 00:39:21.560
It would be nearer the truth to say
that my parents seem to have lost me.

671
00:39:21.680 --> 00:39:25.040
I don't actually know
who I am by birth. I was...

672
00:39:25.200 --> 00:39:27.840
- Well, I was found.
- Found?

673
00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.000
The late Mr Thomas Cardew,

674
00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:33.680
an old gentleman of very charitable
and kindly disposition,

675
00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:36.000
found me and gave me the name
of Worthing,

676
00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:40.200
because he happened to have a first-class
ticket for Worthing in his pocket at the time.

677
00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:44.040
Worthing's a place in Sussex.
It's a seaside resort.

678
00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:46.480
Where did this charitable gentleman

679
00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:51.520
who had a first-class ticket
to this seaside resort find you?

680
00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:54.240
In a handbag.

681
00:39:57.880 --> 00:40:00.680
- A handbag?
- (Audience laughing)

682
00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:05.520
Yes, Lady Bracknell,
I was in a handbag -

683
00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:11.240
a somewhat large, black leather handbag,
with handles to it -

684
00:40:11.360 --> 00:40:12.880
it was an ordinary handbag, in fact.

685
00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:19.240
In what locality did this Mr James,
or Thomas, Cardew

686
00:40:19.360 --> 00:40:22.640
come across this ordinary handbag?

687
00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:24.680
In the cloak-room at Victoria Station.

688
00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:28.280
- It was given to him in mistake for his own.
- The cloak-room at Victoria Station?

689
00:40:28.400 --> 00:40:29.920
Yes.
The Brighton line.

690
00:40:30.040 --> 00:40:32.520
The line is immaterial.

691
00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:35.360
(Audience laughing)

692
00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:37.000
Mr Worthing...

693
00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:43.240
I confess, I... I feel somewhat bewildered
by what you have just told me.

694
00:40:43.360 --> 00:40:48.080
To be born, or at any rate bred,
in a handbag,

695
00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:50.600
whether it had handles or not,

696
00:40:50.720 --> 00:40:56.440
seems to me to display a contempt
for the ordinary decencies of family life

697
00:40:56.560 --> 00:41:01.600
that reminds one of the worst excesses
of the French Revolution.

698
00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:05.680
And I presume you know
what that unfortunate movement led to?

699
00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:08.160
- No. Do you?
- (Audience laughing)

700
00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:13.640
As for the particular locality
in which the handbag was found,

701
00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:22.440
a cloak-room at a railway station
might serve to conceal a social indiscretion -

702
00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:27.160
indeed has probably been used
for that very purpose before now -

703
00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:30.280
but it could hardly be regarded
as an assured basis

704
00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:34.320
for a recognised position in good society.

705
00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:38.720
May I ask you then
what you'd advise me to do?

706
00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:42.840
I need hardly say I'd do anything in the world
to ensure Gwendolen's happiness.

707
00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.760
I would strongly advise you, Mr Worthing,

708
00:41:45.880 --> 00:41:50.240
to try and acquire some relations
as soon as possible,

709
00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:56.200
and to make a definite effort to produce
at any rate one parent of either sex,

710
00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:58.720
before the season is quite over.

711
00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:02.760
I don't see how I could possibly
manage to do that.

712
00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:04.680
I can produce the handbag
at any moment.

713
00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:06.240
It's in my dressing-room at home.

714
00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:08.320
I really think that should satisfy you,
Lady Bracknell.

715
00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:12.160
Me, sir?
What has it to do with me?

716
00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:15.680
You could hardly imagine
that I and Lord Bracknell

717
00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:18.880
would dream of allowing
our only daughter,

718
00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:22.200
a girl brought up with the utmost care,

719
00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:25.680
to marry into a cloak-room

720
00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:29.720
and form an alliance with a parcel!

721
00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:34.600
Good day, Mr Worthing!

722
00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:37.880
(Applause)

723
00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:46.360
(Audience laughing)

724
00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:56.880
- (♪ "Marry You" by Bruno Mars on piano)
- Oh, for goodness' sake!

725
00:43:07.040 --> 00:43:09.600
Don't play that ghastly tune, Algy!

726
00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:13.840
- How idiotic you are!
- (♪ Song ends with glissando)

727
00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:19.240
Didn't it go off all right, old boy?

728
00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:21.760
You don't mean to say
Gwendolen refused you?

729
00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:25.000
I know it is a way she has.
She's always refusing people.

730
00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.840
I think it is most ill-natured of her.

731
00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:30.200
- Gwendolen is as right as a trivet.
- Well...

732
00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:32.400
As far as she is concerned,
we are engaged.

733
00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:36.560
- Her mother...
- Ah!

734
00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:38.400
- Perfectly unbearable.
- (Algernon laughing)

735
00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:41.240
- Never met such a Gorgon.
- (Audience laughing)

736
00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:45.200
I don't really know what a Gorgon is like,
but I'm quite sure Lady Bracknell is one.

737
00:43:45.320 --> 00:43:48.680
In any case, she is a monster,
without being a myth,

738
00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:50.280
- which is rather unfair.
- Jack!

739
00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:52.400
I beg your pardon, Algy.

740
00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:56.280
I suppose I shouldn't talk about your own aunt
that way before you.

741
00:43:56.400 --> 00:44:01.960
My dear boy,
I love hearing my relations abused.

742
00:44:02.080 --> 00:44:05.000
It is the only thing that makes me
put up with them at all.

743
00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:08.400
Relations are simply
a tedious pack of people

744
00:44:08.520 --> 00:44:11.000
who haven't the faintest knowledge
of how to live,

745
00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:13.600
nor the smallest instinct
about when to die.

746
00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:16.480
- Oh, that is nonsense!
- It isn't!

747
00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:20.080
Well, I won't argue about the matter.
You always want to argue about things.

748
00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:23.680
Well, that is exactly what things
were originally made for.

749
00:44:23.800 --> 00:44:26.400
Upon my word, if I thought that,
I'd shoot myself.

750
00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:28.040
(Algernon laughing)

751
00:44:30.400 --> 00:44:33.040
You don't think there's any chance
of Gwendolen becoming like her mother

752
00:44:33.200 --> 00:44:36.000
in about a hundred and fifty years,
do you, Algy?

753
00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:40.200
All women become like their mothers.
That is their tragedy.

754
00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:42.320
No man does.

755
00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:43.920
That is his.

756
00:44:45.280 --> 00:44:47.240
Is that clever?

757
00:44:47.360 --> 00:44:49.480
It is perfectly phrased!

758
00:44:49.600 --> 00:44:52.880
And quite as true as any observation
in civilised life should be.

759
00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:56.400
- I'm sick to death of cleverness.
- Oh!

760
00:44:56.520 --> 00:44:58.560
Everybody's clever nowadays.

761
00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:00.800
You can't go anywhere
without meeting clever people.

762
00:45:00.920 --> 00:45:03.560
The thing has become
an absolute public nuisance.

763
00:45:03.680 --> 00:45:07.080
I wish to goodness
we had a few fools left.

764
00:45:07.240 --> 00:45:08.560
We have.

765
00:45:08.680 --> 00:45:10.760
- (Audience laughing)
- I should extremely like to meet them.

766
00:45:10.880 --> 00:45:11.920
What do they talk about?

767
00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:14.600
The fools?
Oh, clever people, of course.

768
00:45:14.720 --> 00:45:16.440
What fools!

769
00:45:18.440 --> 00:45:19.880
By the way...

770
00:45:20.920 --> 00:45:24.240
...did you tell Gwendolen the truth

771
00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:26.840
about your being Ernest in town,
Jack in the country?

772
00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:30.400
My dear fellow,
the truth is not the sort of thing

773
00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:32.960
- one tells to a nice, sweet, refined girl.
- Oh, Jack!

774
00:45:33.880 --> 00:45:37.000
What extraordinary ideas you have
about the way to behave to a woman!

775
00:45:37.160 --> 00:45:42.280
Oh! The only way to behave to a woman
is to make love to her if she is pretty,

776
00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:44.400
and someone else if she is plain.

777
00:45:44.520 --> 00:45:47.800
- That is nonsense.
- What about your brother? Hmm?

778
00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:49.680
What about the profligate Ernest?

779
00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:51.960
Before the end of the week
I shall have got rid of him.

780
00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:57.640
- Oh!
- I'll say he died in Paris of apoplexy.

781
00:45:58.720 --> 00:46:01.080
Lots of people die quite suddenly
of apoplexy, don't they?

782
00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:03.800
Yes, but it is hereditary, my dear fellow.

783
00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:05.640
It's a sort of thing that runs in families.

784
00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:09.880
You had much better say...
a severe chill.

785
00:46:11.320 --> 00:46:14.360
You're quite sure that a severe chill
isn't hereditary, or anything of that kind?

786
00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:15.960
Of course it isn't!

787
00:46:16.080 --> 00:46:17.280
- Very well, then.
- Hmm.

788
00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:22.280
My poor brother Ernest is carried off suddenly
in Paris by a severe chill.

789
00:46:22.400 --> 00:46:24.240
- Oh...
- That gets rid of him.

790
00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:27.440
But I... I thought you said Miss Cardew
was a little too much interested

791
00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:29.600
in your poor brother Ernest?

792
00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:31.240
Won't she feel his loss a good deal?

793
00:46:31.360 --> 00:46:35.000
Oh, no, that's all right. Cecily's not
a silly romantic girl, I'm glad to say.

794
00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:37.760
She's got a capital appetite,
goes on long walks,

795
00:46:37.880 --> 00:46:39.680
and pays no attention at all
to her lessons.

796
00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:41.880
I'd rather like to see Cecily.

797
00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:44.640
- I will take very good care you never do.
- Uh!

798
00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:47.240
She's excessively pretty.

799
00:46:47.360 --> 00:46:49.840
- She's only just turned eighteen.
- Have you told Gwendolen yet

800
00:46:49.960 --> 00:46:53.280
you have an excessively pretty ward
who only just turned eighteen?

801
00:46:53.400 --> 00:46:56.360
One doesn't just
blurt these things out to people.

802
00:46:56.480 --> 00:46:59.000
Cecily and Gwendolen are perfectly certain
to be extremely great friends.

803
00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:01.200
I'll bet you anything you like
that half an hour after they have met,

804
00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:02.760
they'll be calling each other sister.

805
00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:06.000
Women only do that when they've called
each other a lot of other things first.

806
00:47:06.160 --> 00:47:08.680
- (Audience laughing)
- Now, my dear boy,

807
00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:13.680
if we want to get a good table at Willis's,
we really must go and dress.

808
00:47:13.800 --> 00:47:15.080
Do you know it is nearly seven?

809
00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:17.080
Oh!
It's always nearly seven.

810
00:47:17.240 --> 00:47:20.600
- Well, I'm hungry.
- I never knew when you weren't.

811
00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:24.080
(Clears throat) What shall we do after dinner?
Shall we go to the theatre?

812
00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:26.560
Oh, no!
I loathe listening.

813
00:47:26.680 --> 00:47:28.640
(Audience laughing)

814
00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:35.040
Well, let us go to the club?

815
00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:37.280
No, I hate talking.

816
00:47:37.400 --> 00:47:39.440
Well, we might trot round
the Empire at ten?

817
00:47:39.560 --> 00:47:42.440
No, I can't bear looking at things.
It's so silly.

818
00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:44.240
Well, what shall we do?

819
00:47:44.360 --> 00:47:48.560
- Nothing!
- It is awfully hard work doing nothing.

820
00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:53.160
However, I don't mind hard work
when there's no definite object of any kind.

821
00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:55.200
Miss Fairfax.

822
00:47:55.320 --> 00:47:56.480
Oh!

823
00:47:56.600 --> 00:47:58.480
Gwendolen, upon my word!

824
00:47:58.600 --> 00:47:59.800
Algy, kindly turn your back.

825
00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:02.280
I have something very particular to say
to Mr Worthing.

826
00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:06.320
Really, Gwendolen,
I don't think I can allow this at all.

827
00:48:06.440 --> 00:48:10.680
Algy, you always adopt
a strictly immoral attitude towards life.

828
00:48:10.800 --> 00:48:12.840
You are not quite old enough to do that.

829
00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:14.520
(Grunts)

830
00:48:15.840 --> 00:48:17.560
- (Squeals)
- My own darling!

831
00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:20.840
Ernest, we may never be married.

832
00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:23.920
From the expression on mamma's face
I fear we never shall.

833
00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:28.400
Few parents nowadays pay any regard
to what their children say to them.

834
00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:32.440
The old-fashioned respect
for the young is fast dying out.

835
00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:36.920
Whatever influence I ever had over mamma,
I lost at the age of three.

836
00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:40.520
But although she may prevent us
from becoming man and wife,

837
00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:45.320
and I may marry someone else,
and marry often, ooh,

838
00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:51.360
nothing she can possibly do
can alter my eternal devotion to you.

839
00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:52.880
Dear Gwendolen!

840
00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:55.080
The story of your romantic origin,

841
00:48:55.240 --> 00:48:59.840
as related to me by mamma,
with unpleasing comments,

842
00:48:59.960 --> 00:49:04.240
has naturally stirred
the deeper fibres of my nature.

843
00:49:04.360 --> 00:49:09.440
Your Christian name
has always had an irresistible fascination.

844
00:49:09.560 --> 00:49:14.800
The simplicity of your character makes you
exquisitely incomprehensible to me.

845
00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:19.320
Your town address at the Albany I have,
but what is your address in the country?

846
00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:22.480
- The Manor House.
- The Manor House.

847
00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:23.720
- Woolton.
- Woolton.

848
00:49:23.840 --> 00:49:25.600
- Hertfordshire.
- Hertfordshire.

849
00:49:25.720 --> 00:49:28.440
Oh, there is a good postal service,
I suppose?

850
00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:31.560
It may be necessary
to do something desperate.

851
00:49:31.680 --> 00:49:35.640
Of course, that will require
serious consideration.

852
00:49:35.760 --> 00:49:37.920
I will communicate with you daily.

853
00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:40.760
- My own one!
- How long do you remain in town?

854
00:49:40.880 --> 00:49:42.880
- Till Monday.
- Good!

855
00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:45.760
Algy, you may turn round now.

856
00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:48.640
Thanks,
I've turned around already.

857
00:49:48.760 --> 00:49:50.440
You may also ring the bell.

858
00:49:50.560 --> 00:49:52.320
(Bell ringing)

859
00:49:52.440 --> 00:49:54.600
You will let me see you to your carriage,
my own darling?

860
00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:56.560
Oh, certainly.

861
00:49:57.320 --> 00:49:59.640
- I will see Miss Fairfax out.
- Yes, sir.

862
00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:02.240
(Algernon blowing kisses)

863
00:50:04.920 --> 00:50:06.360
(Sighs)

864
00:50:07.880 --> 00:50:09.360
Savoy.

865
00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:10.520
Savoy.

866
00:50:11.240 --> 00:50:12.640
Savoy.

867
00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:16.240
Dalston Superstore.

868
00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:17.600
(Audience laughing)

869
00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:20.680
Oh! (Laughing)

870
00:50:21.840 --> 00:50:23.760
Oh, wonderful.

871
00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:25.640
(Algernon laughing)

872
00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:28.520
- There's a sensible, intellectual girl!
- (Algernon laughing)

873
00:50:28.640 --> 00:50:33.200
- The only girl I've ever cared for in my life.
- Mm! (Laughing)

874
00:50:33.320 --> 00:50:35.040
What on earth are you so amused at?

875
00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:38.640
Oh, just a little anxious
about poor Bunbury, that's all.

876
00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:43.800
If you don't take care, your friend Bunbury
will get you into a serious scrape someday.

877
00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:45.800
(Gasps) I love scrapes.

878
00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:48.560
They are the only things
that are never serious.

879
00:50:48.680 --> 00:50:51.920
That is nonsense.
You never talk anything but nonsense.

880
00:50:53.320 --> 00:50:55.280
Nobody ever does.

881
00:50:59.080 --> 00:51:01.040
(Audience laughing)

882
00:51:02.240 --> 00:51:04.360
Lane! Lane...

883
00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:06.160
Yes, sir?

884
00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:09.720
- A glass of sherry, Lane.
- Yes, sir.

885
00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:17.040
Tomorrow, Lane...

886
00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:20.360
...I'm going Bunburying.

887
00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:23.080
(♪ Drum roll)

888
00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:31.320
(Audience laughing)

889
00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:35.800
- Yes, sir.
- I shall probably not be back till Monday.

890
00:51:35.920 --> 00:51:39.440
You can hang up my dress clothes,
my smoking jacket,

891
00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:43.320
- and all the Bunbury suits.
- Yes, sir.

892
00:51:43.440 --> 00:51:45.800
(♪ Music starts)

893
00:52:03.560 --> 00:52:05.880
(♪ Music intensifies)

894
00:52:46.160 --> 00:52:48.320
(♪ Music fades)

895
00:52:48.440 --> 00:52:51.520
I hope tomorrow will be a fine day, Lane.

896
00:52:51.640 --> 00:52:53.840
It never is, sir.

897
00:52:53.960 --> 00:52:56.560
Lane, you are a perfect pessimist.

898
00:52:56.680 --> 00:53:00.720
I do my best to give satisfaction, sir.

899
00:53:00.840 --> 00:53:03.760
(♪ Music intensifies)

900
00:53:04.720 --> 00:53:06.240
(♪ Music pauses on single note)

901
00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:09.560
- (♪ Music resumes)
- (Audience cheering, applauding)

902
00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:36.160
(♪ Music stops)

903
00:53:36.280 --> 00:53:37.480
Cecily!

904
00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:40.880
- ♪ Oh, girls, they want to have fun
- (Audience laughing)

905
00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:43.400
♪ Oh, girls just want to have...

906
00:53:43.520 --> 00:53:46.880
- ♪ That's all they really want
- Cecily!

907
00:53:47.000 --> 00:53:49.800
♪ Some fun... ♪

908
00:53:49.920 --> 00:53:51.720
Cecily!

909
00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:54.280
- Yes, Miss Prism?
- Cecily!

910
00:53:54.400 --> 00:53:58.920
Surely, such a utilitarian occupation
as the watering of flowers

911
00:53:59.040 --> 00:54:02.360
is rather Moulton's duty than yours?

912
00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:08.240
Especially at a moment
when intellectual pleasures await you.

913
00:54:08.360 --> 00:54:11.000
Your German grammar is on the table.

914
00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:13.000
Pray open it at page fifteen.

915
00:54:13.160 --> 00:54:15.160
We will repeat yesterday's lesson.

916
00:54:15.280 --> 00:54:17.440
But I don't like German.

917
00:54:17.560 --> 00:54:19.720
It isn't at all a becoming language.

918
00:54:19.840 --> 00:54:24.200
I know perfectly well that I look quite plain
after my German lesson.

919
00:54:24.320 --> 00:54:27.400
Child, you know
how anxious your guardian is

920
00:54:27.520 --> 00:54:30.960
that you should improve yourself
in every way.

921
00:54:31.080 --> 00:54:33.240
He laid particular stress on your German,

922
00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:35.840
as he was leaving for town yesterday.

923
00:54:35.960 --> 00:54:40.720
Indeed, he always lays stress
on your German when he is leaving for town.

924
00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:43.960
Dear Uncle Jack is so very serious!

925
00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:47.760
Sometimes he is so serious,
I think he cannot be quite well.

926
00:54:47.880 --> 00:54:51.160
Your guardian enjoys the best of health,

927
00:54:51.280 --> 00:54:55.520
and his gravity of demeanour
is especially to be commended

928
00:54:55.640 --> 00:54:59.760
in one so comparatively young as he is.

929
00:54:59.880 --> 00:55:05.160
I know no one who has a higher sense of duty
and responsibility.

930
00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:09.360
I suppose that is why he often looks
a little bored when we three are together.

931
00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:12.640
Cecily!
I am surprised at you.

932
00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:16.800
Mr Worthing has many troubles in his life.

933
00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:22.840
Idle merriment and triviality
would be out of place in his conversation.

934
00:55:22.960 --> 00:55:30.000
You must remember his constant anxiety
about that unfortunate young man, his brother.

935
00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:34.480
I wish Uncle Jack would allow
that unfortunate young man, his brother,

936
00:55:34.600 --> 00:55:36.720
- to come down here sometimes.
- Oh!

937
00:55:36.840 --> 00:55:39.840
We might have a good influence
over him, Miss Prism.

938
00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:41.960
I am sure you certainly would.

939
00:55:42.080 --> 00:55:44.280
You know German and geology,

940
00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:48.040
and things of that kind
influence a man very much.

941
00:55:48.200 --> 00:55:52.440
I do not think that even I
could produce any effect on a character

942
00:55:52.560 --> 00:55:59.400
that according to his own brother's admission
is irretrievably weak and vacillating.

943
00:56:00.680 --> 00:56:05.040
Indeed I am not sure
that I would desire to reclaim him.

944
00:56:05.200 --> 00:56:07.920
I'm not in favour of this modern mania

945
00:56:08.040 --> 00:56:12.560
for turning bad people into good people
at a moment's notice.

946
00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:15.720
As a man sows, so let him reap.

947
00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:18.800
But men don't sew, Miss Prism.

948
00:56:19.640 --> 00:56:21.840
- You must put away your diary, Cecily.
- Oh!

949
00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:25.040
I really don't see
why you keep a diary at all.

950
00:56:25.200 --> 00:56:29.560
I keep a diary in order to enter
the wonderful secrets of my life.

951
00:56:29.680 --> 00:56:33.360
If I didn't write them down,
I should probably forget all about them.

952
00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:38.800
Memory, my dear Cecily,
is the diary that we all carry about with us.

953
00:56:38.920 --> 00:56:42.760
Yes, but it usually chronicles
the things that have never happened,

954
00:56:42.880 --> 00:56:44.680
and couldn't possibly have happened.

955
00:56:44.800 --> 00:56:48.800
I believe that memory is responsible
for nearly all the three-volume novels

956
00:56:48.920 --> 00:56:50.600
Woman's World sends us.

957
00:56:50.720 --> 00:56:55.080
Do not speak slightingly
of the three-volume novel, Cecily.

958
00:56:56.520 --> 00:57:00.240
I wrote one myself in earlier days.

959
00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:02.560
Did you really, Miss Prism?

960
00:57:02.680 --> 00:57:05.480
How wonderfully clever you are!

961
00:57:05.600 --> 00:57:07.800
I hope it did not end happily?

962
00:57:07.920 --> 00:57:12.080
I don't like novels that end happily.
They depress me so much.

963
00:57:12.680 --> 00:57:16.560
The good ended happily,
the bad unhappily.

964
00:57:16.680 --> 00:57:18.720
That is what fiction means.

965
00:57:18.840 --> 00:57:22.440
I suppose so,
but it seems very unfair.

966
00:57:22.560 --> 00:57:25.080
And was your novel ever published?

967
00:57:25.240 --> 00:57:27.720
Oh, alas! No.

968
00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:31.720
No, the manuscript unfortunately
was abandoned.

969
00:57:31.840 --> 00:57:36.280
I use the word
in the sense of lost or mislaid.

970
00:57:37.040 --> 00:57:41.880
To your work, child,
these speculations are profitless.

971
00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:43.040
Genders.

972
00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:46.960
In German,
diminutives are always neuter,

973
00:57:47.080 --> 00:57:50.200
that is they belong to neither sex,

974
00:57:50.320 --> 00:57:52.920
even when appearances
are to the contrary.

975
00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:57.760
As in "das Mädchen", neuter,
the young girl,

976
00:57:57.880 --> 00:58:00.440
"das Fräulein", the young lady,
you see?

977
00:58:00.560 --> 00:58:02.960
But I see dear Dr Chasuble
coming up through the garden.

978
00:58:03.080 --> 00:58:04.880
Oh... ah!

979
00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:06.800
Dr Chasuble!

980
00:58:06.920 --> 00:58:09.640
Uh, this...
This is indeed a pleasure.

981
00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:11.840
And how are we this morning?

982
00:58:11.960 --> 00:58:14.160
Miss Prism, you are, I trust, well?

983
00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:17.520
Miss Prism has just been complaining
of a slight headache.

984
00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:19.560
I think it would do her so much good

985
00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:22.520
to have a short stroll with you
in the park, Dr Chasuble.

986
00:58:22.640 --> 00:58:26.040
Cecily, I have not mentioned anything
about a headache.

987
00:58:26.200 --> 00:58:28.200
No, dear Miss Prism, I know that,

988
00:58:28.320 --> 00:58:32.080
but I felt instinctively
that you had a headache.

989
00:58:32.240 --> 00:58:35.760
Indeed, I was thinking about that
and not about my German lesson,

990
00:58:35.880 --> 00:58:37.160
when the Rector came in.

991
00:58:37.280 --> 00:58:40.400
I hope, Cecily, you are not inattentive.

992
00:58:40.520 --> 00:58:42.800
Oh, I'm afraid I am.

993
00:58:42.920 --> 00:58:44.640
Well, that is strange.

994
00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:47.960
Were I fortunate enough
to be Miss Prism's pupil,

995
00:58:48.080 --> 00:58:50.320
I would hang upon her lips.

996
00:58:50.440 --> 00:58:52.280
(Audience chuckling)

997
00:58:53.760 --> 00:58:56.400
I spoke metaphorically.

998
00:58:57.440 --> 00:59:00.960
My metaphor was drawn from bees.

999
00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:02.560
Ahem!

1000
00:59:03.520 --> 00:59:07.160
Mr Worthing, I suppose,
has not returned from town yet?

1001
00:59:07.280 --> 00:59:10.400
We do not expect him
till Monday afternoon.

1002
00:59:10.520 --> 00:59:15.040
Oh, yes, he usually likes
to spend his Sunday in London.

1003
00:59:15.200 --> 00:59:18.960
He is not one of those
whose sole aim is enjoyment,

1004
00:59:19.080 --> 00:59:24.880
as, by all accounts, that unfortunate
young man his brother seems to be.

1005
00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:29.440
But I must not disturb Egeria
and her pupil any longer.

1006
00:59:29.560 --> 00:59:31.680
Uh... Egeria?

1007
00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:34.280
My name is Laetitia, Doctor.

1008
00:59:34.400 --> 00:59:39.360
A classical allusion merely,
drawn from the Pagan authors.

1009
00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:41.880
I shall see you both no doubt
at Evensong?

1010
00:59:42.000 --> 00:59:47.640
(Moaning) Oh, I think, dear Doctor,
I will have a stroll with you.

1011
00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:51.840
I find I have a headache after all.
A walk might do it good.

1012
00:59:51.960 --> 00:59:54.800
With pleasure, Miss Prism,
with pleasure.

1013
00:59:56.440 --> 01:00:00.280
We might go
as far as the schools and back.

1014
01:00:00.400 --> 01:00:02.880
- That would be delightful.
- (Audience chuckling)

1015
01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:09.640
Cecily, you shall read your Political Economy
in my absence.

1016
01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:14.160
The chapter on the Fall of the Rupee
you may omit.

1017
01:00:14.280 --> 01:00:17.600
It is somewhat too sensational.

1018
01:00:17.720 --> 01:00:21.400
Even these metallic problems
have their melodramatic side.

1019
01:00:21.520 --> 01:00:24.320
Reading Political Economy, Cecily?

1020
01:00:24.440 --> 01:00:29.480
I suppose you know all about relations
between Capital and Labour?

1021
01:00:29.600 --> 01:00:33.440
All I know is about the relations
between Capital and Idleness.

1022
01:00:33.560 --> 01:00:36.640
Cecily, that sounds like Socialism!

1023
01:00:36.760 --> 01:00:38.480
- (Audience laughing)
- Dear child!

1024
01:00:38.600 --> 01:00:43.600
The fact is, you have fallen lately
into the habit of thinking for yourself.

1025
01:00:43.720 --> 01:00:45.520
You should give it up.

1026
01:00:45.640 --> 01:00:48.760
It's not quite womanly.

1027
01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:51.560
- A wilful lamb!
- Mm.

1028
01:01:01.840 --> 01:01:04.160
Horrid Political Economy!

1029
01:01:04.280 --> 01:01:05.800
Horrid Geography!

1030
01:01:05.920 --> 01:01:08.320
Horrid, horrid German! Ugh!

1031
01:01:10.880 --> 01:01:15.880
Mr Ernest Worthing
has just driven over from the station, Miss.

1032
01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:18.560
He has brought his luggage with him.

1033
01:01:19.720 --> 01:01:23.440
"Mr Ernest Worthing, B4, The Albany."

1034
01:01:23.560 --> 01:01:25.720
Uncle Jack's brother!

1035
01:01:25.840 --> 01:01:28.160
Did you tell him
Mr Worthing was in town?

1036
01:01:28.280 --> 01:01:31.080
Oh, yes, Miss.
He seemed very much disappointed.

1037
01:01:31.240 --> 01:01:34.440
I mentioned that you and Miss Prism
were in the garden.

1038
01:01:34.560 --> 01:01:39.640
He said he was anxious
to speak to you privately for a moment.

1039
01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:42.480
Ask Mr Ernest Worthing to come here.

1040
01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:46.320
I suppose you'd better talk
to the housekeeper about a room for him.

1041
01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:48.360
Yes, Miss.

1042
01:01:51.000 --> 01:01:54.920
I have never met
any really wicked person before.

1043
01:01:55.760 --> 01:01:58.280
I feel rather frightened.

1044
01:01:58.400 --> 01:02:01.440
I'm so afraid he will look
just like everyone else.

1045
01:02:04.160 --> 01:02:05.360
He does!

1046
01:02:05.480 --> 01:02:06.520
(Audience laughing)

1047
01:02:06.640 --> 01:02:08.040
(Both chuckling)

1048
01:02:08.200 --> 01:02:09.720
(Algernon clearing his throat)

1049
01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:13.160
You're my little cousin Cecily,
I'm sure.

1050
01:02:13.280 --> 01:02:15.880
You are under some strange mistake.

1051
01:02:16.000 --> 01:02:17.360
- I am not little.
- Oh.

1052
01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:19.960
In fact, I believe I am
more than usually tall for my age.

1053
01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:21.360
Ah! (Chuckles)

1054
01:02:21.480 --> 01:02:23.920
But I am your cousin Cecily.

1055
01:02:24.040 --> 01:02:27.000
You, I see from your card,
are Uncle Jack's brother,

1056
01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:31.480
my cousin Ernest,
my wicked cousin Ernest.

1057
01:02:31.600 --> 01:02:35.360
Oh! I'm really not wicked at all,
Cousin Cecily.

1058
01:02:35.480 --> 01:02:37.000
You mustn't think that I'm wicked.

1059
01:02:37.160 --> 01:02:40.080
If you are not, then you have
certainly been deceiving us all

1060
01:02:40.240 --> 01:02:42.720
in a very inexcusable manner.

1061
01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:44.840
I hope you have not been leading
a double life,

1062
01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:48.360
pretending to be wicked
and being really good all the time.

1063
01:02:48.480 --> 01:02:50.040
That would be hypocrisy.

1064
01:02:52.400 --> 01:02:54.240
(Audience laughing)

1065
01:02:54.360 --> 01:02:59.400
Oh! Of course
I have been rather reckless.

1066
01:02:59.520 --> 01:03:01.000
I am glad to hear it.

1067
01:03:01.160 --> 01:03:07.000
In fact, now that you mention the subject,
I have been very bad in my own small way.

1068
01:03:07.160 --> 01:03:10.000
I don't think you should be so proud of that,

1069
01:03:10.160 --> 01:03:12.320
though I'm sure
it must've been very pleasant.

1070
01:03:12.440 --> 01:03:15.080
It is much pleasanter
being here with you.

1071
01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:18.280
- I can't understand how you are here at all.
- (Algernon laughing)

1072
01:03:18.400 --> 01:03:20.600
Uncle Jack won't be back
till Monday afternoon.

1073
01:03:20.720 --> 01:03:24.160
That is a great disappointment.

1074
01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:27.560
I am obliged to go up by the first train
on Monday morning.

1075
01:03:27.680 --> 01:03:30.680
I have a business appointment
that I'm anxious... to miss.

1076
01:03:31.200 --> 01:03:33.640
Couldn't you miss it anywhere
but in London?

1077
01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:35.960
No, no, the appointment is in London.

1078
01:03:36.080 --> 01:03:40.680
Well, I know, of course, how important it is
not to keep a business engagement

1079
01:03:40.800 --> 01:03:44.440
if one wants to retain
any sense of the beauty of life,

1080
01:03:44.560 --> 01:03:48.680
but still I think you had better wait
till Uncle Jack arrives.

1081
01:03:48.800 --> 01:03:51.520
I know he wants to speak to you
about your emigrating.

1082
01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:54.360
(Audience laughing)

1083
01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:55.520
About my what?

1084
01:03:56.000 --> 01:03:58.720
Your emigrating.
He's gone up to buy your outfit.

1085
01:03:58.840 --> 01:04:02.360
I certainly wouldn't let Jack buy my outfit.

1086
01:04:02.480 --> 01:04:05.320
He has no taste in neckties at all.

1087
01:04:05.440 --> 01:04:08.880
- I don't think you'll require neckties.
- Oh?

1088
01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:11.840
Uncle Jack is sending you to Australia.

1089
01:04:11.960 --> 01:04:14.960
Australia!
(Laughs) I'd sooner die.

1090
01:04:15.760 --> 01:04:17.800
Well, he said at dinner
on Wednesday night,

1091
01:04:17.920 --> 01:04:23.320
that you would have to choose between
this world, the next world, and Australia.

1092
01:04:23.440 --> 01:04:27.720
Well! The accounts that I have received
of Australia and the next world

1093
01:04:27.840 --> 01:04:30.960
are not particularly encouraging,
Cousin Cecily.

1094
01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:32.920
This world is good enough for me.

1095
01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:36.640
Yes, but are you good enough for it?

1096
01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:39.920
- I'm afraid I'm not that.
- Mm.

1097
01:04:40.040 --> 01:04:44.400
That is why I want you to reform me.

1098
01:04:45.520 --> 01:04:49.640
You might make that your mission,
if you don't mind, Cousin Cecily.

1099
01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:52.200
- I'm afraid I've no time, this afternoon.
- Ah!

1100
01:04:52.760 --> 01:04:55.560
Well, would you mind
my reforming myself this afternoon?

1101
01:04:56.080 --> 01:04:58.360
It is rather Quixotic of you.

1102
01:04:58.480 --> 01:05:01.280
- But I think you should try.
- I will. (Chuckles)

1103
01:05:09.640 --> 01:05:11.800
- I feel better already.
- Ha!

1104
01:05:11.920 --> 01:05:13.600
You are looking a little worse.

1105
01:05:13.720 --> 01:05:17.400
Oh! (Laughs)
Well, that is because I'm hungry.

1106
01:05:17.960 --> 01:05:19.360
How thoughtless of me.

1107
01:05:19.480 --> 01:05:23.160
I should've remembered that
when one is going to lead an entirely new life,

1108
01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:26.400
one requires regular and wholesome meals.

1109
01:05:26.520 --> 01:05:28.440
- Won't you come in?
- Thank you.

1110
01:05:28.560 --> 01:05:31.320
But might I have a button-hole first?

1111
01:05:31.440 --> 01:05:34.600
I never have any appetite
unless I have a button-hole first.

1112
01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:37.040
A Maréchal Niel?

1113
01:05:37.200 --> 01:05:40.160
No! No, I'd sooner have a pink rose.

1114
01:05:40.880 --> 01:05:41.920
Why?

1115
01:05:42.040 --> 01:05:44.720
Because you are like a pink rose,
Cousin Cecily.

1116
01:05:45.560 --> 01:05:48.400
I don't think it can be right for you
to talk to me like that.

1117
01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:49.560
Oh?

1118
01:05:49.680 --> 01:05:52.160
Miss Prism
never says such things to me.

1119
01:05:52.280 --> 01:05:57.160
Then Miss Prism
is a short-sighted old lady.

1120
01:05:57.280 --> 01:06:02.000
You are the prettiest girl I ever saw.

1121
01:06:02.160 --> 01:06:04.640
Miss Prism says
all good looks are a snare.

1122
01:06:04.760 --> 01:06:08.800
They are a snare that every sensible man
would like to get caught in.

1123
01:06:08.920 --> 01:06:09.960
Oh!

1124
01:06:10.080 --> 01:06:13.360
I don't think I would care
to catch a sensible man.

1125
01:06:13.480 --> 01:06:15.960
I shouldn't know
what to talk to him about.

1126
01:06:17.800 --> 01:06:19.160
(Screaming)

1127
01:06:19.280 --> 01:06:20.800
(Audience laughing)

1128
01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:26.840
(Laughing) You are too much alone,
dear Dr Chasuble.

1129
01:06:29.320 --> 01:06:32.000
You... You should get married.

1130
01:06:32.600 --> 01:06:36.800
A misanthrope, I can understand.
A womanthrope, never!

1131
01:06:36.920 --> 01:06:41.280
Believe me, I do not deserve
so neologistic a phrase.

1132
01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:44.920
The precept as well as the practice
of the Primitive Church

1133
01:06:45.040 --> 01:06:48.080
was distinctly against matrimony.

1134
01:06:48.240 --> 01:06:49.280
That is obviously the reason

1135
01:06:49.400 --> 01:06:52.760
why the Primitive Church
has not lasted up to the present day.

1136
01:06:52.880 --> 01:06:56.400
And you do not seem to realise,
dear Doctor,

1137
01:06:56.520 --> 01:06:59.720
that by persistently remaining single,

1138
01:06:59.840 --> 01:07:04.640
a man converts himself
into a permanent public temptation.

1139
01:07:06.080 --> 01:07:07.640
(Audience laughing)

1140
01:07:07.760 --> 01:07:09.640
Men should be more careful.

1141
01:07:09.760 --> 01:07:13.200
This very celibacy
leads weaker vessels astray.

1142
01:07:13.840 --> 01:07:17.280
But is a man not equally attractive
when married?

1143
01:07:17.400 --> 01:07:20.520
- No married man is ever attractive...
- (Audience laughing)

1144
01:07:20.640 --> 01:07:22.520
...except to his wife.

1145
01:07:23.200 --> 01:07:26.040
And often, I've been told,
not even to her.

1146
01:07:26.200 --> 01:07:27.480
(Audience laughing)

1147
01:07:27.600 --> 01:07:32.080
That depends on the intellectual sympathies
of the woman.

1148
01:07:32.240 --> 01:07:36.640
Maturity can always be depended on.

1149
01:07:39.720 --> 01:07:42.400
Ripeness can be trusted.

1150
01:07:42.520 --> 01:07:45.040
- Young women are green.
- Oh!

1151
01:07:45.200 --> 01:07:48.400
Well, I spoke horticulturally.

1152
01:07:48.520 --> 01:07:51.240
My metaphor was drawn from fruits.

1153
01:08:04.640 --> 01:08:06.320
(Audience laughing)

1154
01:08:09.800 --> 01:08:12.880
Oh! Uh... no! Huh!
(Chuckling)

1155
01:08:13.000 --> 01:08:15.040
But where is Cecily?

1156
01:08:15.200 --> 01:08:18.080
Perhaps she followed us to the schools.

1157
01:08:18.240 --> 01:08:20.800
- Mr Worthing!
- Mr Worthing?

1158
01:08:20.920 --> 01:08:23.040
(Audience laughing)

1159
01:08:23.200 --> 01:08:25.320
This is indeed a surprise.

1160
01:08:25.440 --> 01:08:28.560
We did not look for you
till Monday afternoon.

1161
01:08:30.520 --> 01:08:33.160
I've returned sooner than I expected.

1162
01:08:33.280 --> 01:08:34.920
Dr Chasuble, I hope you are well?

1163
01:08:35.040 --> 01:08:41.320
Dear Mr Worthing, I trust this garb of woe
does not betoken some terrible calamity?

1164
01:08:42.720 --> 01:08:44.560
- My brother.
- (Miss Prism gasping)

1165
01:08:45.800 --> 01:08:48.720
More... More shameful debts
and extravagance?

1166
01:08:48.840 --> 01:08:50.880
Still leading his life of pleasure?

1167
01:08:51.480 --> 01:08:53.800
- Dead!
- (Audience laughing)

1168
01:08:53.920 --> 01:08:56.920
- Your brother Ernest dead?
- Quite dead.

1169
01:08:57.040 --> 01:09:00.800
What a lesson for him!
I trust he will profit by it.

1170
01:09:01.360 --> 01:09:06.880
Mr Worthing,
I offer you my sincere condolence.

1171
01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:09.800
You have at least
the consolation of knowing

1172
01:09:09.920 --> 01:09:13.920
you were always the most generous
and forgiving of brothers.

1173
01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:15.480
Poor Ernest!

1174
01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:18.720
He had many faults,
but it is a sad, sad blow.

1175
01:09:18.840 --> 01:09:21.040
Very sad indeed.

1176
01:09:22.480 --> 01:09:24.960
Were you with him at the end?

1177
01:09:26.320 --> 01:09:27.680
No.

1178
01:09:27.800 --> 01:09:29.240
- He died abroad.
- (Miss Prism gasping)

1179
01:09:29.360 --> 01:09:31.040
In Paris, in fact.

1180
01:09:31.200 --> 01:09:35.520
I had a telegram last night
from the manager of the Grand Hotel.

1181
01:09:35.640 --> 01:09:38.240
Was the cause of death mentioned?

1182
01:09:38.360 --> 01:09:40.800
A severe chill, it seems.

1183
01:09:40.920 --> 01:09:44.080
As a man sows, so shall he reap.

1184
01:09:44.240 --> 01:09:47.880
Charity, dear Miss Prism, charity!
None of us are perfect.

1185
01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:51.200
I myself am peculiarly susceptible
to draughts.

1186
01:09:51.320 --> 01:09:53.040
(Audience laughing)

1187
01:09:53.760 --> 01:09:56.960
Will the interment take place here?

1188
01:09:57.080 --> 01:10:01.360
No. He seemed to have expressed
a desire to be buried in Paris.

1189
01:10:01.480 --> 01:10:02.760
In Paris?

1190
01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:06.920
I fear that hardly points
to any very serious state of mind at the last.

1191
01:10:07.760 --> 01:10:10.920
You would no doubt wish me
to make some slight allusion

1192
01:10:11.040 --> 01:10:14.160
to this tragic domestic affliction
next Sunday.

1193
01:10:14.280 --> 01:10:17.760
My sermon on the meaning
of the manna in the wilderness

1194
01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:21.600
can be adapted to almost any occasion.

1195
01:10:21.720 --> 01:10:26.880
Joyful, or, as in the present case,
distressing.

1196
01:10:28.040 --> 01:10:31.800
I've preached it at harvest celebrations,
christenings, confirmations,

1197
01:10:31.920 --> 01:10:35.480
on days of humiliation and festal days.

1198
01:10:35.600 --> 01:10:39.840
The last time I delivered it was
in the Cathedral, as a charity sermon

1199
01:10:39.960 --> 01:10:44.840
on behalf of the Society for the Prevention
of Discontent among the Upper Orders.

1200
01:10:45.480 --> 01:10:48.360
The Bishop, who was present,
was much struck

1201
01:10:48.480 --> 01:10:54.200
by some of the analogies I drew.

1202
01:10:54.760 --> 01:10:58.480
Ah! That reminds me, you mentioned
christenings I think, Dr Chasuble?

1203
01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:01.000
- I suppose you know how to christen all right?
- (Laughing)

1204
01:11:01.160 --> 01:11:03.640
I mean, of course,
you are continually christening, aren't you?

1205
01:11:05.680 --> 01:11:07.600
It is... It is, I'm afraid to say,

1206
01:11:07.720 --> 01:11:10.760
one of the Rector's most constant duties
in this parish.

1207
01:11:10.880 --> 01:11:14.640
I've often spoken
to the poorer classes on this subject.

1208
01:11:14.760 --> 01:11:17.520
But they don't seem to know
what thrift is.

1209
01:11:17.640 --> 01:11:20.360
The Church rejects no babe,
Miss Prism.

1210
01:11:20.480 --> 01:11:24.160
In every child,
there is the making of a saint.

1211
01:11:24.600 --> 01:11:28.280
But is there any particular infant
in whom you are interested, Mr Worthing?

1212
01:11:28.400 --> 01:11:32.560
Your brother, I believe,
was unmarried, was he not?

1213
01:11:32.680 --> 01:11:33.720
Oh, yes.

1214
01:11:33.840 --> 01:11:36.600
People who live entirely for pleasure
usually are.

1215
01:11:37.840 --> 01:11:40.440
But it is not for any child, dear Doctor.

1216
01:11:40.560 --> 01:11:43.320
No! The fact is,
I would like to be christened myself,

1217
01:11:43.440 --> 01:11:45.600
this afternoon,
if you have nothing better to do.

1218
01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:48.920
But surely, Mr Worthing,
you have been christened already?

1219
01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:51.600
I don't remember anything about it.

1220
01:11:51.720 --> 01:11:54.360
Have you any grave doubts
on the subject?

1221
01:11:54.920 --> 01:11:57.000
I certainly intend to have.

1222
01:11:57.920 --> 01:11:59.760
Of course, if the thing
would bother you in any way,

1223
01:11:59.880 --> 01:12:02.280
or if you think I'm a little too old now...

1224
01:12:02.400 --> 01:12:03.520
Not at all.

1225
01:12:03.640 --> 01:12:09.840
The sprinkling, and, indeed, the immersion
of adults is a perfectly canonical practice.

1226
01:12:09.960 --> 01:12:12.960
- Immersion!
- You need have no apprehensions.

1227
01:12:13.080 --> 01:12:17.320
Sprinkling is all that is necessary,
or indeed I think advisable.

1228
01:12:17.440 --> 01:12:19.040
Our weather is so changeable.

1229
01:12:19.920 --> 01:12:23.080
At what hour would you wish
the ceremony performed?

1230
01:12:23.240 --> 01:12:26.800
Oh, I might trot round about five
if that would suit you.

1231
01:12:26.920 --> 01:12:28.680
Perfectly, perfectly!

1232
01:12:28.800 --> 01:12:33.800
In fact, I have two similar ceremonies
to perform at that time.

1233
01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:36.200
A case of twins that occurred recently

1234
01:12:36.320 --> 01:12:39.600
in one of the outlying cottages
on your own estate.

1235
01:12:39.720 --> 01:12:43.680
Poor Jenkins the carter,
a most hard-working man.

1236
01:12:43.800 --> 01:12:46.800
I don't see much fun in being christened
along with other babies.

1237
01:12:46.920 --> 01:12:49.360
It'd be childish.
Would half-past five do?

1238
01:12:49.480 --> 01:12:51.480
Admirably! Admirably!

1239
01:12:51.600 --> 01:12:53.960
And now, dear Mr Worthing,

1240
01:12:54.080 --> 01:12:59.040
I will not intrude any longer
into a house of sorrow.

1241
01:12:59.200 --> 01:13:03.560
I would merely beg you
not to be too much bowed down by grief.

1242
01:13:03.680 --> 01:13:09.600
What seem to us bitter trials
are often blessings in disguise.

1243
01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:13.440
This seems to me a blessing
of an extremely obvious kind.

1244
01:13:13.560 --> 01:13:14.640
(Cecily) Uncle Jack!

1245
01:13:15.280 --> 01:13:19.480
Oh! (Chuckles)
I am pleased to see you back.

1246
01:13:19.600 --> 01:13:22.160
But what horrid clothes you've got on!

1247
01:13:22.280 --> 01:13:24.080
- Do go and change them.
- Cecily!

1248
01:13:24.240 --> 01:13:25.880
My child! My child!

1249
01:13:28.920 --> 01:13:30.840
What is the matter, Uncle Jack?

1250
01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:32.400
Do look happy!

1251
01:13:32.520 --> 01:13:36.720
You look as if you had toothache,
and I've got such a surprise for you.

1252
01:13:36.840 --> 01:13:39.200
Who do you think
is in the dining room?

1253
01:13:40.200 --> 01:13:41.240
Your brother!

1254
01:13:41.360 --> 01:13:44.040
(Audience laughing)

1255
01:13:44.960 --> 01:13:47.080
- Who?
- Your brother Ernest.

1256
01:13:47.240 --> 01:13:49.000
He arrived about half an hour ago.

1257
01:13:49.160 --> 01:13:50.880
What nonsense!
I haven't got a brother.

1258
01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:53.400
Oh, don't say that.

1259
01:13:53.520 --> 01:13:58.080
However badly he may have behaved to you
in the past, he is still your brother.

1260
01:13:58.240 --> 01:14:01.240
You couldn't be so heartless
as to disown him.

1261
01:14:01.360 --> 01:14:02.720
I'll tell him to come out,

1262
01:14:02.840 --> 01:14:05.400
and you will shake hands with him,
won't you, Uncle Jack?

1263
01:14:06.240 --> 01:14:09.160
These are very joyful tidings.

1264
01:14:09.320 --> 01:14:10.800
(Audience laughing)

1265
01:14:10.920 --> 01:14:13.720
After we had all been resigned
to his loss,

1266
01:14:13.840 --> 01:14:17.920
his sudden return
seems to me peculiarly distressing.

1267
01:14:18.040 --> 01:14:19.840
My brother is in the dining room?

1268
01:14:19.960 --> 01:14:22.840
I don't know what this all means.
I think it's perfectly absurd.

1269
01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:25.040
(Gasps) Good heavens!

1270
01:14:28.040 --> 01:14:30.160
(Audience laughing)

1271
01:14:30.960 --> 01:14:33.920
- Good heavens!
- Brother John!

1272
01:14:34.040 --> 01:14:39.800
I have come down from town
to tell you that I am very sorry

1273
01:14:39.920 --> 01:14:42.480
for all the trouble
that I have given you,

1274
01:14:42.600 --> 01:14:47.080
and that I intend to lead
a better life in the future.

1275
01:14:48.200 --> 01:14:49.440
(Audience chuckling)

1276
01:14:49.560 --> 01:14:53.880
Uncle Jack, you are not going to refuse
your own brother's hand?

1277
01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:56.720
Nothing will induce me to take his hand.

1278
01:14:56.840 --> 01:14:59.960
I think his coming down here
is perfectly disgraceful.

1279
01:15:01.960 --> 01:15:03.800
- He... He knows perfectly well why.
- No.

1280
01:15:03.920 --> 01:15:06.280
Uncle Jack, do be nice.

1281
01:15:06.400 --> 01:15:08.080
There is some good in everyone.

1282
01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:12.800
Ernest has just been telling me
about his poor invalid friend Mr Bunbury

1283
01:15:12.920 --> 01:15:14.880
- whom he goes to visit so often.
- (Audience laughing)

1284
01:15:15.560 --> 01:15:19.560
And surely there must be much good
in one who is kind to an invalid,

1285
01:15:19.680 --> 01:15:23.160
and leaves the pleasures of London
to sit by a bed of pain.

1286
01:15:24.920 --> 01:15:27.320
Oh! He's been talking to you
about Bunbury, has he?

1287
01:15:27.440 --> 01:15:31.040
Yes, he's told me all about poor Mr Bunbury,
and his terrible state of health.

1288
01:15:31.200 --> 01:15:33.080
Bunbury!

1289
01:15:33.240 --> 01:15:37.720
I... I won't have him talk to you
about Bunbury or about anything else.

1290
01:15:37.840 --> 01:15:39.920
It is enough to drive one perfectly frantic.

1291
01:15:40.040 --> 01:15:44.280
I must admit
the faults were all on my side.

1292
01:15:44.400 --> 01:15:49.560
But I must say that I think that Brother John's
coldness to me is peculiarly painful.

1293
01:15:49.680 --> 01:15:52.760
I expected a more enthusiastic welcome,

1294
01:15:52.880 --> 01:15:56.600
especially considering
this is my first time that I have come here.

1295
01:15:57.240 --> 01:15:59.360
Uncle Jack!

1296
01:15:59.480 --> 01:16:01.600
- (Grunting)
- Jackie...

1297
01:16:03.080 --> 01:16:06.400
If you do not shake hands with Ernest,
I will never forgive you.

1298
01:16:06.520 --> 01:16:07.960
Never forgive me?

1299
01:16:08.080 --> 01:16:09.720
Never, never, never!

1300
01:16:10.560 --> 01:16:11.760
(Audience laughing)

1301
01:16:12.320 --> 01:16:14.760
Well, this is the last time
I shall ever do it.

1302
01:16:17.000 --> 01:16:19.640
- You young scoundrel, Algy!
- (Chuckles) Argh!

1303
01:16:19.760 --> 01:16:22.720
You have got to get out of this place
as soon as possible.

1304
01:16:22.840 --> 01:16:24.560
I don't allow any Bunburying here.

1305
01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:28.480
It's pleasant, is it not,
to see so perfect a reconciliation?

1306
01:16:28.600 --> 01:16:31.240
You've done a beautiful action
today, dear child.

1307
01:16:31.360 --> 01:16:33.960
Uh, let us not be premature
in our judgements.

1308
01:16:34.080 --> 01:16:36.560
My little task of reconciliation is over.

1309
01:16:36.680 --> 01:16:38.280
I feel very happy.

1310
01:16:40.560 --> 01:16:43.560
Luncheon is served.

1311
01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:46.520
Oh, that is good news!
I am excessively hungry!

1312
01:16:46.640 --> 01:16:48.360
- But you have lunched already.
- Oh!

1313
01:16:48.480 --> 01:16:49.920
Lunched already?

1314
01:16:50.040 --> 01:16:53.080
Well, if he has lunched once,
he can't be expected to lunch twice.

1315
01:16:53.240 --> 01:16:55.440
It would be absurd.

1316
01:16:55.560 --> 01:16:58.440
To partake of two luncheons in one day
would not be liberty.

1317
01:16:58.560 --> 01:16:59.880
It would be licence.

1318
01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:03.960
Even the Pagan philosophers
condemned excess in eating.

1319
01:17:04.080 --> 01:17:06.840
Aristotle speaks of it with severity.

1320
01:17:06.960 --> 01:17:09.720
Doctor,
will you escort the ladies in to lunch?

1321
01:17:09.840 --> 01:17:10.920
With pleasure.

1322
01:17:11.040 --> 01:17:14.800
I have put Mr Ernest's things
in the room next to yours, sir.

1323
01:17:14.920 --> 01:17:17.200
- I suppose that is all right?
- What?

1324
01:17:17.320 --> 01:17:19.720
Mr Ernest's luggage, sir.

1325
01:17:19.840 --> 01:17:22.920
I have unpacked it
and put it in the room next to your own.

1326
01:17:23.040 --> 01:17:24.520
- His luggage?
- Yes, sir.

1327
01:17:24.640 --> 01:17:28.800
Three portmanteaus,
a dressing-case, two hat-boxes,

1328
01:17:28.920 --> 01:17:32.000
and a large luncheon-basket.

1329
01:17:33.720 --> 01:17:37.080
I'm afraid I can't stay more
than a week this time.

1330
01:17:37.240 --> 01:17:39.480
Merriman,
order the dog-cart at once.

1331
01:17:39.600 --> 01:17:41.800
Mr Ernest has suddenly
been called back to town.

1332
01:17:41.920 --> 01:17:43.480
Oh. Yes, sir.

1333
01:17:48.200 --> 01:17:52.840
Oh! What a fearful liar you are, Jack.

1334
01:17:52.960 --> 01:17:55.160
- I've not been called back to town at all.
- Yes, you have.

1335
01:17:55.280 --> 01:17:58.200
- I haven't heard anyone call me.
- Your duty as a gentleman calls you back.

1336
01:17:58.320 --> 01:18:03.040
My duty as a gentleman has never interfered
with my pleasures in the smallest degree.

1337
01:18:03.200 --> 01:18:05.400
- I can quite understand that.
- Well...

1338
01:18:05.520 --> 01:18:07.800
Cecily is a darling.

1339
01:18:07.920 --> 01:18:11.720
No! You are not to talk of Miss Cardew
like that. I don't like it.

1340
01:18:11.840 --> 01:18:14.280
Well, I don't like your clothes.

1341
01:18:14.400 --> 01:18:16.200
You look perfectly ridiculous in them.

1342
01:18:16.320 --> 01:18:19.160
Why on earth don't you go up
and change?

1343
01:18:20.160 --> 01:18:22.440
It's perfectly childish
to be in deep mourning

1344
01:18:22.560 --> 01:18:25.720
for a man who is actually staying
for a whole week in your house as a guest.

1345
01:18:25.840 --> 01:18:28.080
(Audience laughing)

1346
01:18:28.240 --> 01:18:29.720
I call it grotesque.

1347
01:18:29.840 --> 01:18:32.680
You are certainly not staying with me for
a whole week as a guest or as anything else.

1348
01:18:32.800 --> 01:18:34.520
You've got to leave by the four-five train.

1349
01:18:34.640 --> 01:18:36.920
I certainly won't leave you
so long as you are in mourning.

1350
01:18:37.040 --> 01:18:38.600
I should think it most unkind.

1351
01:18:38.720 --> 01:18:41.880
If I were in mourning,
you would stay with me, I suppose.

1352
01:18:42.000 --> 01:18:44.880
I should think it very unfriendly
if you didn't.

1353
01:18:45.000 --> 01:18:46.920
- Well, will you go if I change my clothes?
- Yes.

1354
01:18:47.720 --> 01:18:49.240
So long as you don't take so long.

1355
01:18:49.360 --> 01:18:52.880
I never saw anybody take so long to dress
with such little result.

1356
01:18:53.000 --> 01:18:54.760
(Audience laughing)

1357
01:18:55.240 --> 01:18:59.160
Well, at any rate, that's better than being
always constantly overdressed as you are.

1358
01:18:59.320 --> 01:19:01.320
If I'm occasionally a little over-dressed,

1359
01:19:01.440 --> 01:19:04.560
I make up for it by always being
immensely over-educated.

1360
01:19:04.680 --> 01:19:08.360
Your vanity is ridiculous,
your conduct an outrage,

1361
01:19:08.480 --> 01:19:11.920
and your presence in my garden
utterly absurd.

1362
01:19:15.720 --> 01:19:17.800
- However...
- Uh?

1363
01:19:17.920 --> 01:19:21.080
...you've got to catch the four-five train,

1364
01:19:21.240 --> 01:19:23.880
and I hope you'll have
a pleasant journey back to town.

1365
01:19:24.000 --> 01:19:27.800
This Bunburying, as you call it,

1366
01:19:27.920 --> 01:19:30.920
has not been
a great success for you, has it?

1367
01:19:35.880 --> 01:19:38.680
I think it has been a great success.

1368
01:19:38.800 --> 01:19:43.400
I'm in love with Cecily,
and that is everything. (Laughs)

1369
01:19:43.520 --> 01:19:47.640
Well, that is all very well,
but one can't Bunbury when one is hungry.

1370
01:19:47.760 --> 01:19:50.400
I think I'll join them at lunch.
Bon appétit.

1371
01:19:50.920 --> 01:19:52.680
- (♪ Jaunty tune playing)
- (Algernon laughing)

1372
01:19:52.800 --> 01:19:55.480
- (Applause)
- (♪ Drum roll)

1373
01:19:55.600 --> 01:19:57.640
(♪ Music stops)

1374
01:20:28.520 --> 01:20:31.120
(♪ Drum roll, jaunty tune playing)

1375
01:20:47.480 --> 01:20:49.480
- (♪ Music ends)
- Oh!

1376
01:20:51.280 --> 01:20:54.120
Ah, there she is.

1377
01:20:54.240 --> 01:20:57.000
Oh, I thought you were with Uncle Jack.

1378
01:20:57.120 --> 01:21:00.840
Oh! He has gone to order
the dog-cart for me.

1379
01:21:00.960 --> 01:21:04.000
Oh, is he going to take you
for a nice drive?

1380
01:21:04.880 --> 01:21:07.560
He is going to send me away.

1381
01:21:07.680 --> 01:21:10.400
- Then have we got to part?
- I'm afraid so.

1382
01:21:10.520 --> 01:21:13.360
It is a very painful parting.

1383
01:21:13.480 --> 01:21:16.000
It is always painful to part from people

1384
01:21:16.120 --> 01:21:19.920
whom one has known
for a very brief space of time.

1385
01:21:20.040 --> 01:21:23.640
The absence of old friends
one can endure with equanimity.

1386
01:21:23.800 --> 01:21:28.360
But even a momentary separation from
anyone to whom one has just been introduced

1387
01:21:28.480 --> 01:21:30.120
is almost unbearable.

1388
01:21:30.960 --> 01:21:32.080
Thank you.

1389
01:21:32.200 --> 01:21:34.160
- (Both scream as gong sounds)
- (Algernon) Oh, God!

1390
01:21:34.800 --> 01:21:37.600
The dog-cart is at the door, sir.

1391
01:21:37.720 --> 01:21:40.560
It can wait, Merriman, for five minutes.

1392
01:21:40.680 --> 01:21:42.840
Oh! Yes, Miss.

1393
01:21:42.960 --> 01:21:44.960
(Audience chuckling)

1394
01:21:48.560 --> 01:21:49.800
Cecily...

1395
01:21:51.080 --> 01:21:57.320
I... I hope I shall not offend you
if I state quite frankly and quite openly

1396
01:21:58.040 --> 01:22:01.440
that you seem to me to be in every way

1397
01:22:01.560 --> 01:22:06.880
the visible personification
of absolute perfection.

1398
01:22:07.000 --> 01:22:09.640
I think your frankness
does you great credit, Ernest.

1399
01:22:09.800 --> 01:22:10.920
(Audience laughing)

1400
01:22:11.040 --> 01:22:13.640
If you will allow me,
I will copy your remarks into my diary.

1401
01:22:17.880 --> 01:22:20.520
- Do you really keep a diary?
- Mm!

1402
01:22:20.640 --> 01:22:22.720
I'd give anything to look at it.
May I?

1403
01:22:22.880 --> 01:22:24.080
- Oh, no.
- No...

1404
01:22:24.960 --> 01:22:29.680
You see, it is simply a very young girl's record
of her own thoughts and impressions,

1405
01:22:29.840 --> 01:22:32.280
and consequently meant for publication.

1406
01:22:32.960 --> 01:22:35.960
When it appears in volume form,
I hope you will order a copy.

1407
01:22:36.080 --> 01:22:39.280
- (Algernon chuckles)
- But pray, Ernest, don't stop.

1408
01:22:39.400 --> 01:22:42.360
I delight in taking down from dictation.

1409
01:22:42.480 --> 01:22:47.200
I have reached "absolute perfection".

1410
01:22:47.320 --> 01:22:49.080
(Audience chuckles)

1411
01:22:49.200 --> 01:22:52.720
Oh, you can go on.
I am quite ready for more.

1412
01:22:53.840 --> 01:22:57.240
- (Coughs) Ahem! Sorry!
- Oh, don't cough, Ernest.

1413
01:22:57.360 --> 01:23:01.480
When one is dictating,
one should speak fluently and not cough.

1414
01:23:01.600 --> 01:23:05.040
Besides,
I don't know how to spell a cough.

1415
01:23:07.400 --> 01:23:09.280
Mm...

1416
01:23:10.040 --> 01:23:12.560
Uh, Cecily... Huh!

1417
01:23:13.520 --> 01:23:23.160
Ever since I first looked upon
your wonderful and incomparable beauty,

1418
01:23:23.280 --> 01:23:25.600
I have dared to love you...

1419
01:23:25.720 --> 01:23:28.560
- (Cecily repeating) "...love you..."
- (Audience laughing)

1420
01:23:28.680 --> 01:23:35.400
...uh, wildly, passionately,
devotedly, hopelessly.

1421
01:23:35.520 --> 01:23:37.200
I don't think that you should tell me

1422
01:23:37.320 --> 01:23:41.360
that you love me wildly, passionately,
devotedly, hopelessly.

1423
01:23:41.480 --> 01:23:44.040
Hopelessly doesn't seem
to make much sense, does it?

1424
01:23:44.160 --> 01:23:45.200
Cecily!

1425
01:23:45.320 --> 01:23:47.920
- The dog-cart is waiting, sir.
- Ooh!

1426
01:23:49.160 --> 01:23:52.560
Tell it to come round next week,
at the same hour.

1427
01:23:53.600 --> 01:23:54.640
(Grunts)

1428
01:23:54.800 --> 01:23:56.360
Yes, sir.

1429
01:23:57.160 --> 01:23:59.080
(Audience laughing)

1430
01:23:59.200 --> 01:24:01.120
Uncle Jack would be very much annoyed

1431
01:24:01.240 --> 01:24:03.920
if he knew you were staying on
till next week, at the same hour.

1432
01:24:04.040 --> 01:24:07.560
Oh, I don't care about Uncle Jack.

1433
01:24:07.680 --> 01:24:10.520
I don't care for anybody
in the whole world but you.

1434
01:24:10.640 --> 01:24:12.360
Cecily...

1435
01:24:13.560 --> 01:24:15.280
...I love you.

1436
01:24:17.360 --> 01:24:19.880
You will marry me, won't you?

1437
01:24:20.000 --> 01:24:22.080
You silly boy! Of course.

1438
01:24:22.200 --> 01:24:24.360
- Oh! (Laughing)
- (Audience laughing)

1439
01:24:24.480 --> 01:24:28.840
Why, we have been engaged
for the last three months.

1440
01:24:28.960 --> 01:24:30.480
For the last three months?

1441
01:24:30.600 --> 01:24:33.480
Yes, it will be exactly three months
on Thursday.

1442
01:24:33.600 --> 01:24:35.880
But how did we become engaged?

1443
01:24:36.000 --> 01:24:40.040
Well, ever since dear Uncle Jack
first confessed to us

1444
01:24:40.160 --> 01:24:44.120
that he had a younger brother
who was very wicked and bad,

1445
01:24:44.240 --> 01:24:46.880
you of course have formed
the chief topic of conversation

1446
01:24:47.000 --> 01:24:49.040
between myself and Miss Prism.

1447
01:24:49.160 --> 01:24:54.640
And of course a man who is much talked about
is always very attractive.

1448
01:24:54.800 --> 01:24:58.520
One feels there must be
something in him after all.

1449
01:24:58.640 --> 01:25:04.320
I daresay it was foolish of me,
but I fell in love with you, Ernest. (Chuckles)

1450
01:25:06.040 --> 01:25:07.600
Darling!

1451
01:25:07.720 --> 01:25:11.280
And, uh... when was the engagement
actually settled?

1452
01:25:11.400 --> 01:25:14.000
- On the 14th of February last.
- Ugh!

1453
01:25:14.480 --> 01:25:17.080
Worn out by your entire ignorance
of my existence,

1454
01:25:17.200 --> 01:25:20.560
I determined to end the matter
one way or the other,

1455
01:25:20.680 --> 01:25:22.680
and after a long struggle with myself,

1456
01:25:22.840 --> 01:25:27.080
I accepted you
under this dear old tree here.

1457
01:25:27.200 --> 01:25:29.320
The next day,
I bought this little ring in your name...

1458
01:25:29.440 --> 01:25:30.600
(Gasps)

1459
01:25:30.720 --> 01:25:35.400
...and this is the bangle with the true
lovers' knot I promised you always to wear.

1460
01:25:35.520 --> 01:25:38.320
Did I buy this for you?

1461
01:25:38.440 --> 01:25:40.360
It's very pretty, isn't it?

1462
01:25:40.480 --> 01:25:43.440
Yes, you've
wonderfully good taste, Ernest.

1463
01:25:43.560 --> 01:25:47.520
It's the excuse I've always given
for your leading such a bad life.

1464
01:25:47.640 --> 01:25:52.440
And this is the box
in which I keep all your dear letters.

1465
01:25:53.600 --> 01:25:55.560
(Audience laughing)

1466
01:25:57.920 --> 01:26:00.160
My letters!

1467
01:26:00.280 --> 01:26:05.120
Uh... but my own Cecily,
I have never written you any letters.

1468
01:26:05.240 --> 01:26:08.480
You need hardly remind me of that, Ernest.

1469
01:26:08.600 --> 01:26:13.240
I remember only too well that I was forced
to write your letters for you.

1470
01:26:13.360 --> 01:26:16.720
I wrote always three times a week,
and sometimes oftener.

1471
01:26:16.880 --> 01:26:20.080
Oh... Oh, do let me read them, Cecily!

1472
01:26:20.200 --> 01:26:22.200
- Oh, I couldn't possibly.
- (Algernon grunts)

1473
01:26:22.320 --> 01:26:24.920
They would make you far too conceited.

1474
01:26:25.040 --> 01:26:29.800
The three you wrote me after I had broken off
the engagement are so beautiful,

1475
01:26:29.920 --> 01:26:31.960
and so badly spelled,

1476
01:26:32.080 --> 01:26:35.840
that even now I can hardly read them
without crying a little.

1477
01:26:36.440 --> 01:26:38.720
But was our engagement ever broken off?

1478
01:26:38.880 --> 01:26:40.320
Of course it was.

1479
01:26:40.440 --> 01:26:43.000
On the 22nd of last March.

1480
01:26:43.120 --> 01:26:45.480
- You can see the entry if you like.
- Mm.

1481
01:26:48.160 --> 01:26:51.240
"Today I broke off my engagement
with Ernest.

1482
01:26:51.360 --> 01:26:53.480
"I feel it is better to do so.

1483
01:26:53.600 --> 01:26:55.880
"The weather still continues charming."

1484
01:26:56.440 --> 01:26:59.360
Ah! But why on earth
did you break it off?

1485
01:26:59.480 --> 01:27:02.320
What had I done, Cecily?
I had done nothing at all.

1486
01:27:02.440 --> 01:27:04.960
I am very much hurt indeed
to hear you broke it off.

1487
01:27:05.080 --> 01:27:08.200
Particularly when the weather
was so charming.

1488
01:27:08.320 --> 01:27:09.560
(Audience laughing)

1489
01:27:09.680 --> 01:27:12.560
It would have hardly been
a really serious engagement

1490
01:27:12.680 --> 01:27:15.960
- if it hadn't been broken off at least once.
- Ah!

1491
01:27:16.080 --> 01:27:18.360
But I forgave you
before the week was out.

1492
01:27:18.480 --> 01:27:21.440
What a perfect angel you are, Cecily.

1493
01:27:21.560 --> 01:27:24.080
You dear romantic boy.

1494
01:27:26.240 --> 01:27:28.000
- Oh!
- Oh?

1495
01:27:28.120 --> 01:27:30.320
Oh! (Giggles hysterically)

1496
01:27:30.920 --> 01:27:32.240
- Yes!
- Oh!

1497
01:27:32.360 --> 01:27:33.960
(Grunting)

1498
01:27:35.320 --> 01:27:36.840
Mm! Mm!

1499
01:27:37.720 --> 01:27:39.440
(Audience laughing)

1500
01:27:42.040 --> 01:27:44.560
I hope your hair curls naturally, does it?

1501
01:27:45.800 --> 01:27:48.240
- Yes, darling...
- (Audience laughing)

1502
01:27:48.360 --> 01:27:50.240
...with a little help from others.
(Chuckles)

1503
01:27:50.360 --> 01:27:51.680
I am so glad.

1504
01:27:51.840 --> 01:27:54.840
You will never break off
our engagement again, Cecily?

1505
01:27:54.960 --> 01:27:58.640
I don't think I could break it off
now that I've actually met you.

1506
01:27:58.800 --> 01:28:02.680
Besides, of course,
there is the question of your name.

1507
01:28:02.840 --> 01:28:05.040
Yes, of course.

1508
01:28:05.160 --> 01:28:07.080
You must not laugh at me, darling,

1509
01:28:07.200 --> 01:28:14.040
but it had always been a girlish dream of mine
to love someone whose name was Ernest.

1510
01:28:15.000 --> 01:28:20.400
There is something in that name
that seems to inspire absolute confidence.

1511
01:28:20.520 --> 01:28:24.440
Oh, I pity any poor married woman
whose husband is not called Ernest.

1512
01:28:24.560 --> 01:28:26.080
(Algernon chuckling)

1513
01:28:26.880 --> 01:28:30.400
- (Audience laughing)
- Uh... But, my dear child,

1514
01:28:30.520 --> 01:28:34.320
do you mean to say
that you could not love me if...

1515
01:28:34.440 --> 01:28:35.800
(Laughing)

1516
01:28:35.920 --> 01:28:38.160
...if I had some other name?

1517
01:28:38.280 --> 01:28:40.200
- But what name?
- Oh, any name you like.

1518
01:28:40.320 --> 01:28:42.640
Algernon, for instance.

1519
01:28:42.800 --> 01:28:45.320
But I don't like the name of Algernon.

1520
01:28:45.440 --> 01:28:49.840
Well, my own
sweet, loving little darling,

1521
01:28:49.960 --> 01:28:53.600
I... I really can't see why you should object
to the name of Algernon.

1522
01:28:54.080 --> 01:28:58.600
It's not at all a bad name.
In fact, it is rather an aristocratic name.

1523
01:28:58.720 --> 01:29:02.600
Half of the chaps who get
into the Bankruptcy Court are called Algernon.

1524
01:29:03.680 --> 01:29:05.720
But seriously, Cecily,

1525
01:29:05.880 --> 01:29:10.640
if... (Laughs) if my name was Algy,
couldn't you love me?

1526
01:29:11.960 --> 01:29:14.680
I might respect you, Ernest,

1527
01:29:14.840 --> 01:29:17.080
I might admire your character,

1528
01:29:17.200 --> 01:29:22.640
but I fear that I should not be able
to give you my undivided attention.

1529
01:29:23.600 --> 01:29:29.240
Um... Cecily, your Rector here
is thoroughly experienced, I suppose,

1530
01:29:29.360 --> 01:29:32.440
in the practice of all the rites
and ceremonials of the Church?

1531
01:29:32.560 --> 01:29:35.960
Oh, yes.
Dr Chasuble is a most learned man.

1532
01:29:36.080 --> 01:29:39.200
He has never written a single book,
so you can imagine how much he knows.

1533
01:29:39.840 --> 01:29:42.560
Well, I... I must see him at once
on a most important christening...

1534
01:29:42.680 --> 01:29:44.720
- on most important business.
- Oh!

1535
01:29:44.880 --> 01:29:46.360
I shan't be more than half an hour.

1536
01:29:46.480 --> 01:29:49.880
Considering that we have been engaged
since February the 14th,

1537
01:29:50.000 --> 01:29:52.320
and that I only met you today
for the first time,

1538
01:29:52.440 --> 01:29:56.480
I think it is rather hard that you should
leave me for so long a period as half an hour.

1539
01:29:56.600 --> 01:29:58.280
Couldn't you make it twenty minutes?

1540
01:29:59.040 --> 01:30:02.320
I shall be back in no time.

1541
01:30:03.200 --> 01:30:06.480
(Grunting, laughing)

1542
01:30:09.640 --> 01:30:12.360
What an impetuous boy he is!

1543
01:30:13.280 --> 01:30:15.320
I like his shirt so much.

1544
01:30:16.400 --> 01:30:19.040
I must enter his proposal into my diary.

1545
01:30:22.600 --> 01:30:26.520
A Miss Fairfax has just called
to see Mr Worthing.

1546
01:30:26.640 --> 01:30:29.480
On very important business,
Miss Fairfax states.

1547
01:30:29.600 --> 01:30:31.440
Isn't Mr Worthing in his library?

1548
01:30:31.560 --> 01:30:35.880
Mr Worthing went over in the direction
of the Rectory some time ago.

1549
01:30:36.920 --> 01:30:38.720
Pray ask the lady to come out here.

1550
01:30:38.880 --> 01:30:42.400
Mr Worthing is sure to be back soon,
and you can bring tea.

1551
01:30:42.520 --> 01:30:44.280
Oh, yes, Miss.

1552
01:30:44.400 --> 01:30:46.480
(Audience chuckling)

1553
01:30:46.600 --> 01:30:48.600
Miss Fairfax!

1554
01:30:48.720 --> 01:30:51.040
I suppose one of the many good
elderly women

1555
01:30:51.160 --> 01:30:56.400
who are associated with Uncle Jack
in some of his philanthropic work in London.

1556
01:30:56.520 --> 01:31:00.320
I don't quite like women
who are interested in philanthropic work.

1557
01:31:00.440 --> 01:31:03.320
I think it is so forward of them.

1558
01:31:03.440 --> 01:31:05.200
Miss Fairfax.

1559
01:31:06.800 --> 01:31:09.320
Huh! Oh...

1560
01:31:09.440 --> 01:31:14.720
Pray let me introduce myself to you.

1561
01:31:14.880 --> 01:31:18.920
- My name is Cecily Cardew. (Squeals)
- (Gwendolen grunts)

1562
01:31:19.040 --> 01:31:21.000
Cecily Cardew?

1563
01:31:21.120 --> 01:31:23.120
What a very sweet name!

1564
01:31:23.240 --> 01:31:26.360
Something tells me
we are going to be great friends.

1565
01:31:26.480 --> 01:31:29.560
I like you already more than I can say.

1566
01:31:29.680 --> 01:31:32.040
My first impressions of people
are never wrong.

1567
01:31:32.160 --> 01:31:34.200
How nice of you to like me so much

1568
01:31:34.320 --> 01:31:38.280
after we have known each other
such a comparatively short time.

1569
01:31:38.400 --> 01:31:40.040
Pray sit down.

1570
01:31:40.160 --> 01:31:43.400
- I may call you Cecily, may I not?
- Oh, with pleasure!

1571
01:31:43.520 --> 01:31:45.920
And you'll always
call me Gwendolen, won't you?

1572
01:31:46.040 --> 01:31:49.200
- If you wish.
- Then that's all quite settled, is it not?

1573
01:31:49.320 --> 01:31:51.320
- I hope so.
- (Both chuckling)

1574
01:31:52.560 --> 01:31:54.960
Oh, uh... OK, uh...

1575
01:31:55.800 --> 01:31:57.840
(Squeals) Ooh! (Chuckles)

1576
01:32:03.240 --> 01:32:06.840
Perhaps this might be a favourable opportunity
for my mentioning who I am.

1577
01:32:06.960 --> 01:32:09.480
My father is Lord Bracknell. (Chuckles)

1578
01:32:10.800 --> 01:32:12.720
You have never heard of papa,
I suppose?

1579
01:32:12.880 --> 01:32:14.440
Oh, I don't think so.

1580
01:32:14.560 --> 01:32:19.200
Outside the family circle, papa,
I am glad to say, is entirely unknown.

1581
01:32:19.320 --> 01:32:21.600
I think that is quite as it should be.

1582
01:32:21.720 --> 01:32:25.560
The home seems to me
to be the proper sphere for the man.

1583
01:32:25.680 --> 01:32:29.880
And certainly once a man begins
to neglect his domestic duties,

1584
01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:34.440
he becomes painfully effeminate,
does he not?

1585
01:32:34.560 --> 01:32:35.840
And I don't like that.

1586
01:32:35.960 --> 01:32:37.680
It makes men so very attractive.

1587
01:32:37.840 --> 01:32:39.400
(Both laughing)

1588
01:32:41.240 --> 01:32:45.720
Uh, Cecily, mamma, whose views
on education are remarkably strict,

1589
01:32:45.880 --> 01:32:48.280
has brought me up
to be extremely short-sighted -

1590
01:32:48.400 --> 01:32:50.400
it is part of her system -

1591
01:32:50.520 --> 01:32:53.080
so do you mind my looking at you
through my glasses?

1592
01:32:53.200 --> 01:32:55.640
Oh! Not at all, Gwendolen.

1593
01:32:55.800 --> 01:32:58.720
I am very fond of being looked at.

1594
01:32:58.880 --> 01:33:00.480
Oh!

1595
01:33:00.600 --> 01:33:03.040
Uh... So, uh...

1596
01:33:04.240 --> 01:33:07.280
You are here on a short visit,
I suppose?

1597
01:33:07.400 --> 01:33:10.640
- Oh, no! I live here.
- Really?

1598
01:33:10.800 --> 01:33:16.280
Your mother, no doubt,
or some female relative of advanced years,

1599
01:33:16.400 --> 01:33:17.800
resides here also?

1600
01:33:17.920 --> 01:33:22.040
Oh, no! I have no mother,
nor, in fact, any relations.

1601
01:33:22.160 --> 01:33:24.160
- Indeed?
- My dear guardian,

1602
01:33:24.280 --> 01:33:26.000
with the assistance of Miss Prism,

1603
01:33:26.120 --> 01:33:28.400
has the arduous task
of looking after me.

1604
01:33:28.520 --> 01:33:30.240
- Your guardian?
- Yes.

1605
01:33:30.360 --> 01:33:32.160
I am Mr Worthing's ward.

1606
01:33:32.280 --> 01:33:33.560
Oh!

1607
01:33:33.680 --> 01:33:37.120
It is strange he never mentioned
to me that he had a ward.

1608
01:33:37.240 --> 01:33:39.120
How secretive of him!

1609
01:33:39.240 --> 01:33:41.960
He grows more interesting hourly.

1610
01:33:42.080 --> 01:33:47.400
I am not sure, however, that the news
inspires me with feelings of unmixed delight.

1611
01:33:47.520 --> 01:33:50.200
I am very fond of you, Cecily.

1612
01:33:50.320 --> 01:33:52.640
I have liked you ever since I met you!

1613
01:33:52.800 --> 01:33:57.000
But I am bound to state that now that I know
that you are Mr Worthing's ward,

1614
01:33:57.120 --> 01:34:03.840
I cannot help expressing a wish you were,
well, just a little older than you seem to be

1615
01:34:03.960 --> 01:34:06.680
and not quite so very alluring
in appearance.

1616
01:34:06.840 --> 01:34:08.920
In fact, if I may speak candidly...

1617
01:34:09.040 --> 01:34:10.200
Pray do!

1618
01:34:10.320 --> 01:34:12.920
I think that whenever
one has anything unpleasant to say,

1619
01:34:13.040 --> 01:34:16.360
one should always be quite candid.

1620
01:34:16.480 --> 01:34:20.400
Well, to speak
with perfect candour, Cecily,

1621
01:34:20.520 --> 01:34:23.960
I wish that you were fully forty-two,
and more than usually plain for your age.

1622
01:34:24.080 --> 01:34:25.120
(Audience laughing)

1623
01:34:25.240 --> 01:34:27.880
Ernest has a strong upright nature.

1624
01:34:28.000 --> 01:34:30.320
He is the very soul of truth and honour.

1625
01:34:30.440 --> 01:34:33.920
Disloyalty would be as impossible
to him as deception.

1626
01:34:34.040 --> 01:34:37.120
But even men
of the noblest possible moral character

1627
01:34:37.240 --> 01:34:41.240
are extremely susceptible to the influence
of the physical charms of others.

1628
01:34:41.360 --> 01:34:43.960
- I...
- Modern, no less than Ancient History,

1629
01:34:44.080 --> 01:34:47.280
supplies us with many
most painful examples of what I refer to.

1630
01:34:47.400 --> 01:34:51.640
If it were not so, indeed,
History would be quite unreadable.

1631
01:34:51.800 --> 01:34:55.120
- I beg your pardon, Gwendolen.
- Mm?

1632
01:34:55.240 --> 01:34:57.920
- Did you say Ernest?
- Yes.

1633
01:34:58.040 --> 01:35:01.680
But it is not Mr Ernest Worthing
who is my guardian.

1634
01:35:01.840 --> 01:35:04.280
It is his brother, his elder brother.

1635
01:35:04.400 --> 01:35:07.200
Ernest never mentioned to me
that he had a brother.

1636
01:35:07.320 --> 01:35:11.880
I am sorry to say they have not been
on good terms for a long time.

1637
01:35:12.000 --> 01:35:14.480
Ah! That accounts for it.

1638
01:35:14.600 --> 01:35:18.840
And now that I think of it, I have never heard
any man mention his brother.

1639
01:35:18.960 --> 01:35:22.200
The subject seems distasteful to most men.

1640
01:35:22.320 --> 01:35:26.440
Cecily, you have lifted
a load from my mind.

1641
01:35:26.560 --> 01:35:28.960
I was growing almost anxious.

1642
01:35:29.080 --> 01:35:30.840
It would have been terrible

1643
01:35:30.960 --> 01:35:33.920
if any cloud had come across
a friendship like ours,

1644
01:35:34.040 --> 01:35:35.520
would it not?

1645
01:35:35.640 --> 01:35:37.560
Of course you are quite, quite sure

1646
01:35:37.680 --> 01:35:40.320
that it is not Mr Ernest Worthing
who is your guardian?

1647
01:35:41.040 --> 01:35:43.040
- Quite sure.
- (Sighs with relief)

1648
01:35:43.160 --> 01:35:46.840
In fact, I am going to be his.

1649
01:35:46.960 --> 01:35:48.720
I beg your pardon?

1650
01:35:48.880 --> 01:35:53.480
Dearest Gwendolen, there is no reason
why I should make a secret of it to you.

1651
01:35:53.600 --> 01:35:58.040
Our little county newspaper
is sure to chronicle the fact next week.

1652
01:35:58.160 --> 01:36:01.840
Mr Ernest Worthing and I
are engaged to be married.

1653
01:36:01.960 --> 01:36:06.280
(Shakily) My darling Cecily,

1654
01:36:06.400 --> 01:36:09.040
I think there must be some slight error.

1655
01:36:09.160 --> 01:36:11.600
Mr Ernest Worthing is engaged to me.

1656
01:36:11.720 --> 01:36:15.480
The announcement will appear
in the Morning Post on Saturday at the latest.

1657
01:36:16.520 --> 01:36:20.040
I am afraid you must be
under some misconception.

1658
01:36:20.160 --> 01:36:24.280
Ernest proposed to me
exactly ten minutes ago.

1659
01:36:26.120 --> 01:36:29.600
It is certainly very curious,

1660
01:36:29.720 --> 01:36:33.680
for he asked me to be his wife
yesterday afternoon at 5:30.

1661
01:36:33.840 --> 01:36:36.840
If you would care
to verify the incident, pray do so.

1662
01:36:36.960 --> 01:36:39.800
(Audience laughing)

1663
01:36:39.920 --> 01:36:42.120
I never travel anywhere without my diary.

1664
01:36:42.240 --> 01:36:45.320
One should always have
something sensational to read in the train.

1665
01:36:45.440 --> 01:36:46.680
(Audience laughing)

1666
01:36:47.160 --> 01:36:51.640
I am sorry, dear Cecily,
if it is any disappointment to you,

1667
01:36:51.800 --> 01:36:55.240
but I'm afraid that I have the prior claim.
(Chuckles)

1668
01:36:55.360 --> 01:36:58.920
It would distress me more than I can tell you,
dear Gwendolen,

1669
01:36:59.040 --> 01:37:03.280
if it caused you
any mental or physical anguish,

1670
01:37:03.400 --> 01:37:08.040
but I feel bound to point out
that since Ernest proposed to you,

1671
01:37:08.160 --> 01:37:10.840
he clearly has changed his mind.

1672
01:37:10.960 --> 01:37:14.880
If the poor fellow has been entrapped
into any foolish promise,

1673
01:37:15.000 --> 01:37:21.360
I shall consider it my duty to rescue him
at once, and with a firm hand.

1674
01:37:21.480 --> 01:37:26.080
Whatever unfortunate entanglement
my dear boy may have got into,

1675
01:37:26.200 --> 01:37:29.240
I will never reproach him
with it after we are married.

1676
01:37:29.360 --> 01:37:32.960
Do you allude to me, Miss Cardew,
as an entanglement?

1677
01:37:33.080 --> 01:37:36.080
Huh! You are presumptuous.

1678
01:37:36.200 --> 01:37:40.320
On an occasion of this kind, it becomes
more than a moral duty to speak one's mind.

1679
01:37:40.440 --> 01:37:42.840
It becomes a pleasure.

1680
01:37:43.800 --> 01:37:48.840
Do you suggest, Miss Fairfax,
that I entrapped Ernest into an engagement?

1681
01:37:48.960 --> 01:37:50.680
- Mm-hmm!
- How dare you?

1682
01:37:50.840 --> 01:37:53.960
This is no time for wearing
the shallow mask of manners.

1683
01:37:54.080 --> 01:37:57.120
When I see a spade, I call it a spade.

1684
01:37:57.240 --> 01:38:01.400
Huh! I am glad to say
that I have never seen a spade.

1685
01:38:02.240 --> 01:38:05.480
It is obvious our social spheres
have been widely different.

1686
01:38:05.600 --> 01:38:09.080
Shall I lay tea here as usual, Miss?

1687
01:38:09.200 --> 01:38:11.640
- Yes, as usual.
- Huh. Yes.

1688
01:38:14.200 --> 01:38:15.520
Mm.

1689
01:38:16.440 --> 01:38:18.080
(Clears throat)

1690
01:38:18.200 --> 01:38:21.800
Are there many interesting walks
in the vicinity, Miss Cardew?

1691
01:38:21.920 --> 01:38:24.400
Oh! Yes! A great many.

1692
01:38:24.520 --> 01:38:29.040
From the top of one of the hills quite close,
one can see five counties.

1693
01:38:29.160 --> 01:38:30.480
Five counties!

1694
01:38:30.600 --> 01:38:34.200
I don't think I should like that.
I hate crowds.

1695
01:38:34.320 --> 01:38:36.240
I suppose that is why you live in town?

1696
01:38:37.920 --> 01:38:40.520
Quite a well-kept garden this is,
Miss Cardew.

1697
01:38:40.640 --> 01:38:42.600
So glad you like it, Miss Fairfax.

1698
01:38:42.720 --> 01:38:45.680
I had no idea
there were any flowers in the country.

1699
01:38:45.840 --> 01:38:51.280
Oh, flowers are as common here,
Miss Fairfax, as people are in London.

1700
01:38:51.400 --> 01:38:55.600
Personally, I cannot understand
how anybody manages to exist in the country,

1701
01:38:55.720 --> 01:38:57.960
if anybody who is anybody does.

1702
01:38:58.080 --> 01:39:00.400
The country always bores me to death.

1703
01:39:01.440 --> 01:39:02.880
(Chuckles)

1704
01:39:03.000 --> 01:39:04.200
Ah!

1705
01:39:04.320 --> 01:39:07.280
- May I offer you some tea, Miss Fairfax?
- Thank you.

1706
01:39:07.400 --> 01:39:09.600
(Roars) Detestable girl!

1707
01:39:10.320 --> 01:39:13.600
But I require tea!

1708
01:39:16.720 --> 01:39:18.440
- Sugar?
- No, thank you.

1709
01:39:18.560 --> 01:39:21.040
Sugar is not fashionable anymore.

1710
01:39:27.080 --> 01:39:28.560
(Audience chuckling)

1711
01:39:33.720 --> 01:39:35.120
(Audience laughing)

1712
01:39:35.880 --> 01:39:38.880
- Cake or bread and butter?
- Bread and butter, please.

1713
01:39:39.000 --> 01:39:41.840
Cake is rarely seen
in the best houses nowadays.

1714
01:39:57.880 --> 01:40:00.600
Hand that to Miss Fairfax.

1715
01:40:01.560 --> 01:40:03.160
(Audience chuckling)

1716
01:40:05.800 --> 01:40:07.840
(Audience laughing)

1717
01:40:09.360 --> 01:40:10.800
(Shrieks)

1718
01:40:17.000 --> 01:40:19.080
(Both chuckling)

1719
01:40:23.160 --> 01:40:25.000
(Both chuckling)

1720
01:40:31.200 --> 01:40:33.080
Mm! (Gargling)

1721
01:40:33.200 --> 01:40:34.280
(Audience laughing)

1722
01:40:38.480 --> 01:40:39.720
(Shrieks)

1723
01:40:41.280 --> 01:40:43.520
You have filled my tea
with lumps of sugar,

1724
01:40:43.640 --> 01:40:45.600
and though I asked most distinctly
for bread and butter,

1725
01:40:45.720 --> 01:40:47.400
you have given me cake.

1726
01:40:49.200 --> 01:40:52.280
I am known for the gentleness
of my disposition

1727
01:40:52.400 --> 01:40:55.040
and the extraordinary sweetness
of my nature,

1728
01:40:55.160 --> 01:40:59.240
but I warn you, Miss Cardew,
you may go too far.

1729
01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:03.240
To save my poor, innocent,
trusting boy

1730
01:41:03.360 --> 01:41:05.200
from the machinations
of any other girl,

1731
01:41:05.320 --> 01:41:07.360
there are no lengths
to which I would not go.

1732
01:41:07.480 --> 01:41:10.200
From the moment I saw you,
I distrusted you.

1733
01:41:10.320 --> 01:41:12.120
I felt that you were false and deceitful.

1734
01:41:12.240 --> 01:41:14.000
I am never deceived in such matters.

1735
01:41:14.120 --> 01:41:16.680
My first impressions of people
are invariably right.

1736
01:41:16.840 --> 01:41:21.600
It seems to me, Miss Fairfax,
that I am trespassing on your valuable time.

1737
01:41:21.720 --> 01:41:25.040
No doubt you have many other calls
of a similar character to make

1738
01:41:25.160 --> 01:41:26.880
- in the neighbourhood.
- Oh, you...

1739
01:41:27.000 --> 01:41:28.240
Cake!

1740
01:41:28.360 --> 01:41:29.560
(Grunts)

1741
01:41:29.680 --> 01:41:31.560
(Squeals)

1742
01:41:31.680 --> 01:41:33.320
(Gwendolen gasps)

1743
01:41:40.480 --> 01:41:41.920
(Audience laughing)

1744
01:41:42.040 --> 01:41:44.160
(Shrieking, shouting)

1745
01:41:44.280 --> 01:41:45.880
There goes Ernest!

1746
01:41:46.000 --> 01:41:47.360
Gwendolen!

1747
01:41:47.480 --> 01:41:50.280
- Darling! Are you...?
- A moment!

1748
01:41:54.720 --> 01:41:58.720
May I ask if you're engaged
to be married to this young lady?

1749
01:41:58.880 --> 01:42:00.960
- To dear little Cecily?
- Mm-hmm.

1750
01:42:01.080 --> 01:42:02.400
Of course not!

1751
01:42:02.520 --> 01:42:05.080
What could've put such an idea
into your pretty little head?

1752
01:42:05.200 --> 01:42:07.920
Thank you. You may!

1753
01:42:08.040 --> 01:42:10.880
I knew there must be
some misunderstanding.

1754
01:42:11.000 --> 01:42:13.560
The gentleman whose arm
is at present round your waist

1755
01:42:13.680 --> 01:42:16.800
is my dear guardian, Mr John Worthing.

1756
01:42:16.920 --> 01:42:18.920
I beg your pardon?

1757
01:42:19.040 --> 01:42:20.960
This is Uncle Jack.

1758
01:42:21.080 --> 01:42:25.200
Jack! Oh... no!

1759
01:42:25.320 --> 01:42:30.200
- Here is Ernest.
- ♪ Meet me at the altar in your white dress ♪

1760
01:42:30.320 --> 01:42:32.680
- My own love!
- A moment, Ernest!

1761
01:42:32.840 --> 01:42:34.480
May I ask you,

1762
01:42:34.600 --> 01:42:37.880
are you engaged to be married
to this young lady?

1763
01:42:38.000 --> 01:42:40.240
To what young lady?

1764
01:42:40.360 --> 01:42:42.800
- Good heavens! Gwendolen!
- Yes! To good heavens, Gwendolen.

1765
01:42:42.960 --> 01:42:45.800
- I mean to Gwendolen.
- Of course not!

1766
01:42:45.920 --> 01:42:49.560
What could've put such an idea
into your pretty little head?

1767
01:42:49.680 --> 01:42:52.240
- Thank you. You may.
- (Chuckles)

1768
01:42:52.360 --> 01:42:55.200
I felt there was some slight error,
Miss Cardew.

1769
01:42:55.320 --> 01:42:59.280
The gentleman who is now embracing you
is my cousin, Mr Algernon Moncrieff.

1770
01:42:59.400 --> 01:43:01.120
Algernon!

1771
01:43:04.160 --> 01:43:06.120
Are you really called Algernon?

1772
01:43:07.560 --> 01:43:09.240
- I cannot deny it.
- Oh!

1773
01:43:09.360 --> 01:43:11.240
Is your name really John?

1774
01:43:11.360 --> 01:43:12.880
I could deny it if I liked.

1775
01:43:13.000 --> 01:43:14.600
(Audience laughing)

1776
01:43:14.720 --> 01:43:16.960
I could deny anything if I liked.

1777
01:43:17.080 --> 01:43:19.360
But my name is certainly John.

1778
01:43:20.120 --> 01:43:21.600
It has been John for years.

1779
01:43:22.920 --> 01:43:26.040
A gross deception
has been practised on both of us.

1780
01:43:26.160 --> 01:43:29.160
My poor wounded Cecily!

1781
01:43:29.280 --> 01:43:30.520
(Audience laughing)

1782
01:43:30.640 --> 01:43:33.120
My sweet wronged Gwendolen!

1783
01:43:33.240 --> 01:43:36.520
You will always call me sister,
will you not?

1784
01:43:36.640 --> 01:43:38.600
(Audience laughing)

1785
01:43:39.560 --> 01:43:43.600
There is just one question
I would like to be allowed to ask my guardian.

1786
01:43:43.720 --> 01:43:45.280
An admirable idea!

1787
01:43:45.400 --> 01:43:49.680
Mr Worthing, there is just one question
I would like to be permitted to put to you.

1788
01:43:49.840 --> 01:43:51.520
Where is your brother Ernest?

1789
01:43:51.640 --> 01:43:55.160
We are both engaged to be married
to your brother Ernest,

1790
01:43:55.280 --> 01:43:57.040
so it is a matter of some importance to us

1791
01:43:57.160 --> 01:43:59.520
to know where your brother Ernest
is at present.

1792
01:44:04.320 --> 01:44:06.320
Gwendolen...

1793
01:44:06.440 --> 01:44:08.280
Cecily...

1794
01:44:14.720 --> 01:44:18.320
It's very painful for me
to be forced to speak the truth.

1795
01:44:18.440 --> 01:44:19.880
It's the first time in my life

1796
01:44:20.000 --> 01:44:23.560
that I've ever been reduced
to such a painful position, and...

1797
01:44:24.520 --> 01:44:27.320
...I'm really quite inexperienced
in doing anything of the kind.

1798
01:44:27.440 --> 01:44:30.800
However,
I will tell you quite frankly...

1799
01:44:33.360 --> 01:44:35.800
...that I have no brother Ernest.

1800
01:44:36.640 --> 01:44:38.720
I have no brother at all.

1801
01:44:38.880 --> 01:44:41.440
I have never had a brother in my life,

1802
01:44:41.560 --> 01:44:45.360
and I certainly have not the smallest intention
of ever having a brother in the future.

1803
01:44:47.280 --> 01:44:49.280
- No brother at all?
- (Jack) None!

1804
01:44:49.400 --> 01:44:52.000
- Had you never a brother of any kind?
- Never.

1805
01:44:52.120 --> 01:44:54.640
- Not even of any kind.
- (Audience laughing)

1806
01:44:57.800 --> 01:45:00.080
I'm afraid it is quite clear, Cecily,

1807
01:45:00.200 --> 01:45:03.320
that neither of us are engaged
to be married to anyone.

1808
01:45:03.440 --> 01:45:08.680
It is not a very pleasant position
for a young girl to find herself in. Is it?

1809
01:45:08.840 --> 01:45:10.240
Let us go inside the house.

1810
01:45:10.360 --> 01:45:12.280
They will hardly venture
to come after us there.

1811
01:45:12.400 --> 01:45:15.080
No, men are so cowardly, aren't they?

1812
01:45:15.200 --> 01:45:16.480
Mm...

1813
01:45:17.000 --> 01:45:19.400
- Cecily...
- Curtain!

1814
01:45:19.520 --> 01:45:21.880
- (♪ Jaunty tune playing)
- Oh! (Shrieks)

1815
01:45:27.400 --> 01:45:29.960
(♪ Drum roll, music stops)

1816
01:45:30.080 --> 01:45:31.960
- (Clears throat)
- (Clank)

1817
01:45:36.480 --> 01:45:38.560
(Audience laughing)

1818
01:45:39.880 --> 01:45:44.440
This ghastly state of affairs
is what you call Bunburying, I suppose?

1819
01:45:44.560 --> 01:45:48.320
Yes, and a perfectly wonderful
Bunbury it is.

1820
01:45:48.440 --> 01:45:51.320
The most wonderful Bunbury
I have ever had in my life.

1821
01:45:51.440 --> 01:45:54.240
Well, you've no right whatsoever
to Bunbury here.

1822
01:45:54.360 --> 01:45:55.640
That is absurd.

1823
01:45:55.800 --> 01:45:58.680
One has a right to Bunbury
anywhere one chooses.

1824
01:45:58.840 --> 01:46:01.080
Every serious Bunburyist knows that.

1825
01:46:01.200 --> 01:46:04.040
Serious Bunburyist!
Good heavens!

1826
01:46:04.160 --> 01:46:05.880
(Audience laughing)

1827
01:46:06.000 --> 01:46:10.280
Well, one must be serious about something,
if one wants to have any amusement in life.

1828
01:46:10.400 --> 01:46:12.920
I happen to be serious about Bunburying.

1829
01:46:13.040 --> 01:46:15.800
What on earth you are serious about,
I haven't the remotest idea.

1830
01:46:15.920 --> 01:46:17.680
About everything, I should fancy.

1831
01:46:17.840 --> 01:46:20.520
You have such an absolutely trivial nature.

1832
01:46:24.000 --> 01:46:25.840
Well...

1833
01:46:25.960 --> 01:46:29.240
The only small satisfaction I have
in the whole of this wretched business

1834
01:46:29.360 --> 01:46:32.160
- is that your friend Bunbury is quite exploded.
- Oh!

1835
01:46:32.280 --> 01:46:35.520
You won't be able to run down to the country
quite so often as you used to do, dear Algy.

1836
01:46:35.640 --> 01:46:36.680
And a very good thing, too.

1837
01:46:36.840 --> 01:46:39.600
Your brother's a little off colour,
isn't he, dear Jack?

1838
01:46:39.720 --> 01:46:41.280
You won't be able to disappear to London

1839
01:46:41.400 --> 01:46:43.880
quite so frequently
as your wicked custom was.

1840
01:46:44.000 --> 01:46:45.120
Not a bad thing either.

1841
01:46:45.240 --> 01:46:47.880
- As for your conduct towards Miss Cardew...
- (Guffaws)

1842
01:46:48.000 --> 01:46:51.920
...I must say that your taking in
a sweet, simple, innocent girl like that

1843
01:46:52.040 --> 01:46:53.640
- is quite inexcusable.
- Mm.

1844
01:46:53.800 --> 01:46:55.440
To say nothing of the fact
that she is my ward.

1845
01:46:55.560 --> 01:46:58.480
I can see no possible defence at all

1846
01:46:58.600 --> 01:47:04.440
for your deceiving a brilliant, clever, thoroughly
experienced young lady like Miss Fairfax.

1847
01:47:04.560 --> 01:47:06.320
To say nothing of the fact
she is my cousin.

1848
01:47:06.440 --> 01:47:09.560
I wanted to be engaged to Gwendolen,
that is all. I love her.

1849
01:47:09.680 --> 01:47:12.720
Well, I simply wanted to be engaged
to Cecily, that is all.

1850
01:47:12.880 --> 01:47:14.120
I adore her.

1851
01:47:14.240 --> 01:47:17.160
There is certainly no chance
of your marrying Miss Cardew.

1852
01:47:17.280 --> 01:47:22.200
I don't think there's much likelihood, Jack,
of you and Miss Fairfax ever being united.

1853
01:47:22.320 --> 01:47:25.320
- Well, that's no business of yours.
- If it was my business, I wouldn't talk about it.

1854
01:47:26.040 --> 01:47:28.960
It's very vulgar
to talk about one's business.

1855
01:47:30.080 --> 01:47:33.720
Only people like stockbrokers do that,
and then merely at dinner parties.

1856
01:47:38.480 --> 01:47:40.680
How you can sit there,
calmly eating muffins

1857
01:47:40.840 --> 01:47:43.600
when we're in this horrible trouble,
I can't make out.

1858
01:47:45.160 --> 01:47:46.680
You seem to me to be perfectly heartless.

1859
01:47:46.840 --> 01:47:49.080
Well, I can't eat muffins
in an agitated manner.

1860
01:47:49.200 --> 01:47:51.720
The butter would probably get on my cuffs.

1861
01:47:51.880 --> 01:47:55.600
One should always eat muffins quite calmly.
It is the only way to eat them.

1862
01:47:55.720 --> 01:47:59.440
I say it's perfectly heartless you're eating
muffins at all, under the circumstances.

1863
01:48:07.880 --> 01:48:09.800
(Audience laughing)

1864
01:48:12.080 --> 01:48:16.200
When I am in trouble,
eating is the only thing that consoles me.

1865
01:48:16.320 --> 01:48:18.160
Indeed,
when I'm in really great trouble,

1866
01:48:18.280 --> 01:48:21.360
as anyone who knows me intimately
will tell you,

1867
01:48:21.480 --> 01:48:24.040
I refuse everything except food and drink.

1868
01:48:24.160 --> 01:48:27.880
At the present moment, I am eating muffins
because I am unhappy.

1869
01:48:28.000 --> 01:48:30.280
Besides,
I'm particularly fond of muffins.

1870
01:48:31.000 --> 01:48:34.480
Well, that's no reason why you should
eat them all in that greedy way.

1871
01:48:34.600 --> 01:48:36.040
Jack!

1872
01:48:36.160 --> 01:48:38.280
I wish you would have some tea-cake.

1873
01:48:38.400 --> 01:48:41.120
- I don't like tea-cake.
- Good heavens!

1874
01:48:43.240 --> 01:48:46.560
I suppose a man may eat his own muffins
in his own garden.

1875
01:48:46.680 --> 01:48:49.400
You just said it was perfectly heartless
to eat muffins.

1876
01:48:49.520 --> 01:48:51.720
I said it was perfectly heartless of you,
under the circumstances.

1877
01:48:51.880 --> 01:48:52.920
That's a very different thing.

1878
01:48:53.040 --> 01:48:55.080
That may be,
but the muffins are the same.

1879
01:48:55.200 --> 01:48:57.120
Algy, I wish to goodness you'd go.

1880
01:48:57.240 --> 01:49:01.120
You can't possibly ask me to go
without having some dinner. It's absurd.

1881
01:49:01.240 --> 01:49:02.520
(Audience laughing)

1882
01:49:02.640 --> 01:49:05.840
I never go without having my dinner.
Nobody ever does.

1883
01:49:09.280 --> 01:49:11.840
Except vegetarians and people like that.

1884
01:49:11.960 --> 01:49:14.000
(Audience laughing)

1885
01:49:14.120 --> 01:49:16.280
I have just made arrangements
with Dr Chasuble

1886
01:49:16.400 --> 01:49:19.640
to be christened at a quarter to six
under the name of Ernest.

1887
01:49:19.800 --> 01:49:23.040
My dear fellow, the sooner you give up
that nonsense the better.

1888
01:49:23.160 --> 01:49:28.160
I made arrangements this morning with
Dr Chasuble to be christened myself at 5:30,

1889
01:49:28.280 --> 01:49:30.920
and I naturally will take the name of Ernest.
Gwendolen would wish it.

1890
01:49:31.040 --> 01:49:34.040
We can't both be christened Ernest.
It's absurd.

1891
01:49:34.160 --> 01:49:36.120
Besides, I have a perfect right
to be christened if I like.

1892
01:49:36.240 --> 01:49:39.280
There is no evidence at all
that I've ever been christened by anybody.

1893
01:49:39.400 --> 01:49:42.440
I should think it extremely probable
I never was, so does Dr Chasuble.

1894
01:49:42.560 --> 01:49:44.560
It's entirely different in your case.
You have been christened.

1895
01:49:44.680 --> 01:49:47.520
Yes, but I haven't been christened
for years.

1896
01:49:47.640 --> 01:49:50.040
Yes, but you have been christened.
That's the important thing.

1897
01:49:50.160 --> 01:49:51.360
Quite so.

1898
01:49:51.480 --> 01:49:54.560
So I know my constitution can stand it.

1899
01:49:54.680 --> 01:49:57.280
If you are not quite sure
about your ever having been christened,

1900
01:49:57.400 --> 01:50:01.000
I must say I think it rather dangerous
you're venturing on it now.

1901
01:50:01.120 --> 01:50:02.680
It might make you very unwell.

1902
01:50:02.840 --> 01:50:06.120
You can hardly have forgotten
that someone very closely connected with you

1903
01:50:06.240 --> 01:50:08.880
was very nearly carried off this week
in Paris by a severe chill.

1904
01:50:09.000 --> 01:50:10.280
(Audience laughing)

1905
01:50:10.920 --> 01:50:13.800
Yes, but you said yourself
that a severe chill wasn't hereditary.

1906
01:50:13.920 --> 01:50:16.680
It usen't to be, I know -
but I daresay it is now.

1907
01:50:16.840 --> 01:50:19.800
Science is always making
wonderful improvements in things.

1908
01:50:19.920 --> 01:50:21.520
(Laughing)

1909
01:50:21.640 --> 01:50:24.960
Oh, that is nonsense -
you are always talking nonsense.

1910
01:50:25.080 --> 01:50:28.720
It's much better to talk nonsense
than to listen to it, my dear fellow.

1911
01:50:28.880 --> 01:50:30.720
I don't listen to you.
I can't listen to you.

1912
01:50:30.880 --> 01:50:33.960
That is merely false modesty
on your part, Jack.

1913
01:50:34.080 --> 01:50:36.920
You know perfectly well
you could listen to me if you tried.

1914
01:50:37.040 --> 01:50:38.520
You always underrate yourself.

1915
01:50:40.720 --> 01:50:42.120
Jack!

1916
01:50:42.240 --> 01:50:44.360
You are at the muffins again!
I wish you wouldn't.

1917
01:50:44.480 --> 01:50:47.320
There is only one left,
and I told you I'm very fond of muffins.

1918
01:50:47.440 --> 01:50:48.840
I hate tea-cake.

1919
01:50:48.960 --> 01:50:51.200
Why on earth do you allow it
to be served up for your guests?

1920
01:50:51.320 --> 01:50:53.120
What ideas you have of hospitality!

1921
01:50:53.240 --> 01:50:54.680
- Algernon!
- What?

1922
01:50:54.840 --> 01:50:58.280
I have already asked you to go.
I don't want you here.

1923
01:50:58.400 --> 01:51:00.600
We've covered the scene change.
Just go!

1924
01:51:00.720 --> 01:51:02.640
(Audience laughing)

1925
01:51:12.680 --> 01:51:15.440
I haven't quite finished my tea yet!

1926
01:51:15.560 --> 01:51:17.440
And there's still one muffin left.

1927
01:51:18.080 --> 01:51:20.560
(♪ Drum roll,
jaunty tune playing)

1928
01:51:22.960 --> 01:51:24.960
(Applause)

1929
01:51:45.680 --> 01:51:47.680
(♪ Music stops)

1930
01:51:48.800 --> 01:51:51.120
(Audience laughing)

1931
01:51:52.160 --> 01:51:54.280
The fact that they did not
follow us at once into the house,

1932
01:51:54.400 --> 01:51:55.960
as anyone else would have done,

1933
01:51:56.080 --> 01:51:59.480
seems to me to show
that they have some sense of shame left.

1934
01:51:59.600 --> 01:52:01.720
They have been eating muffins.

1935
01:52:01.880 --> 01:52:03.320
That looks like repentance.

1936
01:52:04.080 --> 01:52:07.040
They don't seem to notice us at all.
Uh... couldn't you cough?

1937
01:52:07.160 --> 01:52:08.600
- But I haven't got a cough.
- Ugh!

1938
01:52:09.280 --> 01:52:12.560
(Shrieks) They're looking at us.
What effrontery!

1939
01:52:12.680 --> 01:52:15.640
Ooh! They're approaching.
That's very forward of them.

1940
01:52:15.800 --> 01:52:18.520
- Let us preserve a dignified silence.
- Certainly.

1941
01:52:18.640 --> 01:52:20.640
It's the only thing to do now.

1942
01:52:22.240 --> 01:52:23.960
♪ You're beautiful... ♪

1943
01:52:24.080 --> 01:52:26.160
(Audience laughing)

1944
01:52:26.280 --> 01:52:29.200
- ♪ You're beautiful
- (Audience chuckling)

1945
01:52:30.200 --> 01:52:34.200
♪ You're beautiful, it is true

1946
01:52:35.600 --> 01:52:38.200
♪ I saw your face

1947
01:52:39.240 --> 01:52:42.120
♪ In a crowded room

1948
01:52:42.240 --> 01:52:43.880
♪ I just don't know... ♪

1949
01:52:44.000 --> 01:52:46.640
(Audience laughing)

1950
01:52:47.600 --> 01:52:49.720
(Whispering)

1951
01:52:49.880 --> 01:52:53.920
This dignified silence
seems to produce an unpleasant effect.

1952
01:52:54.040 --> 01:52:55.640
A most distasteful one.

1953
01:52:55.800 --> 01:52:58.800
- But we will not be the first to speak.
- Certainly not.

1954
01:52:58.920 --> 01:53:02.280
Mr Worthing, I have something
very particular to ask you.

1955
01:53:02.400 --> 01:53:04.000
Much depends on your reply.

1956
01:53:04.120 --> 01:53:06.800
Gwendolen,
your common sense is invaluable.

1957
01:53:06.920 --> 01:53:11.440
Mr Moncrieff, kindly answer me
the following question.

1958
01:53:12.120 --> 01:53:15.800
Why did you pretend
to be my guardian's brother?

1959
01:53:15.920 --> 01:53:19.280
In order that I may have
an opportunity of meeting you.

1960
01:53:20.520 --> 01:53:24.000
That seems a satisfactory explanation,
does it not?

1961
01:53:24.120 --> 01:53:27.480
- Yes, dear, if you can believe him.
- I don't.

1962
01:53:27.600 --> 01:53:30.840
But that does not affect
the wonderful beauty of his answer.

1963
01:53:31.360 --> 01:53:36.440
True. In matters of grave importance,
style, not sincerity, is the vital thing.

1964
01:53:37.080 --> 01:53:39.480
Mr Worthing,

1965
01:53:39.600 --> 01:53:43.280
what explanation can you offer to me
for pretending to have a brother?

1966
01:53:45.200 --> 01:53:47.680
Was it in order that you might have
an opportunity of coming up to town

1967
01:53:47.840 --> 01:53:49.520
to see me as often as possible?

1968
01:53:49.640 --> 01:53:51.600
Can you doubt it, Miss Fairfax?

1969
01:53:51.720 --> 01:53:54.080
I have the gravest doubts
upon the subject,

1970
01:53:54.200 --> 01:53:56.920
but I intend to crush them.

1971
01:53:57.360 --> 01:54:00.720
This is not the moment
for German scepticism. Hmm?

1972
01:54:02.520 --> 01:54:04.240
Oh!

1973
01:54:04.360 --> 01:54:06.760
- (Giggling)
- (Audience laughing)

1974
01:54:06.920 --> 01:54:09.800
Their explanations
appear to be quite satisfactory,

1975
01:54:09.920 --> 01:54:11.560
especially Mr Worthing's.

1976
01:54:11.680 --> 01:54:14.160
That seems to me
to have the stamp of truth upon it.

1977
01:54:14.280 --> 01:54:17.280
I am more than content
with what Mr Moncrieff said.

1978
01:54:17.400 --> 01:54:21.280
His voice alone
inspires one with absolute credulity.

1979
01:54:21.400 --> 01:54:23.680
- Then you think we should forgive them?
- Yes.

1980
01:54:23.840 --> 01:54:26.360
- I mean no.
- True! I had forgotten.

1981
01:54:26.480 --> 01:54:29.920
There are principles at stake
that one cannot surrender.

1982
01:54:30.040 --> 01:54:31.520
Which of us should tell them?

1983
01:54:31.640 --> 01:54:33.560
The task is not a pleasant one.

1984
01:54:33.680 --> 01:54:35.880
Could we not both speak
at the same time?

1985
01:54:36.000 --> 01:54:38.080
An excellent idea!

1986
01:54:38.200 --> 01:54:41.600
I nearly always speak at the same time
as other people.

1987
01:54:41.720 --> 01:54:44.640
- Will you take the time from me?
- Certainly.

1988
01:54:50.320 --> 01:54:54.360
Your Christian names are still
an insuperable barrier. That is all!

1989
01:54:54.480 --> 01:54:56.520
Our Christian names!

1990
01:54:57.840 --> 01:54:59.080
Is that all?

1991
01:54:59.200 --> 01:55:01.680
But we are going to be christened
this afternoon.

1992
01:55:01.840 --> 01:55:05.440
For my sake you're prepared
to do this terrible thing?

1993
01:55:05.560 --> 01:55:06.600
I am!

1994
01:55:06.720 --> 01:55:10.680
To please me you are ready
to face this fearful ordeal?

1995
01:55:10.840 --> 01:55:13.120
- I am!
- (Audience laughing)

1996
01:55:13.840 --> 01:55:17.240
How absurd to speak
of the equality of the sexes!

1997
01:55:17.360 --> 01:55:20.000
Where questions of self-sacrifice
are concerned,

1998
01:55:20.120 --> 01:55:22.400
men are infinitely beyond us.

1999
01:55:22.520 --> 01:55:25.360
- We are!
- (Audience laughing)

2000
01:55:25.480 --> 01:55:31.000
They have moments of physical courage
of which we women know absolutely nothing.

2001
01:55:31.120 --> 01:55:32.800
- Darling!
- Darling!

2002
01:55:32.920 --> 01:55:33.960
Darling!

2003
01:55:34.080 --> 01:55:35.960
- Darling!
- Darling!

2004
01:55:36.080 --> 01:55:38.120
(Laughing, whooping)

2005
01:55:38.240 --> 01:55:39.880
- Oh, darling!
- Darling!

2006
01:55:40.000 --> 01:55:42.000
- Darling!
- Darling!

2007
01:55:42.120 --> 01:55:43.320
Oh, darling!

2008
01:55:43.440 --> 01:55:44.480
- Darling!
- Darling!

2009
01:55:44.600 --> 01:55:45.640
- Oh!
- Darling!

2010
01:55:45.800 --> 01:55:47.680
- Darling!
- Darling!

2011
01:55:47.840 --> 01:55:49.480
I am dazed with happiness.

2012
01:55:49.600 --> 01:55:52.360
- I hardly know who I'm kissing.
- Me!

2013
01:55:59.040 --> 01:56:01.640
Hello, handsome.
Thank you so much.

2014
01:56:03.800 --> 01:56:06.640
Lady Bracknell!

2015
01:56:10.520 --> 01:56:11.600
Oh...

2016
01:56:11.720 --> 01:56:14.920
Gwendolen!
What does this mean?

2017
01:56:15.040 --> 01:56:20.840
Merely that I am engaged to be married
to Mr Worthing, mamma. (Squeals)

2018
01:56:21.640 --> 01:56:23.240
Come here.

2019
01:56:24.640 --> 01:56:26.360
Sit down.

2020
01:56:26.480 --> 01:56:28.480
Sit down immediately.

2021
01:56:28.600 --> 01:56:31.960
Hesitation of any kind
is a sign of mental decay in the young,

2022
01:56:32.080 --> 01:56:34.920
of physical weakness in the old.

2023
01:56:35.480 --> 01:56:39.920
Apprised, sir, of my daughter's
sudden flight by her trusty maid,

2024
01:56:40.040 --> 01:56:44.480
whose confidence
I purchased by means of a small coin,

2025
01:56:44.600 --> 01:56:48.920
I followed her at once
by a luggage train.

2026
01:56:49.040 --> 01:56:52.800
Her unhappy father is,
I am glad to say,

2027
01:56:52.920 --> 01:56:57.200
under the impression that she is attending
a more than usually lengthy lecture

2028
01:56:57.320 --> 01:56:59.600
by the University Access Scheme

2029
01:56:59.720 --> 01:57:04.240
on the influence
of a permanent income on thought.

2030
01:57:05.160 --> 01:57:07.600
I do not propose to undeceive him.

2031
01:57:07.720 --> 01:57:12.160
Indeed, I have never undeceived him
on any question.

2032
01:57:12.280 --> 01:57:14.600
I would consider it wrong.

2033
01:57:14.720 --> 01:57:18.200
But of course,
you will clearly understand

2034
01:57:18.320 --> 01:57:21.640
that all communication
between yourself and my daughter

2035
01:57:21.800 --> 01:57:24.400
must cease immediately from this moment.

2036
01:57:24.520 --> 01:57:29.480
On this point, as indeed on all points,
I am firm.

2037
01:57:30.040 --> 01:57:32.840
I'm engaged to be married
to Gwendolen, Lady Bracknell!

2038
01:57:32.960 --> 01:57:35.160
You are nothing of the kind, sir.

2039
01:57:35.280 --> 01:57:36.680
- Ugh!
- (Algernon) Good, good, good...

2040
01:57:36.840 --> 01:57:40.680
And now, as regards Algernon!

2041
01:57:41.600 --> 01:57:45.320
Yes! (Laughs)
Yes, Aunt Augusta.

2042
01:57:45.440 --> 01:57:50.920
May I ask if it is in this house
that your invalid friend Mr Bunbury resides?

2043
01:57:51.560 --> 01:57:55.800
Oh... No!
No, Bunbury doesn't live here.

2044
01:57:55.920 --> 01:57:58.080
Bunbury is somewhere else at present.
In fact...

2045
01:57:59.320 --> 01:58:02.000
- ...Bunbury is dead.
- (Audience laughing)

2046
01:58:02.120 --> 01:58:03.360
Dead!

2047
01:58:03.480 --> 01:58:05.480
When did Mr Bunbury die?

2048
01:58:05.600 --> 01:58:08.840
His death must have been
extremely sudden.

2049
01:58:10.240 --> 01:58:12.440
I killed Bunbury this afternoon.

2050
01:58:12.560 --> 01:58:17.560
- (Audience laughing)
- He died! He... he died this afternoon.

2051
01:58:17.680 --> 01:58:19.000
What did he die of?

2052
01:58:19.120 --> 01:58:20.880
- Bunbury?
- Mm-hmm!

2053
01:58:21.000 --> 01:58:24.600
Oh, he was quite exploded.

2054
01:58:24.720 --> 01:58:26.640
Oh, my God!

2055
01:58:26.800 --> 01:58:28.720
- Exploded!
- Mm!

2056
01:58:28.880 --> 01:58:32.720
Was he the victim
of a revolutionary outrage?

2057
01:58:32.880 --> 01:58:37.280
I was not aware that Mr Bunbury
was interested in social justice.

2058
01:58:37.400 --> 01:58:41.680
If so, he is well punished
for his morbidity.

2059
01:58:42.800 --> 01:58:49.080
My dear Aunt Augusta,
what I mean is that Bunbury was found out!

2060
01:58:49.200 --> 01:58:52.720
The doctors found out
that Bunbury couldn't live...

2061
01:58:54.520 --> 01:58:57.120
...and so Bunbury died.

2062
01:58:57.240 --> 01:58:59.080
(Audience laughing)

2063
01:59:01.400 --> 01:59:05.280
He appears to have had great confidence
in the opinion of his physician.

2064
01:59:05.400 --> 01:59:07.080
(Audience laughing)

2065
01:59:07.200 --> 01:59:08.600
Yes.

2066
01:59:11.040 --> 01:59:12.320
I am glad, however,

2067
01:59:12.440 --> 01:59:16.520
that he made up his mind at the last
to some definite course of action,

2068
01:59:16.640 --> 01:59:20.640
and acted under proper medical advice.

2069
01:59:20.800 --> 01:59:25.040
And now that we have finally got rid
of this Mr Bunbury,

2070
01:59:25.160 --> 01:59:28.840
may I ask, Mr Worthing,
who is that young person

2071
01:59:28.960 --> 01:59:32.200
whose hand my nephew Algernon
is now holding

2072
01:59:32.320 --> 01:59:36.800
in what seems to me
a peculiarly unnecessary manner?

2073
01:59:36.920 --> 01:59:40.360
That lady is Miss Cecily Cardew,
my ward.

2074
01:59:40.480 --> 01:59:45.200
I am engaged to be married
to Cecily, Aunt Augusta.

2075
01:59:45.320 --> 01:59:47.200
- I beg your pardon?
- Um, I... (Mumbles)

2076
01:59:47.320 --> 01:59:50.960
Mr Moncrieff and I are engaged
to be married, Lady Bracknell.

2077
01:59:52.320 --> 01:59:54.400
- (Gasps)
- (Audience laughing)

2078
01:59:54.520 --> 01:59:58.440
I do not know whether there is
anything peculiarly exciting

2079
01:59:58.560 --> 02:00:02.120
in the air of this particular part
of Hertfordshire...

2080
02:00:03.600 --> 02:00:07.040
...but the number of engagements
that goes on

2081
02:00:07.160 --> 02:00:10.240
seems to me considerably
above the proper average

2082
02:00:10.360 --> 02:00:14.120
that statistics have laid down
for our guidance.

2083
02:00:15.000 --> 02:00:20.600
I think some preliminary enquiries on my part
would not be out of place.

2084
02:00:20.720 --> 02:00:24.560
Mr Worthing,
is Miss Cardew at all connected

2085
02:00:24.680 --> 02:00:27.160
with any of the larger railway stations
in London?

2086
02:00:27.280 --> 02:00:29.720
(Audience laughing)

2087
02:00:29.880 --> 02:00:32.680
I merely desire information.

2088
02:00:32.840 --> 02:00:38.120
Until yesterday, I had no idea
there were any families or persons

2089
02:00:38.240 --> 02:00:41.560
whose origin was a Terminus.

2090
02:00:43.680 --> 02:00:47.840
Miss Cardew is the granddaughter
of the late Mr Thomas Cardew

2091
02:00:47.960 --> 02:00:52.200
of 149, Belgrave Square, SW,
Gervase Park, Dorking, Surrey,

2092
02:00:52.320 --> 02:00:55.360
and the Sporran, Fifeshire, FIY.

2093
02:00:56.640 --> 02:00:59.400
That sounds not unsatisfactory.

2094
02:00:59.520 --> 02:01:04.000
Three addresses
always inspires confidence,

2095
02:01:04.120 --> 02:01:05.840
even in tradesmen.

2096
02:01:05.960 --> 02:01:09.000
But what proof have I of their authenticity?

2097
02:01:09.120 --> 02:01:11.320
I have carefully preserved
the Court Guides of the period.

2098
02:01:11.440 --> 02:01:13.080
They are open to your inspection,
Lady Bracknell.

2099
02:01:13.200 --> 02:01:16.520
I have known strange errors
in that publication.

2100
02:01:16.640 --> 02:01:20.840
Miss Cardew's family solicitors
are Messrs. Markby, Markby and Markby.

2101
02:01:22.200 --> 02:01:25.800
(Lady Bracknell)
Markby, Markby and Markby?

2102
02:01:25.920 --> 02:01:30.080
A firm of the very highest position
in their profession.

2103
02:01:30.200 --> 02:01:36.440
Indeed I am told that one of the Mr Markbys
is occasionally to be seen at dinner parties.

2104
02:01:37.240 --> 02:01:40.440
So far I am satisfied.

2105
02:01:40.560 --> 02:01:42.880
How extremely kind of you,
Lady Bracknell!

2106
02:01:43.000 --> 02:01:45.600
I also have in my possession,
you'll be pleased to hear,

2107
02:01:45.720 --> 02:01:50.080
certificates of Miss Cardew's birth, baptism,
whooping cough, registration,

2108
02:01:50.200 --> 02:01:52.720
vaccination, confirmation
and the measles -

2109
02:01:52.880 --> 02:01:55.680
both the German
and the English variety.

2110
02:01:55.840 --> 02:01:59.960
Ah!
A life crowded with incident, I see,

2111
02:02:00.080 --> 02:02:03.480
though perhaps somewhat too exciting
for a young girl.

2112
02:02:03.600 --> 02:02:08.000
I am not myself in favour
of premature experiences.

2113
02:02:08.120 --> 02:02:09.880
Gwendolen!

2114
02:02:10.000 --> 02:02:12.480
The time approaches for our departure.

2115
02:02:12.600 --> 02:02:14.480
We have not a moment to lose.

2116
02:02:14.960 --> 02:02:17.840
Oh! As a matter of form, Mr Worthing,

2117
02:02:17.960 --> 02:02:22.920
I had better ask you
if Miss Cardew has any little fortune?

2118
02:02:23.720 --> 02:02:28.160
About a hundred and thirty thousand pounds
in the Funds. That is all.

2119
02:02:28.280 --> 02:02:31.480
Goodbye, Lady Bracknell.
So pleased to have seen you.

2120
02:02:33.160 --> 02:02:36.320
- A moment, Mr Worthing.
- (Audience laughing)

2121
02:02:36.920 --> 02:02:40.600
A hundred and thirty thousand pounds!

2122
02:02:40.720 --> 02:02:43.440
- And in the Funds!
- (Grunting)

2123
02:02:43.560 --> 02:02:45.560
(Audience laughing)

2124
02:02:46.480 --> 02:02:53.160
Miss Cardew seems to me a most attractive
young lady, now that I look at her.

2125
02:02:53.280 --> 02:02:57.440
Few girls of the present day
have any really solid qualities,

2126
02:02:57.560 --> 02:03:01.280
any of the qualities that last
and improve with time.

2127
02:03:01.400 --> 02:03:06.840
We live, I regret to say,
in an age of surfaces.

2128
02:03:08.280 --> 02:03:10.200
(Audience laughing)

2129
02:03:10.320 --> 02:03:12.360
Come over here, dear.

2130
02:03:15.480 --> 02:03:17.080
(Laughing)

2131
02:03:17.200 --> 02:03:19.840
Pretty child!

2132
02:03:19.960 --> 02:03:23.480
Your dress is sadly simple,

2133
02:03:23.600 --> 02:03:29.280
and your hair - oh! - seems almost
as nature might have left it.

2134
02:03:29.400 --> 02:03:31.720
But we can soon alter all of that.

2135
02:03:31.880 --> 02:03:33.920
A thoroughly experienced French maid

2136
02:03:34.040 --> 02:03:39.040
produces a really marvellous result
in a very brief space of time.

2137
02:03:39.160 --> 02:03:43.600
I remember recommending one
to a young Lady Lancing,

2138
02:03:43.720 --> 02:03:47.800
and after three months,
her own husband did not know her.

2139
02:03:48.680 --> 02:03:51.960
- And after six months nobody knew her.
- (Audience chuckling)

2140
02:03:55.160 --> 02:03:58.040
Kindly turn around, sweet child.

2141
02:03:59.880 --> 02:04:03.360
No, no, no,
the side view is what I want.

2142
02:04:03.480 --> 02:04:05.280
Yes!

2143
02:04:05.400 --> 02:04:07.560
Quite as I expected.

2144
02:04:07.680 --> 02:04:12.560
There are distinct social possibilities
in your profile.

2145
02:04:12.680 --> 02:04:17.040
The two weak points in our age
are its want of principle

2146
02:04:17.160 --> 02:04:19.640
- and its want of profile.
- (Algernon and Gwendolen) Mm!

2147
02:04:21.080 --> 02:04:22.440
(Audience laughing)

2148
02:04:22.560 --> 02:04:25.560
The chin a little higher, dear.

2149
02:04:25.680 --> 02:04:29.960
Style largely depends
on the way the chin is worn.

2150
02:04:30.080 --> 02:04:34.480
And they are worn very high,
just at present.

2151
02:04:34.600 --> 02:04:37.080
- Algernon!
- Yes, Aunt Augusta!

2152
02:04:37.200 --> 02:04:41.560
There are distinct social possibilities
in Miss Cardew's profile.

2153
02:04:41.680 --> 02:04:47.520
Cecily is the sweetest, prettiest,
dearest girl in the whole world.

2154
02:04:47.640 --> 02:04:50.120
I don't give a tuppence
about social possibilities.

2155
02:04:50.240 --> 02:04:53.840
Never speak disrespectfully
of society, Algernon.

2156
02:04:53.960 --> 02:04:57.520
- Only people who can't get into it do that.
- (Audience laughing)

2157
02:04:58.280 --> 02:05:00.120
Dear child...

2158
02:05:02.920 --> 02:05:07.800
...of course you know that Algernon
has nothing but his debts to depend on.

2159
02:05:07.920 --> 02:05:11.080
But I do not approve
of mercenary marriages.

2160
02:05:11.200 --> 02:05:16.160
When I married Lord Bracknell,
I had no fortune of any kind.

2161
02:05:16.280 --> 02:05:20.920
But I never dreamed for a moment
of allowing that to stand in my way.

2162
02:05:21.600 --> 02:05:25.920
Well, I suppose I must give my consent.

2163
02:05:26.040 --> 02:05:28.280
Thank you, Aunt Augusta.

2164
02:05:28.400 --> 02:05:31.320
Ce... Cecily, you may kiss me!

2165
02:05:33.680 --> 02:05:35.240
Thank you, Lady Bracknell.

2166
02:05:35.360 --> 02:05:39.200
You may also address me
as Aunt Augusta for the future.

2167
02:05:39.320 --> 02:05:41.400
Thank you, Aunt Augusta.

2168
02:05:41.520 --> 02:05:44.800
Well, the marriage, I think,
had better take place quite soon.

2169
02:05:44.920 --> 02:05:46.080
(Giggling)

2170
02:05:46.200 --> 02:05:48.000
Thank you, Aunt Augusta.

2171
02:05:48.120 --> 02:05:49.800
- Thank you, Aunt Augusta.
- (Laughing)

2172
02:05:50.360 --> 02:05:54.400
To speak frankly, I am not in favour
of long engagements.

2173
02:05:54.520 --> 02:06:00.240
They give people the opportunity of finding out
each other's character before marriage,

2174
02:06:00.360 --> 02:06:03.000
which I never think is advisable.

2175
02:06:03.120 --> 02:06:05.240
I beg your pardon for interrupting you,
Lady Bracknell,

2176
02:06:05.360 --> 02:06:08.520
but this engagement
is quite out of the question.

2177
02:06:08.640 --> 02:06:09.880
I am Miss Cardew's guardian,

2178
02:06:10.000 --> 02:06:13.440
and she cannot marry without my consent
until she comes of age.

2179
02:06:13.560 --> 02:06:17.200
That consent I absolutely decline to give.

2180
02:06:17.320 --> 02:06:20.080
(Lady Bracknell)
Upon what grounds, may I ask?

2181
02:06:20.200 --> 02:06:27.920
Algernon is an extremely, I may almost say
ostentatiously, eligible young man.

2182
02:06:28.040 --> 02:06:32.000
He has nothing,
but he looks everything.

2183
02:06:32.120 --> 02:06:34.240
(Audience laughing)

2184
02:06:34.360 --> 02:06:36.720
What more could one desire?

2185
02:06:36.880 --> 02:06:39.720
It pains me very much to have
to speak frankly to you, Lady Bracknell,

2186
02:06:39.880 --> 02:06:41.160
about your nephew,

2187
02:06:41.280 --> 02:06:45.880
but the fact is, I do not approve at all
of his moral character.

2188
02:06:46.000 --> 02:06:47.880
I suspect him of being untruthful.

2189
02:06:48.000 --> 02:06:51.120
Untruthful!
My nephew Algernon?

2190
02:06:51.240 --> 02:06:52.840
Impossible!

2191
02:06:52.960 --> 02:06:54.400
He went to Oxford!

2192
02:06:56.320 --> 02:06:59.040
I fear there can be no possible doubt
about the matter.

2193
02:06:59.160 --> 02:07:00.320
This afternoon,

2194
02:07:00.440 --> 02:07:04.800
during my temporary absence in London
on an important question of romance,

2195
02:07:04.920 --> 02:07:09.040
he gained admittance to my house by means
of the false pretence of being my brother.

2196
02:07:09.160 --> 02:07:10.200
Jack!

2197
02:07:10.320 --> 02:07:13.200
Under an assumed name he drank,
I've just been informed by my butler,

2198
02:07:13.320 --> 02:07:17.000
an entire bottle
of my Perrier-Jouet, Brut, '89 -

2199
02:07:17.120 --> 02:07:19.880
a wine I was specially reserving for myself.

2200
02:07:20.000 --> 02:07:22.400
Continuing his disgraceful deception,

2201
02:07:22.520 --> 02:07:26.920
he succeeded in the course of the afternoon
in alienating the affections of my only ward.

2202
02:07:27.800 --> 02:07:31.360
- He subsequently stayed to tea...
- What?

2203
02:07:31.480 --> 02:07:34.000
...and devoured every single muffin.

2204
02:07:36.640 --> 02:07:38.920
And what makes his conduct
all the more heartless

2205
02:07:39.040 --> 02:07:41.560
is that he was perfectly well aware
from the first that I have no brother,

2206
02:07:41.680 --> 02:07:44.840
that I never had a brother,
and I don't intend to have a brother,

2207
02:07:44.960 --> 02:07:46.840
not even of any kind.

2208
02:07:48.080 --> 02:07:51.640
I distinctly told him so myself
yesterday afternoon.

2209
02:07:51.800 --> 02:07:53.320
Mm!

2210
02:07:53.440 --> 02:07:57.080
Mr Worthing,
after careful consideration

2211
02:07:57.200 --> 02:08:02.320
I have decided entirely to overlook
my nephew's conduct to you.

2212
02:08:03.000 --> 02:08:04.800
That's very kind of you,
Lady Bracknell.

2213
02:08:04.920 --> 02:08:07.520
My own decision,
however, is unalterable.

2214
02:08:07.640 --> 02:08:09.400
I decline to give my consent.

2215
02:08:09.880 --> 02:08:11.200
Hmm!

2216
02:08:12.680 --> 02:08:14.680
Come over here, dear.

2217
02:08:17.080 --> 02:08:20.080
How old are you, sweet child?

2218
02:08:20.200 --> 02:08:22.520
Well, I am really only eighteen,

2219
02:08:22.640 --> 02:08:26.200
but I always admit to twenty
when I go to evening parties.

2220
02:08:26.320 --> 02:08:29.720
You are perfectly right
in making some slight alteration.

2221
02:08:29.880 --> 02:08:34.400
Indeed, no woman should ever be
quite accurate about her age.

2222
02:08:34.520 --> 02:08:36.720
It looks so calculating.

2223
02:08:37.880 --> 02:08:42.040
Eighteen, but admitting to twenty
at evening parties. Hmm!

2224
02:08:42.160 --> 02:08:46.120
Well, it will not be very long
before you are of age

2225
02:08:46.240 --> 02:08:49.440
and free from the restraints of tutelage.

2226
02:08:49.560 --> 02:08:55.600
So I do not think your guardian's consent is,
after all, a matter of any importance.

2227
02:08:55.720 --> 02:08:59.360
Pray excuse me for interrupting you again,
Lady Bracknell,

2228
02:08:59.480 --> 02:09:02.440
but according to the terms
of her grandfather's will,

2229
02:09:02.560 --> 02:09:06.680
Miss Cardew does not legally come of age
until she's thirty-five.

2230
02:09:06.840 --> 02:09:09.240
- Oh! Oh!
- (Audience laughing)

2231
02:09:10.040 --> 02:09:13.400
That does not seem to me
to be a grave objection.

2232
02:09:14.320 --> 02:09:16.480
(Audience laughing)

2233
02:09:16.600 --> 02:09:20.200
Thirty-five is a very attractive age.

2234
02:09:20.320 --> 02:09:23.560
London society is full of women
of the highest birth

2235
02:09:23.680 --> 02:09:28.400
who have, of their own free choice,
remained thirty-five for years.

2236
02:09:28.520 --> 02:09:30.080
(Audience laughing)

2237
02:09:30.200 --> 02:09:32.880
Lady Dumbleton is an instance in point.

2238
02:09:33.000 --> 02:09:39.560
To my own knowledge she has been thirty-five
ever since she arrived at the age of forty,

2239
02:09:39.680 --> 02:09:42.920
which was many years ago now.

2240
02:09:43.040 --> 02:09:47.880
I see no reason
why our own dear Ce... Uh-uh!

2241
02:09:48.000 --> 02:09:51.440
- (Audience laughing)
- Cecily!

2242
02:09:51.560 --> 02:09:56.880
...should not be as... more attractive
at the age you mention than she is at present.

2243
02:09:57.200 --> 02:10:01.240
And there will be
a large accumulation of property.

2244
02:10:01.360 --> 02:10:04.200
- Algy...
- Mm! (Chuckles)

2245
02:10:04.320 --> 02:10:07.120
...could you wait for me
till I was thirty-five?

2246
02:10:09.520 --> 02:10:11.040
(Audience laughing)

2247
02:10:11.160 --> 02:10:14.560
Of course I could, Cecily.

2248
02:10:14.680 --> 02:10:18.320
- You know I could.
- Yes, I felt it instinctively.

2249
02:10:18.440 --> 02:10:21.040
- But I couldn't wait all that time.
- Oh!

2250
02:10:21.160 --> 02:10:24.040
I hate waiting even five minutes
for anybody.

2251
02:10:24.160 --> 02:10:26.600
It always makes me rather cross.

2252
02:10:26.720 --> 02:10:31.400
I am not punctual myself, I know,
but I do like punctuality in others,

2253
02:10:31.520 --> 02:10:35.440
and waiting, even to be married,
is quite out of the question.

2254
02:10:36.120 --> 02:10:40.640
Then what is to be done, Cecily?

2255
02:10:40.800 --> 02:10:43.160
I don't know, Mr Moncrieff.

2256
02:10:43.280 --> 02:10:45.240
My dear Mr Worthing,

2257
02:10:45.360 --> 02:10:51.000
as Miss Cardew states positively
that she cannot wait until she's thirty-five,

2258
02:10:51.120 --> 02:10:57.040
a remark which I am bound to say seems
to me to show a somewhat impatient nature,

2259
02:10:57.160 --> 02:11:00.960
I would beg of you
to reconsider your decision.

2260
02:11:02.480 --> 02:11:07.960
But my dear Lady Bracknell,
the matter's entirely in your own hands.

2261
02:11:08.080 --> 02:11:11.280
The moment you consent
to my marriage with Gwendolen...

2262
02:11:12.400 --> 02:11:16.560
...I will most gladly allow your nephew
to form an alliance with my ward.

2263
02:11:16.680 --> 02:11:20.440
You must be quite aware
that what you propose is out of the question.

2264
02:11:20.560 --> 02:11:23.560
Then a passionate celibacy
is all any of us can look forward to.

2265
02:11:23.680 --> 02:11:25.280
- Oh!
- (Audience laughing)

2266
02:11:25.400 --> 02:11:29.000
That is not the destiny
I propose for Gwendolen.

2267
02:11:29.120 --> 02:11:31.800
Algernon, of course,
can choose for himself.

2268
02:11:31.920 --> 02:11:36.200
Come, dear, we have already missed five,
if not six, trains.

2269
02:11:36.320 --> 02:11:41.600
To miss any more might expose us
to comment on the platform.

2270
02:11:41.720 --> 02:11:44.640
Everything is quite ready
for the christenings.

2271
02:11:45.360 --> 02:11:47.400
The christenings, sir!

2272
02:11:47.520 --> 02:11:50.560
- Is that not somewhat premature?
- (Audience laughing)

2273
02:11:52.040 --> 02:11:56.840
Both these gentlemen have expressed
a desire for immediate baptism.

2274
02:11:56.960 --> 02:11:58.840
At their age?

2275
02:11:58.960 --> 02:12:02.240
The idea is grotesque and irreligious!

2276
02:12:02.360 --> 02:12:05.920
Algernon, I forbid you to be baptised.

2277
02:12:06.040 --> 02:12:08.440
I will not hear of such excesses.

2278
02:12:08.560 --> 02:12:11.080
Lord Bracknell would be highly displeased

2279
02:12:11.200 --> 02:12:15.120
if he learned that that was the way
in which you wasted your time and money.

2280
02:12:15.240 --> 02:12:20.640
Am I to understand, then, that there are
to be no christenings at all this afternoon?

2281
02:12:20.800 --> 02:12:22.000
I don't think, as things are now,

2282
02:12:22.120 --> 02:12:25.240
it would be of much practical value
to either of us, Dr Chasuble.

2283
02:12:25.360 --> 02:12:28.960
I am grieved to hear such sentiments
from you, Mr Worthing.

2284
02:12:29.080 --> 02:12:33.400
Baptismal regeneration
is not to be lightly spoken of.

2285
02:12:33.520 --> 02:12:36.680
They savour of the heretical views
of the Anabaptists,

2286
02:12:36.840 --> 02:12:41.320
views I've completely refuted
in four of my unpublished sermons.

2287
02:12:41.440 --> 02:12:46.000
However, as your current mood
seems to be one peculiarly secular,

2288
02:12:46.120 --> 02:12:48.240
I shall return to the church at once.

2289
02:12:48.360 --> 02:12:51.720
Indeed, I have just been informed
by the pew-opener

2290
02:12:51.880 --> 02:12:56.920
that for the last hour and a half Miss Prism
has been waiting for me in the vestry.

2291
02:12:57.040 --> 02:12:58.360
Miss Prism!

2292
02:12:58.920 --> 02:13:02.720
Did I hear you mention a Miss Prism?

2293
02:13:02.880 --> 02:13:05.720
Yes, Lady Bracknell.
I'm on my way to join her.

2294
02:13:05.880 --> 02:13:08.760
Pray allow me to detain you for a moment.

2295
02:13:08.920 --> 02:13:13.440
This matter may prove one of vital importance
to Lord Bracknell and myself.

2296
02:13:14.240 --> 02:13:19.640
Is this Miss Prism
a female of repellent aspect,

2297
02:13:19.800 --> 02:13:23.200
remotely connected with education?

2298
02:13:23.320 --> 02:13:28.280
She is the most cultivated of ladies,
and the very picture of respectability.

2299
02:13:28.400 --> 02:13:31.080
It is obviously the same person.

2300
02:13:31.200 --> 02:13:34.640
May I ask what position
she holds in your household?

2301
02:13:35.520 --> 02:13:37.600
I am a celibate, madam.

2302
02:13:37.720 --> 02:13:40.440
Miss Prism, Lady Bracknell,
has been for the last three years

2303
02:13:40.560 --> 02:13:43.400
Miss Cardew's esteemed governess
and valued companion.

2304
02:13:43.520 --> 02:13:47.040
In spite of what I hear of her,
I must see her at once.

2305
02:13:47.160 --> 02:13:48.880
Let her be sent for.

2306
02:13:49.000 --> 02:13:52.480
She approaches. She is nigh.

2307
02:13:52.600 --> 02:13:56.400
I was told you expected me
in the vestry, dear Canon.

2308
02:13:56.520 --> 02:13:59.440
I've been waiting for you there
for an hour and three quarters.

2309
02:13:59.560 --> 02:14:00.800
Prism!

2310
02:14:03.840 --> 02:14:07.160
- (Gasps, then mumbles)
- (Audience laughing)

2311
02:14:07.280 --> 02:14:09.560
Come here, Prism!

2312
02:14:22.200 --> 02:14:24.160
Prism!

2313
02:14:24.280 --> 02:14:27.600
Where is that baby?

2314
02:14:28.960 --> 02:14:33.400
Twenty-eight years ago, Prism,
you left Lord Bracknell's house,

2315
02:14:33.520 --> 02:14:36.520
Number 104,
Upper Grosvenor Street,

2316
02:14:36.640 --> 02:14:42.520
in charge of a perambulator
that contained a baby of the male sex.

2317
02:14:42.640 --> 02:14:45.560
You never returned.

2318
02:14:45.680 --> 02:14:47.480
A few weeks later,

2319
02:14:47.600 --> 02:14:51.520
through the elaborate investigations
of the Metropolitan police,

2320
02:14:51.640 --> 02:14:55.040
the perambulator
was discovered at midnight,

2321
02:14:55.160 --> 02:15:00.720
standing by itself
in a remote corner of Bayswater.

2322
02:15:00.880 --> 02:15:02.360
(Audience laughing)

2323
02:15:02.480 --> 02:15:06.040
It contained the manuscript
of a three-volume novel

2324
02:15:06.160 --> 02:15:10.640
of more than usually revolting
sentimentality.

2325
02:15:10.800 --> 02:15:14.200
But the baby was not there!

2326
02:15:15.080 --> 02:15:16.600
Prism!

2327
02:15:16.720 --> 02:15:20.320
Where is that baby?

2328
02:15:22.320 --> 02:15:24.120
Lady Bracknell,

2329
02:15:24.240 --> 02:15:27.680
I admit with shame that I do not know.

2330
02:15:28.680 --> 02:15:30.840
I only wish I did.

2331
02:15:32.240 --> 02:15:35.560
The plain facts of the case are these.

2332
02:15:36.160 --> 02:15:38.520
On the morning
of the day you mention,

2333
02:15:38.640 --> 02:15:42.000
a day that is for ever branded
on my memory,

2334
02:15:42.120 --> 02:15:47.320
I prepared as usual
to take the baby out in its perambulator.

2335
02:15:47.440 --> 02:15:53.640
I had also with me a somewhat old,
but capacious handbag

2336
02:15:53.800 --> 02:15:57.200
in which I had intended
to place the manuscript

2337
02:15:57.320 --> 02:16:03.360
of a work of fiction that I had written
during my few unoccupied hours.

2338
02:16:03.480 --> 02:16:10.680
In a moment of mental abstraction,

2339
02:16:10.840 --> 02:16:13.840
for which I can never forgive myself,

2340
02:16:13.960 --> 02:16:17.880
I deposited the manuscript
in the bassinette,

2341
02:16:18.000 --> 02:16:21.400
and placed the baby in the handbag.

2342
02:16:23.440 --> 02:16:27.120
- But where did you deposit the handbag?
- Do not ask me, Mr Worthing.

2343
02:16:27.240 --> 02:16:29.680
Miss Prism, this is a matter
of no small importance to me.

2344
02:16:29.840 --> 02:16:33.120
I insist on knowing where you deposited
the handbag that contained that infant.

2345
02:16:33.600 --> 02:16:38.080
I left it in the cloak-room of one
of the larger railway stations in London.

2346
02:16:38.200 --> 02:16:40.040
Which railway station?

2347
02:16:40.800 --> 02:16:42.920
- Victoria.
- (Gasping)

2348
02:16:44.280 --> 02:16:46.080
The Brighton line.

2349
02:16:46.200 --> 02:16:48.120
(Audience laughing)

2350
02:16:49.480 --> 02:16:51.280
I must retire to my room for a moment.

2351
02:16:51.400 --> 02:16:53.240
Gwendolen!
You wait for me here.

2352
02:16:53.360 --> 02:16:55.760
If you're not too long,
I'll wait here for you all my life.

2353
02:16:57.040 --> 02:16:59.400
(Grunting)

2354
02:17:00.840 --> 02:17:02.160
Oh, no! No!

2355
02:17:02.280 --> 02:17:05.280
What do you think this means,
Lady Bracknell?

2356
02:17:05.400 --> 02:17:08.440
I... I dare not even suspect,
Dr Chasuble.

2357
02:17:08.560 --> 02:17:12.160
I need hardly tell you
that in families of high position

2358
02:17:12.280 --> 02:17:16.200
strange coincidences
are not supposed to occur.

2359
02:17:16.320 --> 02:17:19.440
- They are hardly considered the thing.
- (Thump)

2360
02:17:19.560 --> 02:17:21.440
- Oh!
- (Footsteps, rumbling)

2361
02:17:23.120 --> 02:17:26.320
Uncle Jack seems strangely agitated.

2362
02:17:27.120 --> 02:17:30.560
Your guardian has a very emotional nature.

2363
02:17:30.680 --> 02:17:33.400
This noise is extremely unpleasant.

2364
02:17:33.520 --> 02:17:35.920
It sounds as though
he were having an argument.

2365
02:17:36.040 --> 02:17:38.280
I dislike arguments of any kind.

2366
02:17:38.400 --> 02:17:42.720
They are always vulgar,
and often convincing.

2367
02:17:42.880 --> 02:17:44.480
(Noise stops)

2368
02:17:44.600 --> 02:17:46.600
- Oh, it's stopped now.
- (Lady Bracknell) Good.

2369
02:17:47.280 --> 02:17:49.520
(Clattering, glass breaking)

2370
02:17:50.480 --> 02:17:53.960
I wish he would arrive
at some conclusion.

2371
02:17:54.080 --> 02:17:55.880
This suspense is terrible.

2372
02:17:56.000 --> 02:17:57.880
I hope it will last.

2373
02:17:58.800 --> 02:18:00.440
(Sobbing)

2374
02:18:01.560 --> 02:18:03.120
(Clattering)

2375
02:18:04.200 --> 02:18:06.440
Is this the handbag, Miss Prism?

2376
02:18:07.320 --> 02:18:09.280
Examine it carefully before you speak.

2377
02:18:09.400 --> 02:18:12.640
The happiness of more than one life
depends on your answer.

2378
02:18:14.440 --> 02:18:16.560
Well, it...

2379
02:18:17.480 --> 02:18:22.320
It seems... to be... to be mine.

2380
02:18:24.680 --> 02:18:26.840
Yes!

2381
02:18:26.960 --> 02:18:33.400
Here is the injury it received through
the upsetting of a Gower Street omnibus

2382
02:18:33.520 --> 02:18:38.320
in younger and happier days.

2383
02:18:39.520 --> 02:18:43.000
Here is the stain on the lining

2384
02:18:43.120 --> 02:18:47.600
caused by an explosion
of a temperance beverage,

2385
02:18:47.720 --> 02:18:51.560
an incident that occurred at Leamington.

2386
02:18:51.680 --> 02:18:52.720
(Audience chuckling)

2387
02:18:53.720 --> 02:18:57.000
And... And here,

2388
02:18:57.120 --> 02:18:58.800
on the lock,

2389
02:18:58.920 --> 02:19:00.880
are my initials.

2390
02:19:02.160 --> 02:19:04.120
I had forgotten that...

2391
02:19:04.240 --> 02:19:09.520
that in an extravagant mood
I had had them placed there.

2392
02:19:10.240 --> 02:19:12.480
The bag's undoubtedly mine.

2393
02:19:12.600 --> 02:19:17.320
I am delighted to have it
so unexpectedly restored to me.

2394
02:19:17.440 --> 02:19:20.480
It's been quite an inconvenience
being without it all these years.

2395
02:19:20.600 --> 02:19:23.560
(Audience laughing)

2396
02:19:23.680 --> 02:19:25.800
- Miss Prism.
- Yes?

2397
02:19:27.720 --> 02:19:30.680
More is restored to you
than that handbag.

2398
02:19:33.720 --> 02:19:35.600
I was the baby you placed in it.

2399
02:19:37.080 --> 02:19:39.080
You?

2400
02:19:39.200 --> 02:19:41.120
Yes.

2401
02:19:41.240 --> 02:19:42.560
Mother!

2402
02:19:43.360 --> 02:19:46.320
Mr Worthing!
I am unmarried!

2403
02:19:46.440 --> 02:19:48.680
Unmarried!

2404
02:19:48.840 --> 02:19:52.000
I do not deny that is a serious blow.

2405
02:19:53.440 --> 02:19:56.240
But, after all,

2406
02:19:56.360 --> 02:20:00.680
who has the right to cast a stone
against one who has suffered?

2407
02:20:00.840 --> 02:20:04.840
Cannot repentance
wipe out an act of folly?

2408
02:20:04.960 --> 02:20:07.600
Why should there be one law for men,
and another for women?

2409
02:20:07.720 --> 02:20:09.240
Mother, I forgive you.

2410
02:20:09.360 --> 02:20:12.520
Mr Worthing, there is some error.

2411
02:20:13.800 --> 02:20:18.320
There is the lady
who can tell you who you really are.

2412
02:20:19.920 --> 02:20:21.440
(Clicks tongue, scoffs)

2413
02:20:21.560 --> 02:20:23.600
(Audience laughing)

2414
02:20:30.040 --> 02:20:33.440
Lady Bracknell,
I hate to seem inquisitive...

2415
02:20:33.560 --> 02:20:35.800
(Audience laughing)

2416
02:20:36.680 --> 02:20:39.040
...but would you kindly inform me
who I am?

2417
02:20:39.960 --> 02:20:45.080
I'm afraid the news I have to give you
will not altogether please you.

2418
02:20:46.200 --> 02:20:51.960
You are the son of my poor sister,
Mrs Moncrieff,

2419
02:20:52.080 --> 02:20:56.600
and consequently Algernon's elder brother.

2420
02:20:56.720 --> 02:20:59.560
(Audience laughing)

2421
02:20:59.680 --> 02:21:02.000
Algy's elder brother!

2422
02:21:02.640 --> 02:21:04.440
Then I have a brother after all.

2423
02:21:04.560 --> 02:21:07.480
I knew I had a brother!
I always said I had a brother!

2424
02:21:07.600 --> 02:21:11.160
Cecily, how could you have ever doubted
that I had a brother?

2425
02:21:11.280 --> 02:21:13.560
Dr Chasuble, my unfortunate brother.

2426
02:21:13.680 --> 02:21:16.120
Miss Prism, my unfortunate brother.

2427
02:21:16.240 --> 02:21:18.560
Gwendolen, it's my unfortunate brother.

2428
02:21:18.680 --> 02:21:21.400
Algy, you young scoundrel,

2429
02:21:21.520 --> 02:21:24.000
you're gonna have to treat me
with much more respect in the future.

2430
02:21:24.120 --> 02:21:26.280
You've never behaved to me
like a brother in all your life.

2431
02:21:26.840 --> 02:21:30.280
Well, not till today, old boy, I admit.

2432
02:21:30.400 --> 02:21:34.400
However, I did my best,
though I was out of practice.

2433
02:21:34.520 --> 02:21:37.840
My own...! (Mumbles)
But what own are you?

2434
02:21:37.960 --> 02:21:40.840
What is your Christian name
now that you've become someone else?

2435
02:21:41.480 --> 02:21:44.400
Good heavens!
I'd quite forgotten that point.

2436
02:21:44.520 --> 02:21:47.720
Your decision on the subject of my name
is irrevocable, I suppose?

2437
02:21:47.880 --> 02:21:51.680
I never change, except in my affections.

2438
02:21:51.840 --> 02:21:54.600
What a noble nature you have,
Gwendolen!

2439
02:21:54.720 --> 02:21:56.240
(Chuckling)

2440
02:21:56.720 --> 02:21:59.640
Very well. Then the matter
had better be cleared up at once.

2441
02:21:59.800 --> 02:22:01.320
Lady Bracknell,

2442
02:22:01.440 --> 02:22:04.600
at the time when Miss Prism
left me in the handbag,

2443
02:22:04.720 --> 02:22:06.160
had I been christened already?

2444
02:22:06.280 --> 02:22:09.720
Every luxury that money could buy,
including christening,

2445
02:22:09.880 --> 02:22:14.080
had been lavished upon you
by your fond and doting parents.

2446
02:22:14.200 --> 02:22:16.560
So, I was christened!
That is settled.

2447
02:22:16.680 --> 02:22:18.640
Now, what name was I given?
Let me know the worst.

2448
02:22:18.800 --> 02:22:23.800
Being the eldest son, you were naturally
christened after your father.

2449
02:22:25.560 --> 02:22:28.040
Yes, but what was my father's
Christian name?

2450
02:22:29.520 --> 02:22:34.160
I cannot at the present moment recall
what the General's Christian name was.

2451
02:22:34.280 --> 02:22:37.800
- But I have no doubt he had one.
- (Audience laughing)

2452
02:22:37.920 --> 02:22:41.160
He was eccentric, I admit.
But only in later years.

2453
02:22:41.280 --> 02:22:44.600
And that was the result
of the Indian climate,

2454
02:22:44.720 --> 02:22:47.280
and marriage, and indigestion,

2455
02:22:47.400 --> 02:22:49.000
and other things of that kind.

2456
02:22:49.480 --> 02:22:52.040
Algy! Can't you recollect
what our father's Christian name was?

2457
02:22:52.160 --> 02:22:55.720
Oh, my dear boy,
we weren't even on speaking terms.

2458
02:22:55.880 --> 02:22:58.120
He died before I was a year old.

2459
02:22:58.240 --> 02:23:01.680
His name would appear in the Army Lists
of the period, I suppose, Aunt Augusta?

2460
02:23:01.840 --> 02:23:04.920
The General was essentially
a man of peace,

2461
02:23:05.040 --> 02:23:07.560
except in his domestic life.

2462
02:23:07.680 --> 02:23:12.440
But I have no doubt his name would appear
in any military directory.

2463
02:23:14.000 --> 02:23:18.320
The Army Lists
of the last forty years are here.

2464
02:23:18.440 --> 02:23:21.960
These delightful records
should have been my constant study.

2465
02:23:23.160 --> 02:23:24.600
(Audience laughing)

2466
02:23:25.360 --> 02:23:26.400
"G".

2467
02:23:26.520 --> 02:23:27.680
- "G".
- "G".

2468
02:23:27.840 --> 02:23:29.040
- "A".
- "A".

2469
02:23:29.160 --> 02:23:30.200
"A".

2470
02:23:30.320 --> 02:23:31.560
- "Y".
- "Y".

2471
02:23:31.680 --> 02:23:32.720
"Y".

2472
02:23:32.880 --> 02:23:33.920
- "S".
- "S".

2473
02:23:34.040 --> 02:23:35.080
"S".

2474
02:23:35.200 --> 02:23:36.480
And "M".

2475
02:23:41.040 --> 02:23:43.240
Generals...

2476
02:23:43.360 --> 02:23:45.960
Mallam, Maxbohm, Magley...

2477
02:23:46.840 --> 02:23:49.040
What ghastly names they have!

2478
02:23:49.160 --> 02:23:51.360
...Markby, Migsby, Mon... Moncrieff!

2479
02:23:51.480 --> 02:23:55.280
Lieutenant 1840, Captain,
Lieutenant-Colonel, Colonel,

2480
02:23:55.400 --> 02:23:57.160
Colonel...

2481
02:23:57.280 --> 02:23:59.360
General 1869,
Christian names...

2482
02:24:03.600 --> 02:24:04.960
...Ernest John.

2483
02:24:05.080 --> 02:24:07.720
- (Rejoicing)
- (Audience laughing)

2484
02:24:13.400 --> 02:24:15.920
I always told you, Gwendolen,
my name was Ernest, didn't I?

2485
02:24:16.040 --> 02:24:18.480
(Laughing)

2486
02:24:18.600 --> 02:24:21.440
Well, it is Ernest after all.

2487
02:24:21.560 --> 02:24:23.160
I mean it naturally is Ernest.

2488
02:24:23.280 --> 02:24:28.920
Yes, I remember now
that the General was called Ernest.

2489
02:24:29.040 --> 02:24:32.320
I knew I had some particular reason
for disliking the name.

2490
02:24:33.400 --> 02:24:35.240
Ernest! (Chuckling)

2491
02:24:35.360 --> 02:24:37.080
My own Ernest!

2492
02:24:37.200 --> 02:24:40.120
I felt from the first
that you could have no other name!

2493
02:24:40.240 --> 02:24:41.920
- Gwendolen.
- Mm?

2494
02:24:42.040 --> 02:24:44.240
It's a terrible thing
for a man to suddenly find out

2495
02:24:44.360 --> 02:24:46.880
that all his life he's been speaking
nothing but the truth.

2496
02:24:47.000 --> 02:24:49.280
(Audience laughing)

2497
02:24:50.680 --> 02:24:52.840
Can you forgive me?

2498
02:24:52.960 --> 02:24:56.720
I can. For I feel
you are sure to change.

2499
02:24:58.120 --> 02:24:59.880
My own one!

2500
02:25:02.040 --> 02:25:03.640
Laetitia!

2501
02:25:05.240 --> 02:25:06.800
Frederick!

2502
02:25:08.320 --> 02:25:10.120
At last!

2503
02:25:12.640 --> 02:25:14.800
(Audience laughing)

2504
02:25:14.920 --> 02:25:16.600
- Cecily!
- (Cecily giggling)

2505
02:25:16.720 --> 02:25:18.280
At last!

2506
02:25:20.320 --> 02:25:22.440
Gwendolen!
At last!

2507
02:25:24.240 --> 02:25:26.440
(Audience laughing)

2508
02:25:26.560 --> 02:25:31.200
My nephew, you seem to be displaying
signs of triviality.

2509
02:25:31.320 --> 02:25:33.640
On the contrary, Aunt Augusta,

2510
02:25:33.800 --> 02:25:37.160
I have now realised
for the first time in my life...

2511
02:25:37.800 --> 02:25:41.280
...the vital Importance of Being Earnest.

2512
02:25:41.400 --> 02:25:45.360
- (Audience cheering, clapping)
- (♪ Drum roll)

2513
02:25:45.480 --> 02:25:47.920
(Applause)

2514
02:26:00.720 --> 02:26:03.600
(Cheering)

2515
02:26:03.720 --> 02:26:06.360
(♪ Rhythmic music playing)

2516
02:26:41.080 --> 02:26:43.480
(Cheering)

2517
